{"raw": {"https://apply.interfolio.com/193577": {"title": "Professor/Associate Professor", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering Location: Princeton, NJ The School of Engineering and Applied Science at Princeton University seeks applications for a tenured faculty position with demonstrated research expertise, teaching excellence, and leadership, in the broad area of artificial intelligence. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in a related field and should have a commitment to teaching and a demonstrated ability to pursue a high impact research program. The appointment is planned to be at the level of tenured Professor, and it is expected the candidate will hold tenure at their current institution, have a leadership position in industrial research labs, or equivalent. \u00a0An appointment will be made from a home department in the School of Engineering; joint appointments between multiple departments will be considered as appropriate. A start date of September 1, 2027 is preferred. The School of Engineering is committed to fostering an academic environment that acknowledges and encourages diversity and differences. We seek faculty members who will create a climate that embraces excellence, with a strong commitment to teaching and mentoring that will enhance the work of the department and that will attract and retain a diverse student body. Applicant review will begin in October. For fullest consideration, please submit applications no later than November 15, 2026, using the following site: apply.interfolio.com/193577 . Applications require: a cover letter, complete curriculum vitae, descriptions of research and teaching interests, and the contact information for at least four references. This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/180973": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Robotics and Autonomous Systems) ***REVISED***", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Architecture Location: Princeton University Adel Research Group (ARG) at Princeton University invites applicants for full-time Postdoctoral Research Associate(s) to join our team. The postdoc(s) will conduct interdisciplinary research in robotics and autonomous systems with an emphasis on robot learning and real-world deployment for construction-scale robotic fabrication, human\u2013robot collaboration, and advanced manufacturing. The position(s) are advised by Dr. Arash Adel (Assistant Professor of Architecture; Core Faculty, Princeton Robotics; Associated Faculty, Computer Science) and include opportunities for collaboration across Princeton Robotics labs. Appointments are for one year, with the possibility of renewal contingent on satisfactory performance and continued funding. About Us ARG is an interdisciplinary laboratory for advanced research at the intersection of robotics, artificial intelligence (AI), and computational design. We develop learning-enabled and perception-driven robotic systems that operate in complex, unstructured environments, with particular focus on: 1) Robot learning and embodied AI; 2) Multimodal perception and state estimation; 3) Closed-loop planning and control; and 4) End-to-end design-to-fabrication workflows. ARG\u2019s research has been supported by highly competitive grants, including a National Science Foundation\u2019s Future of Work at the Human-Technology Frontier grant. Job Description We seek outstanding Postdoctoral Research Associate(s) with a strong interest in interdisciplinary research on Robotics and Autonomous Systems within the context of construction robotics, human-robot collaboration, and advanced manufacturing. The role is especially suitable for candidates with a strength in robot learning and autonomy who want to see learning-based methods deployed on real and construction-scale robotic platforms. Example research directions may include: Training visuomotor policies for dexterous manipulation and long-horizon assembly, leveraging imitation learning, reinforcement learning, diffusion policies, and/or VLA-style approaches Integrating multimodal perception with task and motion planning for robust building-scale assembly, using RGB/RGB-D sensing, multi-view geometry, and 3D reconstruction Enabling reliable sim-to-real deployment for long-horizon tasks, including domain randomization, uncertainty-aware control, and safety/constraint handling Designing and evaluating human\u2013robot collaboration and shared autonomy for construction workflows, including interaction paradigms, communication interfaces, and performance metrics Developing closed-loop pipelines for building-scale additive manufacturing, including online state estimation, adaptive toolpath re-planning, and process control to improve geometric fidelity, inter-layer adhesion, and robustness to environmental and material variability Primary responsibilities include: Leading collaborative research projects from problem formulation through experimental validation and publication Conducting research and disseminating results through top-tier conferences and journals (e.g., ICRA, CoRL, The International Journal of Robotics Research, Automation in Construction, Advanced Engineering Informatics) Collaborating on large-scale robotically fabricated demonstrators (planning, integration, field testing, and iteration) Contributing to lab operations (e.g., maintaining core software stacks, supporting experimental protocols, and mentoring graduate/undergraduate researchers) Collaborating with the PI and team on proposal development and research translation Required qualifications: Doctoral degree in a related field, such as Robotics, Computer Science, Mechanical/Aerospace Engineering, Civil/Construction Engineering, Architecture, or related disciplines Excellent track record of research and publications related to the job description Strong scientific writing and communication skills Excellent programming skills (Python required; C++ preferred; C#/Unity a plus if you work in simulation or digital-twin environments) Fluency in English Desired qualifications: Experience with Robot Operating System (ROS) (ROS 2 preferred) Experience with sensing technologies, computer vision, and perception Demonstrated experience with machine learning for robotics (e.g., training/evaluating policies in PyTorch/JAX; imitation learning, reinforcement learning, or diffusion-based policy learning) Experience with Generative AI, specifically Large Language Models (LLMs), Vision Language Models (VLMs), and/or Vision-Language-Action Models (VLAs) as applied to robotics (e.g., grounding, tool use, policy learning, or multimodal reasoning) Experience developing custom tools and end effectors for robotic assembly and advanced manufacturing Experience with robotics simulation and evaluation (e.g., MuJoCo, Isaac Sim/Gym, PyBullet) and/or motion planning frameworks (e.g., MoveIt/OMPL) Evidence of strong software engineering practices (version control, testing, documentation, reproducibility) and, ideally, open-source contributions Application Instruction Applicants must apply online via the application portal and submit: Cover letter (summarizing research fit and interests) Curriculum vitae (CV) Three publication samples (conference and/or journal papers) Portfolio (optional; recommended for candidates with substantial design/fabrication work) Contact information for three references We will review applications on a rolling basis, and the application portal will stay open until the position is filled. For full consideration, please submit your application as soon as possible. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. If you have questions regarding the position(s), please contact Dr. Arash Adel ( arash.adel@princeton.edu ). We look forward to receiving your application."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181066": {"title": "***Revised***Associate Professional Specialist", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Architecture Location: Princeton University Overview Adel Research Group (ARG) at Princeton University invites applications for a full-time Associate Professional Specialist to support the lab's operational and research activities. This position is designed for an experienced scholar who will play an active role in lab management, research coordination, grants, and public relations, while also contributing directly to ongoing and new research projects. The position will work closely with Dr. Arash Adel (Assistant Professor of Architecture; Core Faculty, Princeton Robotics; Associated Faculty, Computer Science) and include opportunities for collaboration across Princeton Robotics labs. Appointments are for one year, with the possibility of renewal contingent on satisfactory performance and continued funding. About Us ARG is an interdisciplinary laboratory for advanced research at the intersection of robotics, artificial intelligence (AI), and computational design. We develop learning-enabled and perception-driven robotic systems that operate in complex, unstructured environments, with particular focus on: 1) Robot learning and embodied AI; 2) Multimodal perception and state estimation; 3) Closed-loop planning and control; and 4) End-to-end design-to-fabrication workflows. ARG\u2019s research has been supported by highly competitive grants, including a National Science Foundation\u2019s Future of Work at the Human-Technology Frontier grant. Job Description We seek an outstanding Associate Professional Specialist to join our team. In addition to conducting collaborative research, this role will help oversee day-to-day lab operations, support strategic planning, and coordinate activities across research, administration, and outreach. Primary responsibilities include: Providing assistance in managing the lab\u2019s research operations, including coordination of postdoctoral researchers, PhD students, master\u2019s students, undergraduate researchers, and visiting scholars Supporting the preparation, coordination, and submission of grant proposals, including assisting with technical narratives, budgets, timelines, and reporting Assisting with lab-level project management, including milestones, deliverables, and coordination across funded research projects Supporting public relations and outreach activities, including preparing research communications, coordinating exhibitions, and assisting with press and award submissions Contributing to research and disseminating results through top-tier conferences and journals (e.g., ICRA, CoRL, The International Journal of Robotics Research, Automation in Construction, Advanced Engineering Informatics). Assisting with the development of new research directions, collaborations, and partnerships Supporting the robotic fabrication and construction research agenda of the lab, including experimental design and implementation Required qualifications: Doctoral degree in a related field such as Architecture, Civil/Construction Engineering, Robotics, Mechanical/Aerospace Engineering, Computer Science, or related disciplines Strong track record of peer-reviewed research and publications relevant to construction robotics or closely related fields Demonstrated experience managing or coordinating research projects, teams, or laboratories Excellent scientific writing, communication, and organizational skills Fluency in English Desired qualifications: Prior experience supporting or leading grant writing and funded research projects Experience mentoring students and junior researchers Background in sensing technologies, computer vision, or perception Foundational knowledge of machine learning and data-driven methods Experience with robotic fabrication, custom tools, or end effector development Experience with Robot Operating System (ROS) Strong programming skills (Python required; C++ and/or C# desirable) Knowledge of Rhinoceros 3D, Grasshopper 3D, and RhinoCommon Professional experience in architecture, construction, or advanced fabrication Applicants must apply online via the application portal and submit: Cover letter (summarizing research fit and interests) Curriculum vitae (CV) Three publication samples (conference and/or journal papers) Portfolio (optional; recommended for candidates with substantial design/fabrication work) Contact information for three references We will review applications on a rolling basis, and the application portal will stay open until the position is filled. For full consideration, please submit your application as soon as possible. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H\u20111B visa requiring consular processing. If you have questions regarding the position, please contact Dr. Arash Adel ( arash.adel@princeton.edu ). We look forward to receiving your application."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191961": {"title": "Associate Professor, Professor in Architectural Design", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Architecture Location: Princeton University The School of Architecture at Princeton University invites applications for a full-time tenured position in architectural design at the Associate Professor or full Professor level. Candidates must be qualified to teach and advise both undergraduate and graduate students and must present a strong portfolio of creative professional work or of independent, research-oriented work. Speculative and realized projects are equally welcome, whether buildings, landscapes, exhibitions, installations, or publications. The successful candidate is expected to demonstrate an approach to architecture that expands the field and that integrates design, history and theory, and science and technology. Princeton seeks candidates who will participate actively in design reviews and other school activities beyond their regular course offerings, and who, through their creative practice, teaching, and service, will contribute to the excellence of the academic community at large. Prior teaching experience is required. The position will begin in July 2027.\u00a0 Please apply online at https://apply.interfolio.com/191961 with a letter of interest, personal/research/teaching statment, a complete CV and Bibliography, a portfolio of work and the names of three references.\u00a0\u00a0For fullest consideration, please apply by November 1, 2026. Referees will be contacted for those applicants advancing to the second stage, when additional material will be requested. Review of applications will begin November 1 and continue until the position is filled. (Note: only electronic submissions will be accepted.) This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194279": {"title": "Fellow in Architecture, Urbanism and the Humanities; Princeton Initiative in Architecture, Urbanism and the Humanities Untitled Position", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Architecture Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Princeton Initiative in Architecture, Urbanism, and the Humanities is seeking fellows to be appointed at the rank of postdoctoral research associates or more senior applications for the 2026-2027 academic year. Appointment rank will be determined based on the applicant's qualifications at the time of appointment. The Princeton Initiative in Architecture, Urbanism, and the Humanities is an interdisciplinary program that combines the efforts of a diverse group of faculty, programs, and schools to develop a dynamic understanding of urban issues past, present, and future. Its theme, Cities on the Edge , encompasses several interrelated concepts, including the juncture of built/natural environmental studies, center/ periphery, hemispheric comparatives, migration, New Jersey urbanism, social justice, the humanities as a force of change, and the margin as a place of radical possibility. Additional information about its themes and program may be found at https://arc-hum.princeton.edu/ . We seek to hire fellows with an abiding interest in multi-disciplinary work focused on the intersection of architecture, urbanism, and the humanities. Candidates can come from any discipline. The individual may teach or team-teach an interdisciplinary course on some aspect of urbanism (contingent upon sufficient enrollments and approval from the Dean of the Faculty) and participate in Initiative events. Fields of specialization might include (but are not limited to) the humanistic dimensions of architecture, architectural history, design, urban planning, public policy, urban studies, environmental studies, science and technology studies, geography, history, philosophy, art history, material culture, politics, sociology, anthropology, literature, religion, cultural studies, queer studies, race and ethnicity studies, gender studies, performance studies, visual arts, documentary studies, photography, and creative writing. Fellows may focus on any geographic area. We particularly encourage applications from scholars whose work incorporates the themes of the Initiative as well as the Princeton University Humanities Council, M.S. Chadha Center for Global India, and the School of Architecture. More information can be found here https://humanities.princeton.edu/ and https://soa.princeton.edu/ . We will accept applications from those who have earned a Ph.D. in any discipline (or those who expect to earn their doctorate before December 2026). Applicants must apply online and submit a cover letter, vita, 500-word description of a proposed course (uploaded under \u201cTeaching Statement\u201d), brief (chapter or article-length) single-authored writing sample, 1,000-word description of a research project, and contact information for three references by October 21, 2026, 11:59 pm, EST for full consideration. Letters of recommendation will be requested for a smaller group of applicants at a later date. The Fellows will receive $75,000, plus benefits, for a 12-month appointment with an anticipated start date in January 2027. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190626": {"title": "Assistant, Associate, Full Professor", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Classics Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Classics at Princeton University announces a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor in Roman History, starting in Fall 2027. Applications are welcome from candidates in any area of historical research related to the Roman world to 300 CE. We seek to identify a candidate who has an excellent track record or demonstrates exceptional promise as a scholar and teacher, whose interests complement those already represented among departmental faculty, and who can help us attract and retain a diverse student body. The successful candidate will be able to teach a range of undergraduate and graduate courses in Roman History, as well as Latin at all levels, and a graduate course in theory and methods. Engagement with the interdepartmental Program in the Ancient World is expected. Those wishing to be considered for this position are requested to submit the following materials by October 1 st : a cover letter, a CV, a writing sample of no more than 30 pages, and the names of three referees whom they will ask to provide letters of recommendation. We invite candidates to include in their cover letter a discussion of their prospective contributions to maintaining a culture of intellectual diversity. These materials should be submitted online. Candidates should ask their reference writers to submit their letters by October 1st, 2026 , when review of applications will begin. We plan to conduct the first round of interviews with selected candidates in November and invite finalists to campus in December. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. PhD required. Those wishing to be considered for this position are requested to submit the following materials by October 1 st : a cover letter, a CV, a writing sample of no more than 30 pages, and the names of three referees whom they will ask to provide letters of recommendation. We invite candidates to include in their cover letter a discussion of their prospective contributions to maintaining a culture of intellectual diversity. These materials should be submitted online. Candidates should ask their reference writers to submit their letters by October 1st, 2026 , when review of applications will begin."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192160": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Classics Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Classics at Princeton University may have occasional openings for Lecturers to teach either an undergraduate course or precepts (sections) for undergraduate courses during the 2026-2027 academic year and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. These courses may focus on Greek or Latin literature and culture, Greek or Roman history, or the Latin language. Such positions would require teaching courses and/or leading discussions, attending course lectures, being available for meetings with the course instructor(s), holding regular on-campus office hours, and grading student exams and papers. Appointment duty time is subject to enrollments, and any available position may be for the Fall 2026 and/or Spring 2027 semesters, with the potential for renewal depending on departmental need. Essential Qualifications: 1) Advanced degree in Classics or related field required, PhD preferred, and 2) Prior teaching experience at the college or university level. Interested candidates should apply online with a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and the names and contact information for three references. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and the Department may not review all applications it receives. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193338": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in the Cognitive Science of Values", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Cognitive Science Location: Peretsman Scully Hall The Program in Cognitive Science, in collaboration with the University Center for Human Values, invites applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate in the Cognitive Science of Values for a position expected to start in September of 2027. The successful candidate will be based in the laboratory of Dr. Tania Lombrozo in the Department of Psychology. We aim to support a highly promising scholar with a background in cognitive science or a related discipline, such as psychology or empirically informed/experimental philosophy. The scholar's research agenda should address a topic that engages with both cognitive science and values, such as the role of moral values in decision making or the role of epistemic values in belief revision. The proposed research is expected to yield both theoretical and empirical publications. Candidates will be expected to contribute the equivalent of one course each year to the University Center and/or the Department. This contribution may be fulfilled by teaching a course on a topic related to cognitive science of values ( subject to sufficient enrollments and approval by Project Directors, the Department Chair or Chairs, and the Office of the Dean of the Faculty) or service to the Project or Center of some other sort, subject to approval of the Project and Center Directors. If teaching a semester-long course, the successful candidate would carry the additional part-time rank of Lecturer. The postdoc will be expected to participate in a year-long research seminar for visiting fellows and Center faculty, as well as a year-long seminar for the Center\u2019s cohort of Postdoctoral Associates. (Each of these seminars meets roughly every other week.)\u00a0 The postdoc will also be expected to regularly attend talks, panels, and conferences sponsored by the Center. The candidate will be appointed in the Program in Cognitive Science and will be invited to participate in programs of the University Center for Human Values. Applications are welcome from candidates who have or expect to have a Ph.D. by the appointment start date. Applicants should not have more than two years of postdoctoral experience by the start of appointment. Applications will be evaluated based on the applicant's previous accomplishments, the promise of the proposed research project in the cognitive science of values, and the applicant's ability to contribute to the intellectual life of the Program in Cognitive Science, the University Center for Human Values, and Dr. Lombrozo's laboratory group. Term of Appointment The term of appointment is one year, expected to begin on September 1, 2027, with the possibility of renewal for up to two additional years subject to satisfactory performance, eligibility, meeting all requirements, and continued funding. Appointment will be made to the rank of Postdoctoral Research Associate. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Candidates must submit an online application in Interfolio. A complete application will require: A current curriculum vitae; A brief research proposal (not to exceed 1,000 words including the title), describing the kind of research that the candidate would plan to pursue in this position; A representative manuscript or publication; Contact information for three referees. Referees will be contacted directly. Materials should be submitted online by December 7, 2026, 11:59 pm, EST for full consideration. Letters of reference are to be submitted by the end of the business day on December 11, 2026, 5:00PM EST. We will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. We expect to announce decisions in March 2027.\u00a0This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195297": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in the Social Science of Human Values", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Cognitive Science Location: Peretsman Scully Hall The Program in Cognitive Science and the University Center for Human Values invites applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate in the Social Science of Human Values for a position expected to start in July 2027. The successful candidate will be based in the laboratory of Dr. Molly Crockett in the Department of Psychology. We aim to support a highly promising scholar with a background in the quantitative social sciences or a related discipline, including psychology, computational social science, cognitive science, or empirically informed/experimental philosophy. The scholar's research agenda should address a topic that engages with the social science of human values, such as the role of moral values in decision making, the role of epistemic values in scientific practice, or the expression of values in collective behaviors (e.g., in online social networks). The proposed research is expected to yield both theoretical and empirical publications. The candidate will be appointed in the Program in Cognitive Science and will be expected to contribute to programs of the University Center for Human Values (UCHV). This contribution may be fulfilled by organizing a workshop, mentoring PhD students, or service to the Future Values Initiative, subject to approval of the Initiative and UCHV directors. The postdoc will be expected to participate in a year-long research seminar for visiting fellows and UCHV faculty, as well as a year-long seminar for UCHV\u2019s cohort of Postdoctoral Associates. (Each of these seminars meets roughly every other week.)\u00a0 The postdoc will also be expected to regularly attend talks, panels, and conferences sponsored by UCHV. The term of appointment is one year, expected to begin July 1, 2027, with the possibility of renewal for up to two additional years subject to satisfactory performance, continued funding and meeting all requirements. Appointment will be made to the rank of Postdoctoral Research Associate. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Applications are welcome from candidates who have or expect to have a Ph.D. by the start date. Applicants should have no more than two years of post-Ph.D. research experience by the start of the appointment. Applications will be evaluated based on the applicant's previous accomplishments, the promise of the proposed research project in the cognitive science of values, and the applicant's ability to contribute to the intellectual life of the University Center for Human Values, and Dr. Crockett\u2019s laboratory group. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Candidates must submit an online application in Interfolio. A complete application will require: A current curriculum vitae; A brief research proposal (not to exceed 1,000 words including the title), describing the kind of research that the candidate would plan to pursue in this position; A representative preprint or publication; Contact information for three referees. Referees will be contacted directly. We will solicit letters of reference from shortlisted candidates. Materials should be submitted online by November 20, 2026, 11:59 pm, EST for full consideration. We will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. We expect to announce decisions in February 2027. This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191304": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Comparative Literature Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Comparative Literature at Princeton University has an occasional curricular need for lecturers to teach, co-teach, or lead discussion sections in any comparative literature area, including any period (e.g., medieval, early modern), any language (e.g., Asian, European, African languages), and any texts (e.g., novels, film, popular culture). The department accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. Those who compare texts across two or more languages would be prioritized. Any appointments and duty time are subject to enrollments, and would have an expected start date of September 1, 2026, or January 16, 2027. Please provide a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a statement of teaching interest, and the names and contact information for three references. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need. The department may or may not review all applications it receives, and only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194499": {"title": "Professor/Associate Professor", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Comparative Literature Location: East Pyne Hall Princeton University seeks outstanding applicants for a tenured position in literary and/or cultural studies at the rank of advanced Associate Professor or Professor with an effective start date as early as September 1, 2027. The successful candidate will be jointly appointed in Princeton\u2019s Department of Comparative Literature and either the Department of French and Italian OR the Department of Spanish and Portuguese. The search is open to candidates specializing in any field, but applicants must have an exceptional record of scholarly productivity and work in two or more languages other than English. One of the languages must be French, Spanish, or Portuguese, and the other language(s) may be European or non-European. The successful candidate should have a Ph.D. in literary and/or cultural studies, demonstrated excellence in academic research, and a strong interest in teaching and advising undergraduate and graduate students. We seek faculty members who will create a climate that embraces excellence, with a commitment to teaching and mentoring that will enhance the work of the departments and attract and retain a diverse student body. Candidates must complete an online faculty application at https://apply.interfolio.com/194499 . A cover letter, curriculum vitae, and the names and contact information for three references should be provided via the online application. This position is subject to Princeton University\u2019s background check policy. The departments will begin reviewing applications on December 15, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194670": {"title": "Lecturer, Council of the Humanities", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Council of the Humanities Location: Princeton, NJ The Council of Humanities and the Program in European Cultural Studies at Princeton University invites applications for one or more part-time Lecturer positions in the spring semester of the 2026-2027 academic year, to co-teach the course Architectural Colonialities: Building European Power and/or teach an additional course in European Cultural Studies. Teaching responsibilities will include in-person teaching, grading student work, and holding on campus office hours. Position duty time will be contingent on course enrollment. These positions are expected to start on January 16, 2027. Review of applications will begin immediately. For fullest consideration, please apply by December 7, 2026. Letters of reference will be solicited at the shortlist stage. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing. 1) Advanced degree required, PhD preferred, and 2) Prior teaching experience at the college or university level. Interested candidates should apply online with a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and the names and contact information for three references. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189234": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: East Asian Studies Location: Princeton, NJ Chinese Language Lecturer The Department of East Asian Studies invites applications for one or more positions as lecturer in Chinese language, expected to begin September 1, 2026, or during the 2026-2027 academic year. Positions that begin September 1 are expected to extend for one academic year. While duty time and appointment length will be based on student enrollments, we expect to fill at least one full- or part-time position. Experience teaching Chinese at the college level is preferred. We seek candidates with a minimum requirement of a master's degree in disciplines such as Chinese linguistics, Chinese language pedagogy, Chinese history, Chinese literature, or related fields. Ph.D. is preferred. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H1-B visa requiring visa consular processing. Experience teaching Chinese at the college level is preferred. We seek candidates with a minimum requirement of a master's degree in disciplines such as Chinese linguistics, Chinese language pedagogy, Chinese history, Chinese literature, or related fields. Ph.D. is preferred. Applications must be submitted online and should include: a cover letter curriculum vitae statement of teaching interest contact information for three reference (Reference letters will be requested after a Zoom interview.) and a web link to a 30-50-minute video of the candidate's Chinese language teaching. Submission of a writing sample is optional (e.g., publications in Chinese or English, a dissertation chapter)."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190630": {"title": "Lecturer in English", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: English Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of English at Princeton University has an occasional curricular need for lecturers to teach, co-teach, and/or lead precepts (sections) in department courses, and accepts applications in advance in the event such lecturers are needed. Any successful candidate will attend weekly lectures, lead weekly discussions, hold regular on-campus office hours, meet with the course instructor(s), and assess student work. Appointments and duty time assignments are subject to enrollments, and any available position may be for the Fall 2026 and/or Spring 2027 semester, with the potential for renewal depending on departmental need. Essential Qualification: Advanced degree required; Ph.D. preferred. Preferred Qualification: Experience teaching at the undergraduate level. Please provide a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a statement of teaching interest, and the name and contact information for one reference. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need. The Department may or may not review all applications it receives, and only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. Any available position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. The work location for any available position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187419": {"title": "Lecturer in French", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: French & Italian Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of French and Italian has an occasional curricular need to appoint Lecturers in French to teach a range of courses in language and culture in a dynamic undergraduate program committed to innovation and excellence, and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. Such positions would require teaching courses and/or course sections (including precepts), grading student work, and holding weekly office hours. Part-time or full-time positions for the fall 2026 and/or spring 2027 semesters may be available, dependent upon the needs of the Department and the qualifications of the candidates. Possibility of renewal will be based on course enrollments and satisfactory performance.\u00a0 Review of applications will begin July 15, 2026, and continue until any available position(s) are filled. Native or near-native fluency in French and English, proven experience teaching French as a Foreign Language at the university level, and excellent teaching credentials. Master\u2019s degree or enrollment in a Ph.D. program required; Ph.D. preferred. Experience in culture-based instruction, integration of new technologies and curriculum development. Demonstrated interest in francophone studies or film and media will be a plus. Ability to supervise and mentor graduate student instructors is highly desirable. Preference will be given to candidates with a background in French as a Foreign Language and/or Linguistics, SLA or Applied Linguistics, although other fields will be considered. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and the Department may not review all applications it receives. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. Applicants are to submit the following documents: Curriculum vitae, including description of teaching experience Cover letter that describes teaching interests, areas of expertise, and scholarly experience in the relevant fields Statement of teaching: no more than 500 words explaining how the candidate has approached or would approach the courses they wish to teach Two recent teaching evaluations Complete contact information for two professional references This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H1-B visa requiring visa consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187438": {"title": "Lecturer in Italian", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: French & Italian Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of French and Italian has an occasional curricular need to appoint Lecturers in Italian to teach a range of courses in language and culture in a dynamic undergraduate program committed to innovation, excellence, and inclusivity, and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. Such positions would require teaching courses and/or course sections (including precepts), grading student work, and holding weekly office hours. Any appointments and duty time are subject to enrollments and departmental need, and available position(s) are expected to begin September 1, 2026, or January 16, 2027. Review of applications will begin July 15, 2026, and continue until any available position(s) are filled. Native or near-native fluency in Italian and English, proven experience teaching Italian as a Foreign Language at the university level, and experience in program and curricular development is highly desirable. Master\u2019s degree or enrollment in a Ph.D. program required; Ph.D. preferred. Experience in culture-based instruction, integration of new technologies and curriculum development. Preference will be given to candidates with a background in Italian as a Foreign Language and/or Linguistics, SLA or Applied Linguistics, although other fields will be considered. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and the Department may not review all applications it receives. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. Applicants are to submit the following documents: Curriculum vitae, including description of teaching experience Cover letter that describes teaching interests, areas of expertise, and scholarly experience in the relevant fields Statement of teaching: no more than 500 words explaining how the candidate has approached or would approach the courses they wish to teach Two recent teaching evaluations Complete contact information for two professional references This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H1-B visa requiring visa consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194502": {"title": "Postdoctoral Reseach Associate - Seeger Center for Hellenic Studies", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Hellenic Studies Location: Princeton, NJ The Seeger Center for Hellenic Studies at Princeton University invites applications for two postdoctoral fellowships: (1) The Hannah Seeger Davis Postdoctoral Research Fellowship in Hellenic Studies and (2) The Mary Seeger O'Boyle Postdoctoral Research Fellowship in Hellenic Studies. Scholars in all humanities and social sciences disciplines are eligible to apply. The appointment is expected to start on August 1, 2027 and end on August 1, 2028 , with the possibility of renewal for a second year, following academic review, funding availability, and meeting all other requirements. Candidates must have completed all requirements for the doctoral degree by February 1, 2027 . Candidates must have no more than two years of post-Ph.D. research experience by the start date. Candidates may apply for reimbursement (up to $3,000 per academic year) for research-related expenses. Fellows will be required to conduct research full-time and demonstrate the ability to be on campus daily to fulfill responsibilities related to in-person participation. This position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Fellows are eligible to work remotely one day per week, subject to the requirement that it does not conflict with any events. This position does not include reimbursement of relocation or housing expenses. Candidates may have the opportunity to spend time at the Princeton Athens Center, conducting research during the summer (June/July) and/or winter (December/January). Travel grants will be available via application. If awarded, the grant will be towards travel and lodging during time in residence at the Princeton Athens Center. These fellowships are intended for scholars in Hellenic Studies, with a special emphasis on Modern Greek Studies, Byzantine Studies, or Late Antique Studies, including their relation to the Classical tradition. This postdoctoral research fellowship program aims to advance the scholarship of outstanding Hellenists at an early stage in their careers and thus strengthen the field of post-Classical Greek Studies in the United States and abroad. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate . Candidates must submit: (1) A cover letter with a title and summary (200 words) of the proposed research project; (2) A research proposal (five pages; 2,000 words), including a detailed description of the project and timetable, explicit goals, selected bibliography, and the reason it is proposed to be pursued at Princeton; (3) Curriculum vitae with list of publications; (4) Sample chapter (in English) of dissertation or other recent work; (5) Names and contact information of three referees from individuals who are not current members of the Princeton University faculty; (6) A clear, not password-protected PDF scan of your doctoral degree. Our application system cannot process locked files. Or a letter from a dean or registrar-level officer on department letterhead confirming the date of completion of all the requirements for the doctoral degree. With the exception of the official transcripts, all submitted documents must be in English. DEADLINE: All materials must be received by 11:59 pm EST on December 15, 2026. All reference letters must be received by 11:59 pm EST on December 18, 2026 . Awards will be announced by early March 2027 . This position is subject to the University's background check policy. For the full posting with all relevant details, please visit: https://hellenic.princeton.edu/opportunities/postdoctoral-fellowships-hellenic-studies"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194879": {"title": "Visiting Fellow - Seeger Center for Hellenic Studies", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Hellenic Studies Location: Princeton, NJ The Seeger Center for Hellenic Studies at Princeton University invites applications for a limited number of Stanley J. Seeger Visiting Research Fellowships for scholars based in Greece or other international countries, who wish to spend time in residence at Princeton, pursuing research projects in the humanities or the social sciences, free of teaching and other obligations. Individuals based in the United States are not eligible to apply. Fall term appointments are expected from September 1, 2027 until December 16, 2027 (3.5 months). Spring term appointments are expected from January 15, 2028 unti l May 16, 2028 (4 months). Summer appointments are expected from June 1, 2028 until August 16, 2028 (2.5 months). \u00a0These fellowships are residential, and, therefore, fellows must be at Princeton at all times, devoting the majority of their time to research and writing. They are expected to participate in Hellenic Studies activities and the University's intellectual life. They present their work-in-progress at Hellenic Studies workshops and are encouraged to meet colleagues in their respective academic disciplines. They enjoy full access to the University's library, archival, and computing resources. Eligibility: (a) scholars in all humanities or social science disciplines affiliated with Greek academic institutions, including universities and research institutes; (b) professionals who are affiliated with Greek institutions: libraries, museums, the Archaeological Service, government agencies, etc.; (c) established independent scholars (i.e., individuals not affiliated with an institution), who are residents of Greece or other international countries, and have a distinguished record of published work; (d) Hellenic studies scholars, irrespective of nationality, who hold regular faculty or research appointments at academic institutions in international countries other than Greece, and who specialize in Classical reception studies, Late Antique studies, Byzantine studies, or Modern Greek studies. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. or equivalent doctoral degree. Fellows normally receive a monthly salary of at least $5,290. The salary level may be adjusted if scholars receive outside funding. Fellows are offered modestly furnished, one-bedroom, non-smoking, and pet-free apartments. Fellows are responsible for their own visa, travel expenses, and arrangements. Qualified candidates must apply online at https://apply.interfolio.com/14427/positions . For applications to be considered, the following material must be uploaded via the online application: A cover sheet with a title and summary (200 words) of the proposed research project and a brief bio (one paragraph); A research statement (five pages; 2,000 words max.), including a detailed description of the project, timetable, explicit goals, and a rationale for why it should be pursued at Princeton; Selected bibliography; A standard, detailed curriculum vitae including a list of publications; Two samples of recent work (in English); and All non-US citizens and non-US permanent residents are required to provide TOEFL results or equivalent, or a letter from their department head on department letterhead confirming certification of language proficiency in English. All submitted documents must be in English. You will be asked for contact information for two referees who are not current members of the Princeton University faculty. DEADLINE: All materials must be received by 11:59 p.m. EST on Tuesday, January 5, 2027. All reference letters must be received by 11:59 p.m. EST on Friday, January 8, 2027. We will announce fellowship awards\u00a0in late March 2027. Please review the more detailed job description and qualifications at the following link: https://hellenic.princeton.edu/opportunities/stanley-j-seeger-visiting-research-fellowships"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185435": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Program in Latin American Studies", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Latin American Studies Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Program in Latin American Studies (PLAS) is seeking candidates from any discipline who are engaged in scholarly research on topics related to Latin American Studies, including the Caribbean and Brazil, to fill Postdoctoral Research Associate or more senior research positions. \u00a0Although the call is open, scholars working on policy and public health, the history of disease, international relations, or economic development in the region are particularly encouraged to apply. Candidates will be expected to devote themselves to research and writing and may teach not more than one undergraduate course per semester. When teaching, successful candidates will carry the additional part-time rank of Lecturer, and any teaching opportunities are subject to sufficient enrollmens and the approval of the Dean of the Faculty. Postdoctoral associates are expected to participate regularly in the scholarly and academic activities of the program. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for a 12-month term during the 2027-2028 academic year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. Start date is around September 1, 2027. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Doctoral degree. Academic excellence, potential to bring new ideas and approaches to Princeton University and to interact successfully with a broad range of faculty and students. All candidates must submit the below materials (in English): \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Cover letter \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Curriculum vitae \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Statement of research interests (1,000 \u2013 1,500 words) \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A representative sample of recent work (under 30 written pages or equivalent] \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Syllabi for two potential undergraduate courses \u00b7 Three letters of recommendation"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194387": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Sharmin & Bijan Mossavar-Rahmani Center for Iran/Persian Gulf Studies Location: Princeton University The Sharmin and Bijan Mossavar-Rahmani Center for Iran and Persian Gulf Studies at Princeton University invites applications for the position of Postdoctoral Research Associate (PDRA) or a more senior rank in the relevant fields of Iran and the Persian Gulf in the modern period. Anticipated to start in September 2027, the position is open to scholars of all academic disciplines in the humanities and social sciences. Postdoctoral appointments will be for one year, renewable annually based on funding and meeting all other requirements, for a total of up to three years . The Center promotes interdisciplinary approaches to advancing the study of Iran and the Persian Gulf, with special attention to the region's role and significance in the contemporary world. The goal of the program is to support outstanding scholars of modern Iran at an early stage of their careers and thus to strengthen the field of Iranian and Persian Gulf Studies in the United States and abroad. In addition to their salary, researchers receive funding (up to $4,000 per academic year) for research related expenses (books, conferences, travel expenses, etc.). The Center provides office space and staff support as well as a taxable moving allowance to help defray moving expenses. Fellows are expected to be on campus during the academic year to participate fully in the intellectual life of the Center and the wider University community. Applicants are asked to submit an application by Sunday, November 22, 2026 (11:59 p.m. ET) . The number of fellowships offered each year is contingent on funding. The work location for these positions is in-person, on campus at Princeton University, and the positions are subject to the University's background check policy. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applicants should have (or expect to have by the start of the appointment) a PhD. Applicants should have received their PhD not more than 2 years before the start of the appointment. Appointment cannot be deferred to a later term. Selection is based on exceptional scholarly achievement and evidence of unusual promise, range and quality of teaching experience, and potential contributions to an interdisciplinary community. The Mossavar-Rahmani Center seeks a diverse and international pool of applicants. Applicants should submit: Cover letter Current curriculum vitae Research Proposal Bibliography Writing sample Names and contact information for three individuals who will provide letters of recommendation Dossier specifications and further information on the application process and timeline are detailed on the MRC\u2019s website ( iran.princeton.edu) . We recommend that applicants review it before completing their application."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189852": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Music Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Music at Princeton University may have an occasional curricular need for semester-long, non-tenure-track lecturer positions to teach or co-teach courses and/or lead ensembles, and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. Areas of study/practice may include electronic music, music production, music theory, music history, jazz tradition, musicology, repertoire, and more. The expected start dates would be September 1, 2026, or January 16, 2027, for any available work; any appointments are subject to enrollments and may be for one or two semesters during the 2026-2027 academic year, with the potential for renewal depending on departmental need. An advanced degree in a relevant field is preferred. Experience teaching undergraduate (A.B. or B.M.) level is strongly preferred. Candidates must apply online with a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and the names of three references. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and the Department may not review all applications it receives. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H\u20111B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189875": {"title": "Lecturer - Studio Lessons", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Music Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Music at Princeton University may have occasional openings for semester-long, non-tenure-track lecturer positions to teach in our studio lessons program, and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. Exact appointments and duty time are subject to enrollment in the studio lessons program and may be for one or two semesters during the 2026-2027 academic year, with the potential for renewal depending on departmental need. Responsibilities include teaching private instrumental or vocal lessons to Princeton University students. Lecturers perform their own administrative and clerical support functions, including but not limited to coordinating teaching schedules, entering grades, responding to student inquiries, writing letters of recommendation, and more. Other opportunities may include adjudicating juries, evaluating arts supplements in support of University admissions processes, and coaching student soloists and ensembles. Successful candidates may also have the opportunity to teach music courses subject to departmental need and approval by the Department Chair and Office of the Dean of the Faculty. Candidates must have a bachelor's degree in music or equivalent experience. They must also demonstrate effective studio teaching, ensemble skill-building, and knowledge of repertoire. Preference will be given to candidates with experience in a college or university setting, experience coaching soloists and ensembles, and teaching of master classes. Candidates must apply online with a cover letter, CV, and contact information for three references. If you would like to, you may provide a link to a representative video of a performance or teaching demonstration (not required). Applications will be reviewed based on need, and the Department may not review all applications it receives. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H\u20111B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191679": {"title": "***REVISED*** Assistant Professor", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Music Location: Princeton, NJ We seek a dynamic music scholar to join the Department of Music at Princeton University at the rank of Assistant Professor (tenure-track) beginning on September 1, 2027. Applicants from all areas and approaches within music scholarship are encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will complement and expand our current programs, and will serve as a committed advisor and mentor. Princeton is interested in receiving applications from individuals who, through their research, teaching, and service, will contribute to the diversity of the curriculum, broadly defined. We seek faculty members who will create a climate that embraces excellence, with a strong commitment to teaching and mentoring that will enhance the work of the department and that will attract and retain a diverse student body. Required qualifications: PhD in Music or a related field Preferred qualifications: Specialty in music studies, broadly defined Demonstrated interest in and commitment to student mentorship Innovative research program with the potential of transformative impact in the field Research specialty that complements and expands Princeton's current program Research specialty that connects to broader campus-wide areas of emphasis, such as the Princeton Humanities Initiative This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The application portal will close on October 30, 2026. Interested candidates should apply online with a cover letter, CV (including a publication list), and contact information for three references. No letters of recommendation are required until a later stage in the process."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193875": {"title": "Early Islamic History", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Near Eastern Studies Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Near Eastern Studies at Princeton University invites applications for a tenured or tenure-track faculty position in the field of Early Islamic History, with a primary focus on the period ca. 600\u20131300 CE. We seek a dynamic and innovative scholar whose research engages critically with the historical development of Islamic societies in this formative era. While the core of the position is historical in nature, we welcome applications from candidates whose work is grounded in related disciplines and whose scholarship brings fresh perspectives to the study of early Islam. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and research statement. Please also include the names and contact information for three references. Referees will not be contacted until later in the process. Junior level applicants need to also provide a writing sample, for example one chapter or an article. Review of applications will begin on October 30, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in a relevant field by the time of appointment. A strong record of original research is essential. Proficiency in classical Arabic and strong paleographic skills are also important. We are particularly interested in candidates who can contribute meaningfully to the department\u2019s teaching and research missions at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain an active research agenda, participate fully in departmental activities, and contribute to fostering a diverse and inclusive academic community. This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185637": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Philosophy Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Philosophy at Princeton University may have occasional openings for Lecturers to teach either an undergraduate course or precepts for undergraduate courses during the 2026-2027 academic year , and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. These courses may focus on areas such as ethics, logic, history of philosophy, and introductory philosophy. Serving in a preceptor capacity requires leading weekly discussions, attending course lectures, meeting with the course instructor(s), holding regular on-campus office hours, and grading student exams and papers. Appointment duty time will depend upon student enrollment and department need. Any positions are expected to begin September 1, 2026, or January 16, 2027, with the potential for renewal depending on departmental need. Essential Qualifications: 1) Advanced degree in Philosophy or related field required, PhD preferred, and 2) Prior teaching experience at the college or university level. To apply, please submit a cover letter, CV, and contact information for three references; no letters of recommendation are required at this time. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and the Department may not review all applications it receives. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. These positions are subject to the University background check policy. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/182519": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Religion Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Religion at Princeton University has an occasional curricular need for lecturers to teach courses in areas such as Asian Religions, Islam, Religions of the Mediterranean, Religion & Philosophy, Religion, Ethics & Politics, and Religions in the Americas, and accepts applications in advance if such lecturers are needed. Such positions would require teaching courses and/or precept sections, grading student work, and holding weekly office hours. Appointment duty time is subject to enrollments, and any available positions may be for the Fall 2026 and/or Spring 2027 semesters, depending on departmental need. Advanced degree required; Ph.D. preferred. Interested candidates should apply online with a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a brief statement of teaching experience and interest, and the names and contact information for three references. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and the Department may not review all applications it receives. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190680": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Slavic Lang & Literatures Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Slavic Languages and Literatures at Princeton University has an occasional curricular need for lecturers to teach undergraduate seminars or precepts (sections) in various areas of Slavic literature and culture and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. Such positions would require leading discussions, attending course lectures, being available for meetings with the course instructor(s), holding regular on-campus office hours, and grading student exams and papers. Any appointments are subject to enrollments and may be for one or two semesters during the 2026-27 academic year, with the potential for renewal depending on departmental need. An advanced degree is required; PhDs or doctoral candidates preferred. Please provide a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and the Department may not review all applications it receives. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181763": {"title": "Lecturer in Spanish", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Spanish & Portuguese Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Spanish and Portuguese invites applications for one or more positions as part-time or full-time Lecturers in Spanish, expected to begin September 1, 2026, or during the 2026-27 academic year, with the potential for renewal depending on departmental need and satisfactory performance. Any appointments are subject to enrollments and may be for one or two semesters during the 2026-27 academic year. Native or near-native fluency in Spanish and English, and proven experience in teaching Spanish at the university level, are absolutely essential. Master\u2019s degree or enrollment in a Ph.D. program required; Ph.D. or M.A. working toward Ph.D. preferred. Preference will be given to candidates with a background in Linguistics, SLA, or Applied Linguistics, although other fields will also be considered when there is evidence of sustained experience teaching Spanish at the university level. Proven experience in coordination (course, level, or program) and curriculum development will be a plus. Applications must include the following items: Curriculum vitae, including description of teaching experience Cover letter Teaching portfolio, including teaching philosophy, teacher evaluations, and sample syllabi and materials (lesson plans, reading guides, exams) developed by the candidate Contact information for three professional references. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until any positions are filled. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183743": {"title": "Lecturer in Theater", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Lewis Center for the Arts: Theater, LCA Location: Princeton, NJ The Theater and Music Theater program at the Lewis Center for the Arts, Princeton University, has occasional curricular need for part-time lecturers in all disciplines, and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. These positions are subject to enrollments and will be for one semester, non-tenure track, and potentially renewable, with expected start dates of September 1, 2026, or January 16, 2027. Teaching areas include Acting, Playwriting, Performance Criticism, Theater History, Directing, Dramaturgy, Producing for the Theater, Music Theater, Performance Art, and Theatrical Design. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy, and the work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H\u20111B visa requiring consular processing. Competitive candidates will be active teachers, practitioners, and/or scholars with a national reputation who demonstrate a deep commitment to undergraduate teaching and advising. To apply, please\u00a0submit a resume, cover letter and contact information for up to three references. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and the Department may not review all applications it receives. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193206": {"title": "Professor of Drama", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Lewis Center for the Arts: Theater, LCA Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of English and the Lewis Center for the Arts are seeking an outstanding tenured senior hire working in the field of drama broadly defined, including: the literature, history, practice, and theory of drama; dramatic criticism and the history of dramatic criticism; drama in relation to aesthetics and to other media; the theory and history of theatre and of theatrical performance. This is a joint position between the Theater and Music Theater Program of the Lewis Center for the Arts and the Department of English. In addition to an outstanding record of research and publication, the candidate will have evidence of dedication to teaching and advising both undergraduates and graduate students, and a willingness to serve as an active member of both English and the Lewis Center. We strongly encourage applications from all racial, cultural/national, and economic backgrounds. Applicants should submit a cover letter; curriculum vitae; names and contact information for three references; links to e-publications or copies of recent scholarly work (e.g., articles or monographs); a statement of teaching philosophy (one page maximum); and two sample syllabi, one at the graduate level and one at the undergraduate level. Applications received by December 1, 2026 will receive a thorough review; references will be contacted in the final stages of the search. This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183945": {"title": "Lecturer in Visual Arts", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Lewis Center for the Arts: Visual Arts, LCA Location: Princeton, NJ The Visual Arts program at the Lewis Center for the Arts, Princeton University, has occasional curricular need for part-time lecturers in all disciplines, and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. These positions are subject to enrollments and will be for one semester, non-tenure track, and potentially renewable, with expected start dates of September 1, 2026, or January 16, 2027. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy, and the work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University.\u00a0Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H\u20111B visa requiring consular processing. Competitive candidates are professional artists who demonstrate a commitment to undergraduate teaching and advising. Princeton is interested in candidates who, through their research, teaching, and service, will contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community. Preferred qualifications include an MFA or equivalent experience. To apply, please submit a resume, cover letter, sample syllabi from previous courses taught, and the names and contact information of three references. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and the Department may not review all applications it receives. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188299": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: African American Studies Location: Princeton The Department of African American Studies at Princeton University has an occasional curricular need for lecturers to teach, co-teach, or lead precepts in areas such as African American culture and life, race and public policy, or global race and ethnicity, and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. Any appointments and duty time are subject to enrollments, and may be for one or two semesters during the 2026-2027 academic year. Positions would have an expected start date of September 1, 2026, or January 16, 2027. An advanced degree is required. Please provide a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a statement of teaching interest, and the names and contact information for three references. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need. The department may or may not review all applications it receives, and only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University.The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188814": {"title": "UCHV Fellows in Law and Normative Thinking for Academic Year 2027-28", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Center for Human Values Location: Princeton, NJ, United States The Program in Law and Normative Thinking (PLANT) at the University Center for Human Values invites faculty members of any discipline, legal practitioners, and independent scholars to apply for visiting residential fellowships for 2027-28 devoted to the study of law, broadly conceived. Scholars are expected to reside in or around Princeton or demonstrate to the program\u2019s satisfaction the ability to be on campus daily. The fellowship is a full-time commitment that typically extends from September 1 to June 1. Fellows will devote the full academic year to research, discussion, and scholarly collaboration on topics related to law and normative inquiry. Under exceptional circumstances, fellowships for one semester may be considered. Scholars will participate in the weekly seminar of the Program in Law and Normative Thinking and will also be asked to spend some time mentoring JD/PhD students who attend that seminar. Applicants must have a doctorate, juris doctor, or an equivalent professional degree at the time of submission. The program is open to senior and junior scholars, domestic and international scholars, and those based in law schools or in the practice of law and those who are based in other disciplines. This is not, however, a post-doctoral or visiting assistant professor opportunity designed to launch young scholars into academic careers. The selection committee looks closely at the research proposal. Successful applicants should demonstrate substantial expertise in law-related matters, but in explaining research projects, applicants should write for an audience of academic generalists (not necessarily lawyers). The selection committee will evaluate applicants on: the quality of their achievements in their field of specialization and their ability to benefit from the activities of the program; the quality and significance of their proposed research projects and writing sample; the contributions they are likely to make in the future to legal scholarship and practice and their ability to contribute both to legal studies at Princeton and to the programs and mission of the University Center for Human Values . Candidates must submit an online application. Required materials: 1.\u00a0 A cover letter explaining your intellectual trajectory and suitability for the fellowship 2.\u00a0 A CV 3.\u00a0 A research statement of maximum 1,500 words for a project to be pursued in the course of the fellowship 4.\u00a0 A writing sample consisting of a single article or chapter (published or unpublished) 5.\u00a0 Contact information for two referees whom we will ask for letters (note that referees will be asked to send in their letters fairly quickly \u2013 by December 10, 2026, for best consideration). The deadline for submission is December 3, 2026 , 11:59 p.m. EST. Letters of reference should be submitted by December 10, 2026, 11:59 p.m. EST. The anticipated start date is on or about September 1, 2027, with some limited flexibility. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H\u20111B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193832": {"title": "*Revised* Professor of Bioethics", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Center for Human Values Location: Princeton, NJ Princeton University invites applications from scholars with a strong interest in bioethics broadly construed (including biomedical ethics, environmental ethics, animal ethics, and the ethical implications of recent developments in the life sciences) and who is also attentive to discussions of policy and of individual choice. Appointment is expected to be at the rank of Full Professor, to begin on or after July 1, 2027. The successful candidate would join the University Center for Human Values: an interdisciplinary community of scholars whose additional principal affiliations currently include history, philosophy, politics, psychology, public policy and international affairs, religion, and sociology.\u00a0 Scholars of distinction with research interests and training in philosophy, political theory, legal scholarship, religious ethics and related areas, as well as those working on any of the ethical issues described above in other disciplines or from interdisciplinary approaches, are encouraged to apply. \u00a0A joint appointment with an appropriate discipline may be considered. Princeton University is interested in all qualified candidates who can contribute, through their research, teaching, and/or service, to the intellectual diversity and excellence of our academic community. This position is subject to Princeton University's background check policy. For full consideration, applications must be received by November 1, 2026. Applications require a letter of application, a curriculum vitae, a sample of writing in the candidate's field of specialization, and the names of three referees (whom we would approach only after notification of shortlisted candidates)."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190688": {"title": "Associate Professor or Professor", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Princeton University Department of Economics The Department of Economics seeks a distinguished senior faculty member in the field of Industrial Organization or International Economics. Candidates must complete an online faculty application at https://apply.interfolio.com/190688. Complete applications, including cover letter and CV will be reviewed beginning September 15, 2026. Applicants should provide contact information for three references; no references will be contacted until the final stage of the hiring process. Princeton is especially interested in candidates who, through their research, teaching and service, will contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter and a CV. Please provide contact information for three references. No letters of recommendation are required until the interview stage."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191051": {"title": "Assistant Professor - Economics", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Economics invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position starting in September 2027.\u00a0 Appointments will normally be at the rank of assistant professor. Complete applications including cover letter, CV, job market papers, and three letters of recommendation will be reviewed starting November 15, 2026. Primary emphasis is placed on qualifications, including Ph.D., outstanding academic record and teaching experience. Princeton is especially interested in candidates who, through their research, teaching and service, will contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Ph.D. in Economics"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192271": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Microeconomic Theory", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Princeton University Department of Economics The William S. Dietrich II Economic Theory Center of the Department of Economics will have an opening for a Postdoctoral Research Associate in September 2027. Primary criteria are the quality of the applicant's research and interest/experience in microeconomic theory. This position provides an outstanding opportunity for independent research as well as opportunities for collaboration with Princeton faculty and graduate students. Postdoctoral Research Associate may participate in the teaching program if mutually agreed, with sufficient course enrollments, and with the approval of the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. Postdoctoral Research Associate is expected to participate actively in the intellectual activities of Princeton University. Position at the postdoctoral rank are for one academic year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. Proof of Ph.D. completion is required. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Applicants must have (or expect to have by the start of the appointment) a PhD in Economics. Applicants must apply online and submit a cover letter, CV, research statement, two letters of recommendation, and one or at most two research papers. Applications will be reviewed beginning December 2026. For more information, please refer to: https://economics.princeton.edu/research-centers/the-william-s-dietrich-ii-economic-theory-center/research-programs/."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192281": {"title": "Visiting Research Scholar - Microeconomic Theory", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Princeton, NJ The William S. Dietrich II Economic Theory Center of the Department of Economics may have an opening for a Visiting Research Scholar, expected to start in September 2027. Primary criteria are the quality of the applicant's research and interest/experience in microeconomic theory. Visiting Research Scholars spend an academic year in residence at Princeton. They are given office space and the opportunity to pursue their own research on any topic in microeconomic theory. Visiting Research Scholars may participate in the University's teaching program if mutually agreed, with sufficient course enrollments, and with the approval of the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. Visiting Research Scholars must be employed at an institution other than Princeton during their visit. The amount that Princeton provides will depend on the level of support the Visiting Research Scholar will receive from his or her home institution during the visit. Applicants must have a doctorate or equivalent degree. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing. Applications will be reviewed beginning December 1, 2026. Applicants should include a c.v., a brief research statement, and contact information for two references. For more information, please refer to: https://economics.princeton.edu/research-centers/the-william-s-dietrich-ii-economic-theory-center/research-programs/ ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192302": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Industrial Organization", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Princeton University Department of Economics The Department of Economics will have an opening for one or more Postdoctoral Research Associates anticipated to start September 2027 for one academic year. Primary criteria are the quality of the applicant's research and interest/experience in Industrial Organization. These positions provide an outstanding opportunity for independent research as well as opportunities for collaboration with Princeton faculty and graduate students. Postdoctoral Research Associates may participate in the teaching program if mutually agreed, with sufficient course enrollments, and with the approval of the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. Postdoctoral Research Associates are expected to participate actively in the intellectual activities of Princeton University. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one academic year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. Proof of Ph.D. completion is required. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions are in-person on campus at Princeton University. Applicants must have (or expect to have by the start of the appointment) a PhD in Economics. Applicants must apply online and submit a cover letter, CV, research statement, three letters of recommendation, and one to two research papers. Applications will be reviewed beginning December 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192304": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Public Economics", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Princeton University Department of Economics The Department of Economics will have an opening for one or more Postdoctoral Research Associates anticipated to start September 2027. Primary criteria are the quality of the applicant's research and interest/experience in Public Economics. These positions provide an outstanding opportunity for independent research as well as opportunities for collaboration with Princeton faculty and graduate students. Postdoctoral Research Associates may participate in the teaching program if mutually agreed, with sufficient course enrollments, and with the approval of the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. Postdoctoral Research Associates are expected to participate actively in the intellectual activities of Princeton University. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one academic year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. Proof of Ph.D. completion is required. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions are in-person on campus at Princeton University. Applicants must have (or expect to have by the start of the appointment) a PhD in Economics. Applicants must apply online and submit a cover letter, CV, research statement, three letters of recommendation, and one to two research papers. Applications will be reviewed beginning December 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192306": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Econometrics", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Princeton University Department of Economics The Department of Economics will have an opening for one or more Postdoctoral Research Associates anticipated to start September 2027. Primary criteria are the quality of the applicant's research and interest/experience in Econometrics. These positions provide an outstanding opportunity for independent research as well as opportunities for collaboration with Princeton faculty and graduate students. Postdoctoral Research Associates may participate in the teaching program if mutually agreed, with sufficient course enrollments, and with the approval of the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. Postdoctoral Research Associates are expected to participate actively in the intellectual activities of Princeton University. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one academic year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. Proof of Ph.D. completion is required. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions are in-person on campus at Princeton University. Applicants must have (or expect to have by the start of the appointment) a PhD in Economics. Applicants must apply online and submit a cover letter, CV, research statement, three letters of recommendation, and one to two research papers. Applications will be reviewed beginning December 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192310": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Macroeconomics", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Princeton University Department of Economics The Louis A. Simpson Center for the Study of Macroeconomics of the Department of Economics will have an opening for one or more Postdoctoral Research Associates anticipated to start September 2027. Primary criteria are the quality of the applicant's research and interest/experience in macroeconomics. These positions provide an outstanding opportunity for independent research as well as opportunities for collaboration with Princeton faculty and graduate students. Postdoctoral Research Associates may participate in the teaching program if mutually agreed, with sufficient course enrollments, and with the approval of the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. Postdoctoral Research Associates are expected to participate actively in the intellectual activities of Princeton University. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one academic year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. Proof of Ph.D. completion is required. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions are in-person on campus at Princeton University. Applicants must have (or expect to have by the start of the appointment) a PhD in Economics. Applicants must apply online and submit a cover letter, CV, research statement, three letters of recommendation, and one to two research papers. Applications will be reviewed beginning December 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193024": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Princeton, NJ The Economics Department at Princeton University may have an occasional curricular need for lecturers to teach or advise in areas such as Corporate Finance, Asset Pricing, Industrial Organization, Applied Microeconomics, Macroeconomics, Econometrics, and Behavioral Economics, and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. Any appointments may be for one or two semesters during the 2027-2028 academic year. Appointment duty time would depend on student enrollments, and appointments may be renewable depending on departmental need. An advanced degree is required. Please provide a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a statement of teaching interest, and contact information for one reference. No letters of recommendation are required until the interview stage. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and the Department may not review all applications it receives. Review will occur on a rolling basis as needs arise, and will continue until any positions are filled. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/180765": {"title": "Lecturer in American Studies", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Effron Center for Study of America Location: Princeton, NJ Princeton University's Effron Center for the Study of America has an occasional curricular need for Lecturers specializing in American Studies, Asian American Studies, Latino Studies, and/or Native American and Indigenous Studies to teach undergraduate courses, or precepts (sections) of lecture classes, and accepts applications in advance if such lecturers are needed. Appointment duty time will depend upon departmental need and student enrollments, and any academic year 2026-27 positions are expected to begin September 1, 2026, or January 16, 2027, depending on the needs of the Center. Any potential for renewal will be subject to departmental need. Such positions may require leading discussions, attending course lectures, being available for meetings with the course instructor(s), holding regular on-campus office hours, and grading student exams and papers. Candidates working in the social sciences, environmental humanities, gender and sexuality studies, and comparative race and ethnicity are encouraged to apply. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing. An advanced degree is required. Please provide a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a statement of teaching interest, and the names and contact information for two references. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and the Center may not review all applications it receives. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192642": {"title": "Assistant, Associate or Full Professor  *Revised*", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Effron Center for Study of America Location: Princeton, NJ The Effron Center for the Study of America at Princeton University is undertaking a search for a tenure-track or tenured faculty member in any field in the Humanities and the Social Sciences, and across all geographic areas, as part of a major effort to advance Native American and Indigenous studies in teaching and research. This position will support the establishment of the new Program in Native American and Indigenous studies on campus, broadening and deepening curricular offerings and research opportunities. Appointments will be made in The Effron Center for the Study of America. The Effron Center is a collaborative hub of interdisciplinary scholarship housing the programs in American studies, Asian American studies, Latino studies, and Native American and Indigenous studies. The successful candidate should be prepared to work with students, faculty, and alumni to promote and sustain a vital, innovative, and intellectually expansive community for Native American and Indigenous Studies, and collaboratively engage with the center's other programs. A joint appointment with an appropriate discipline may be considered. Ph.D. required. Candidates must demonstrate research excellence and a strong publishing record, commensurate with their rank, and be committed to undergraduate teaching. Senior scholars should submit a letter of interest along with a curriculum vitae and a list of three references (to be contacted later in the search process). Junior scholars should submit a letter of interest along with a curriculum vitae, a list of three references, and one writing sample (article or dissertation/book chapter). --- Applications must be submitted online at https://apply.interfolio.com/192642 Candidates are encouraged to apply by November 1, 2026. Questions about this search may be directed to: effron-center@princeton.edu This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192761": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Native American and Indigenous Studies", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Effron Center for Study of America Location: Morrison Hall, Princeton, NJ The Effron Center for the Study of America at Princeton University seeks a postdoctoral research associate in Native American and Indigenous studies, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. Ph.D. required. Postdoctoral appointments are initally for one year with the renewal for a subsequent year(s) based on satisfactory performance, continued funding and meeting all other requirements. Contingent upon sufficient enrollments and approval from the Dean of the Faculty, the successsful candidate may teach one course per academic semester, including \"Introduction to Native American and Indigenous studies,\" and will participate in the intellectual community of the Effron Center. Candidates working in the social sciences, humanities, gender and sexuality studies, and comparative race and ethnicity are encouraged to apply. The work location is in person on campus, and we expect the associate to demonstrate to the Chair's satisfaction the ability to be on campus at least three days per week and on short notice, in order to fulfill all responsibilities. The fellow will enjoy full access to Princeton University libraries, a computer-equipped shared campus office during the appointment period, and access to various University facilities. -- Applicants must apply online by 11:59pm on November 15, 2026 and submit: Cover letter CV Research statement discussing the applicant's current and future scholarly projects, including a detailed overview of the dissertation and/or book project Writing sample (an article or a chapter) Two professional references Sample syllabus of an introductory course to Native American and Indigenous studies Additional materials, such as a teaching statement and additional writing samples, may be requested at a later stage. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Questions? Please email effron-center@princeton.edu or all 609-258-4710."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192794": {"title": "Anschutz Distinguished Fellow in American Studies", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Effron Center for Study of America Location: Princeton, NJ The Effron Center for the Study of America at Princeton University works to fulfill Princeton's institutional vision - in the nation's service and the service of humanity - by offering curricula, supporting research and hosting discussion on the evolving experiences and identities of the peoples of the territories known as America. By exploring and relating issues raised separately by the humanities, arts, and social and natural sciences, and by engaging with a wide range of scholarly methods and theories, we aim to create a generative space for new understandings of - and approaches to - issues that profoundly affect contemporary lives and will profoundly affect future generations. The appointment period will be for either Fall 2027 or Spring 2028 semester. The Anschutz Fellow(s) will teach one interdisciplinary, three credit-hour, undergraduate seminar course for the Program in American Studies with an enrollment cap of 15 students. In addition to teaching and holding weekly office hours to meet with enrolled students, the Fellow(s) will be expected to contribute to the intellectual life of the Effron Center for the Study of America, including participation in a wide range of events and activities on campus. The work location is in person on campus, and we expect the Fellow(s) to be in residence in Princeton or in the local vicinity, or demonstrate to the Chair's satisfaction the ability to be on campus at least two to three days per week and on short notice, in order to fulfill all responsibilities. The Fellow(s) will also deliver one public lecture to the University. The Fellow(s) will enjoy full access to Princeton University libraries, a computer-equipped shared campus office during the appointment period and access to various University facilities. Stipend and appointments will be determined on the basis of seniority and current institutional affiliations. The Anschutz Distinguished Fellowship, created throught an endowment by the Anschutz family, will be awarded in the academic year 2027-2028 to one or more distinguised writes, critics, journalists, musicians, artists, or other contributors to the arts, letters, public service, or commerce. Candidates need not be academic scholars. However, the selection committee will place great weight on indications of the candidate's teaching ability as well as the rigor, innovation, and interdisciplinary emphasis of the proposed seminar course. Please submit the following materials online no later than 11:59pm ET on November 15, 2026: a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, a writing sample, a preliminary syllabus, a short statement describing your proposed seminar and public lecture, and contact information for three references. A short list of finalists may be asked to submit further materials later. These positions may be subject to the University's background check policy. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H1-B visa requiring consular processing. For additional information, please email effron-center@princeton.edu or call 609-258-4710."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187517": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: History Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of History at Princeton University has an occasional curricular need for lecturers to teach undergraduate seminars or precepts (sections) in areas such as the history of North and South America, Asia, Europe (including Great Britain), Africa, and history of science, and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. Such positions would require teaching courses and/or leading discussions, attending course lectures, being available for meetings with the course instructor(s), holding regular on-campus office hours, and grading student exams and papers. Appointment duty time is subject to enrollments, and any available position may be for the Fall 2026 and/or Spring 2027 semesters, with the potential for renewal depending on departmental need. An advanced degree is required; Ph.D \u00a0preferred. Interested candidates should apply online with a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a brief statement of your teaching experience and interests, and the names and contact information for three references. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and the Department may not review all applications it receives. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188237": {"title": "Visiting Research Scholar", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: History Location: Princeton, New Jersey Property and Poverty. What is property - a cornerstone of personal freedom, a glue that keeps communities together, or a tool for oppression? Is it an institution rooted in the nature of society or an invention of the state? Does property require poverty as its corollary? The Shelby Cullom Davis Center for Historical Studies at Princeton University seeks applications from scholars researching the history of property and dispossession, wealth and poverty, and the practices of owning and disowning things, people, bodies, and ideas. The Davis Center invites applications from historians studying the intellectual, legal, religious, cultural, and economic histories of property and poverty, and from social historians who explore the world of the impoverished, the practices of poor relief, the causes of poverty, and the mechanisms of dispossession. All approaches are welcome, including but not limited: the history of intellectual property and copyright, the history of the commons, the lived experience of poverty, various forms of individual and collective ownership and resistance to them, the administrative and informational technologies enabling the development of property, and the religious and moral aspects of poverty and ownership. Intellectual, environmental, and economic historians, as well as historians of art, gender, race, sexuality from antiquity to the modern period, whose work engages with these subjects, are encouraged to apply. The Davis Center offers appointments for either one semester (usually September-January or January-June) or the academic year. Though the Center can normally offer funding for only a single semester, it welcomes the residence of year-long Fellows who combine Center support with funds from elsewhere. Applicants are encouraged to apply for external funds or sabbatical support and to apply for a year's Fellowship if they have a reasonable expectation of bringing additional funds with them. The Davis Center fellowships are residential. Fellows are required to live in Princeton or the local vicinity or demonstrate to the program's satisfaction the ability to be on campus on a daily basis in order to take an active part in the exchange of ideas with Fellows and others in the university community. The most important intellectual forum of the Center is the weekly Davis Seminar, which meets on Friday mornings during the fall and spring terms for lively and wide-ranging discussion of work by invited outside scholars and by the Fellows themselves. It is the core seminar of the History Department, attended not only by Fellows but also by faculty from the History and other departments at Princeton, graduate students, members of the Institute for Advanced Study at Princeton, faculty from nearby universities, and others. Fellows are expected to attend the weekly seminar and present a paper on their ongoing projects at one of its sessions. Fellows have the privileges of a member of the History Department. They are given offices in a cluster of offices assigned to the Davis Center. The Davis Center does not manage housing for its Fellows, but it assists Fellows in finding appropriate housing through the University Housing Office and private landlords if a Fellow wishes. Fellowships are normally awarded to employed scholars who are expected to return to their positions. Verification of employment will be requested prior to approval by the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. PhD required. The deadline for receipt of applications and letters of recommendation for fellowships for 2027/2028 is December 1, 2026, 11:59 p.m. EST. Applicants must submit a CV, cover letter, research proposal, abstract of proposal, and contact information for three references. Applicants should also indicate their preferred term of visit and their project title on the appropriate forms. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University.The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190698": {"title": "Assistant Professor, History of Britain and its Empire", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: History Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of History invites applications from scholars who specialize in the history of Britain and its empire. Historians who work on any topical area in the field are encouraged to apply. Review of files will begin on September 21, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should provide a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, a research statement outlining the dissertation or book as well as other relevant projects, and one chapter- or article-length writing sample. At least three referees should be entered into the online system, which will automatically generate requests for their letters. For candidates who do not yet have a Ph.D., the recommendation of the principal advisor must include precise information on the present status of the dissertation and the likelihood of completion by summer 2027. The anticipated start date is September 1, 2027. We seek faculty members who will create a climate that embraces excellence, with a strong commitment to teaching and mentoring that will enhance the work of the department and attract and retain a diverse student body. This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191831": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Politics Location: Princeton, NJ  The Department of Politics at Princeton University invites applications for a postdoctoral research associate position. The individual, who will work with Professor Nicholas Kuipers, and collaborate with Professor Alexander Sahn (University of North Carolina, Chapel Hill), will participate in a research project on historical transformations in local state capacity and public service delivery in the United States during the twentieth century. The postdoctoral research associate will oversee the construction of a longitudinal dataset based on newly digitized yearly municipal records. In particular, the postdoctoral researcher will oversee the project's data-processing pipeline, including the digitization, extraction, cleaning, validation, and standardization of information from historical sources. The project will make extensive use of large language models and other computational tools to extract structured information from historical documents. The postdoctoral research associate will also conduct statistical analyses using the resulting data and contribute to the preparation of research papers, reports, and other project materials. The successful candidate will have a PhD and should be able to work independently while collaborating closely with the principal investigators. The candidate should take initiative, be creative and well organized, attend carefully to data quality and reproducibility, and be able to manage multiple components of a large-scale research project while meeting deadlines in a timely fashion. Experience using large language models, either locally or through APIs, is highly desirable, particularly for document processing, information extraction, classification, or data cleaning. Experience working with OCR, historical or administrative records, large relational databases, record linkage, or other forms of computational data construction would also be valuable. Applicants must apply with a cover letter, CV, and contact information for two recommendations, one of which should be from the principal advisor of the doctorate program. The appointment is for one year with the possibility of an extension pending eligibility, satisfactory performance, and continued funding. This position is subject to the university's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. We will begin reviewing applications on September 15, 2026. The selected candidate is expected to begin on January 1, 2027. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192465": {"title": "James Madison Program Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Politics Location: Princeton, NJ The James Madison Program in American Ideals and Institutions at Princeton University is dedicated to the pursuit of scholarly excellence in the fields of constitutional law and political thought. Sponsored by Princeton's Department of Politics, the Program seeks to enrich the intellectual life of Princeton students and faculty by exploring American constitutional principles and structures through research and public discussion. The Program invites recent Ph.D. recipients,\u00a0or recipients who anticipate receiving their Ph.D. before the appointment start date , to apply for appointments as Postdoctoral Research Associates for the anticipated appointment period from September 1, 2027 to September 1, 2028. Appointments will be through the Department of Politics. All James Madison Program researchers are expected to be in residence in Princeton, or the local vicinity, to fulfill responsibilities relating to in-person participation, office hours, and the like. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. They may not be employed by another institution during the term of their Princeton appointment. They enjoy access to Firestone Library and a wide range of other facilities and activities throughout the University. Depending on the needs of the University, teaching opportunities may be available, subject to the approval of the Dean of the Faculty. Researchers appointed in the Madison Program are expected to devote full time to their proposed research. They are, however, encouraged to attend or participate in various activities of the Program, including faculty-student seminars, colloquia, and public lectures. The position is a fully funded, 12-month postdoctoral research associate appointment with benefits. All James Madison Program Postdoctoral Research Associate candidates must have less than two years of post-Ph.D. research experience prior to the anticipated start date. Applications are evaluated on the basis of (1) the significance of the proposed research; (2) the quality of the applicant's past research; and (3) the future contribution the applicant is likely to make to higher education. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate . This position is subject to the University's background check policy. We invite interested applicants to submit their materials through Interfolio. A complete application must include the following: a cover letter a curriculum vitae a scholarly paper written in the past three years a statement (no more than 1,500 words) describing the proposed research contact information for two references For priority consideration, application materials must be received by 11:59 p.m. EST on December 4, 2026 ; applications will continue to be reviewed until positions are filled. Awards will be announced in March 2027 or before. Please address any inquiries to: Thomas Bell, Ph.D. Assistant Director James Madison Program in American Ideals and Institutions Princeton University 83 Prospect Avenue Princeton, NJ 08540 E-mail: thomas.bell@princeton.edu Web address : jmp.princeton.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192471": {"title": "James Madison Program Visiting Fellow", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Politics Location: Princeton, NJ The James Madison Program in American Ideals and Institutions at Princeton University is dedicated to the pursuit of scholarly excellence in the fields of constitutional law and political thought. Sponsored by Princeton's Department of Politics, the Program seeks to enrich the intellectual life of Princeton students and faculty by exploring American constitutional principles and structures through research and public discussion. The Program invites college and university professors and other professionals with established records of scholarship to apply for appointments as Visiting Fellows for the academic year running from September 1, 2027 to June 1, 2028.\u00a0\u00a0One-semester appointments are possible but not preferred. Depending on the needs of the University, teaching opportunities may be available, subject to sufficient enrollment and approval of the Office of Dean of the Faculty. All James Madison Program Visiting Fellows will spend an academic year (9-month term) in residence at Princeton University, or the local vicinity, to fulfill responsibilities relating to in-person participation.\u00a0They enjoy access to Firestone Library and a wide range of other facilities and activities throughout the University. Visiting Fellows are expected to devote full time to their proposed research. They are encouraged, however, to attend or participate in various activities of the Program, including faculty-student seminars, colloquia, and public lectures. All James Madison Program Fellows must have a doctorate or equivalent degree. Positions are open to all regardless of citizenship. We invite interested applicants to submit their materials through Interfolio. A complete application must include the following: cover letter a curriculum vitae a scholarly paper written in the past three years a statement (no more than 1,500 words) describing the proposed research contact information for two references For priority consideration, application materials must be received by 11:59 p.m. EST on December 4, 2026 for appointments beginning in September 2027. Awards will be announced in March 2027 or before. Please address any inquiries to: Thomas Bell, Ph.D. Assistant Director James Madison Program in American Ideals and Institutions Princeton University 83 Prospect Avenue Princeton, NJ\u00a008540 E-mail:\u00a0thomas.bell@princeton.edu\u00a0/ Web address: jmp.princeton.edu"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191685": {"title": "**Revised** Fung Global Fellows Program AY2027-2028", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Princeton Institute for International and Regional Studies Location: Princeton, NJ Princeton University is pleased to announce the call for applications to the Fung Global Fellows Program at the Princeton Institute for International and Regional Studies (PIIRS). The program will select early-career scholars from around the world to be in residence at Princeton for an academic year (10 months) and to engage in research and discussion around a common theme. The fellowships will be awarded to those employed outside the United States who have a faculty appointment or a professional research appointment, and they will be expected to return to their positions at the conclusion of the fellowship. Lastly, they must have demonstrated outstanding scholarly achievement and unusual intellectual promise. Early-career scholars will be appointed at the rank of Visiting Research Scholar. Democratic governance faces mounting pressure around the world. The rise of populist movements, declining trust in institutions, polarization, disinformation, and democratic backsliding (including in democracies once considered as consolidated) have prompted renewed debate about whether democracy's ascendance in the late 20th century was more fragile than assumed. At the same time, democracies have proven resilient in unexpected ways\u2014through grassroots mobilization, institutional safeguards, judicial independence, and civic renewal\u2014raising the question of what, precisely, allows some democratic systems to weather crisis while others erode or collapse. During the 2027-28 academic year, the program theme will be \" Democracy in Crisis? \" We invite applications from scholars who study the challenges confronting democratic governance and/or the sources of democratic resilience. Projects might explore the drivers and dynamics of populism; the erosion of democratic norms and institutions; the role of media, technology, and disinformation in shaping political life; the relationship between economic inequality and democratic legitimacy; or the strategies through which citizens, civil society, and institutions have defended or renewed democratic practice. We are equally interested in work that interrogates the conceptual and normative foundations of democracy itself \u2013 what it means, who it serves, and how it has been imagined and contested across different political and cultural traditions. Applicants may address any region of the world and can bring contemporary and/or historical perspectives to bear. We welcome scholars from across the humanities and social sciences and hope to bring together a cohort whose comparative and interdisciplinary conversations will deepen our understanding of democracy's vulnerabilities and its capacity for renewal. Applicants must apply online. Applications are due on November 16, 2026 (11:59 p.m. EST). Should an offer be made, successful candidates who hold faculty appointments or professional research appointments will be required to submit an official letter from their current employer affirming that they would be permitted to accept the offer and to spend the academic year at Princeton University. The work location for these positions is in-person, on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships may be available, as appropriate. To be eligible, applicants must have received their Ph.D. (or equivalent) generally after September 1, 2017 . Candidates who are currently enrolled in a Ph.D. program or employed as postdocs are not eligible to apply. Fellowships will be awarded on the strength of a candidate's proposed research project, the relationship of that project to the program's theme, the candidate's scholarly record, and their ability to contribute to the intellectual life of the program. For more information, see: https://funggfp.princeton.edu/applications . The following items must be submitted by the applicant, in English, by November 16, 2026: - Cover letter (1.5 pages maximum) - Curriculum Vitae (including publications) - Research proposal (maximum of 3 pages, single spaced) - One writing sample (article or book chapter, maximum of 50 pages) - The names and email addresses for three referees, who will be contacted automatically by the online application system with an invitation to upload their letter of recommendation to the system by the application deadline. (*Please note: all letters of reference should be submitted by your referees before the November 16, 2026, deadline.*)"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193576": {"title": "**Revised** Program in African Studies Postdoctoral Research Associate AY 2027-2028", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Princeton Institute for International and Regional Studies Location: Princeton, NJ, United States The Program in African Studies (PAS) at the Princeton Institute for International and Regional Studies (PIIRS) invites applications for Postdoctoral Research Associate or more senior rank for the 2027-2028 academic year. \u00a0One appointment will be made to an exceptional recent PhD in the humanities, social sciences, interdisciplinary environmental science or engineering, with a focus on African thought, art, media, activism, conservation, economics, urban and rural communities, post-colonialism, and other research related to the African continent and its diaspora. Appointment will be made at the rank of Postdoctoral Research Associate or more senior rank. Postdoctoral Research Associate appointment is for one year (12 months) with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance, continued funding and meeting all other requirements ; those hired at a senior rank may have multi-year appointments. Postdoctoral Research Associate is expected to demonstrate to the program\u2019s satisfaction the ability to be on campus on a daily basis and on short notice in order to fulfill responsibilities relating to in-person participation.\u00a0 The position requires Postdoctoral Research Associate to be on campus at least four days per week. Responsibilities include teaching (one semester-long course per year) and active collaboration in research, discussions, and scholarly events within PAS and PIIRS. In addition, the successful candidate may have the opportunity to advise students in their area of expertise or related areas. \u00a0When teaching, the successful candidate will carry a secondary teaching rank. \u00a0Any teaching role is contingent on sufficient course enrollment and prior approval from the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. In addition to a competitive salary and benefits, the program will provide a research fund in the amount of $3,000 per year.\u00a0 The program will also provide shared office space and a laptop computer. Due to the anticipated volume of applications, only final candidates will be contacted. \u00a0Further information about the Program in African Studies can be found at: http://piirs.princeton.edu/afs . Questions about the application process for this position may be directed to Fiona Romaine, fromaine@princeton.edu . This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy.\u00a0 The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University.\u00a0 Position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applicants must demonstrate outstanding scholarly achievement and excellence in teaching. \u00a0To be eligible, applicants cannot have more than two years of postdoctoral experience prior to the start of the appointment, which is expected to begin on September 1, 2027. Applicants must apply online. Applications are due by November 20, 2026 (11:59 p.m. EST). The following application items are required and should be uploaded by the applicant: \u2022 Cover letter \u2022 Curriculum vitae \u2022 Dissertation abstract \u2022 Writing sample: one chapter of the dissertation or one published article related to the dissertation topic \u2022 Research proposal \u2022 Two course proposals \u2022 Details of prior courses taught and evaluation results if available \u2022 Names and email addresses for three references, who will be contacted automatically by the online application system with an invitation to upload their letter of recommendation by the application deadline.\u00a0 (**Please note: all letters of reference should be submitted by your referees before the November 20, 2026 deadline)."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194539": {"title": "**Revised** Center on Contemporary China Postdoctoral Research Associate AY2027-28", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Princeton Institute for International and Regional Studies Location: Princeton, NJ, United States The Paul and Marcia Wythes Center on Contemporary China (CCC) at The Princeton Institute for International and Regional Studies (PIIRS) invites applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate for the 2027-2028 academic year.\u00a0 One appointment will be made to an exceptional recent PhD who focuses on contemporary China from a social science disciplinary perspective.\u00a0 The appointment will be made at the rank of Postdoctoral Research Associate and will be for one year (12 months), renewable annually based on funding and meeting all other requirements.\u00a0 The position requires Postdoctoral Research Associates to be on campus at Princeton University at least four days per week in order to participate in all of the Center's activities, including student-faculty seminars, workshops, and public lectures. \u00a0The associate will have opportunities to collaborate with the Center's faculty. Questions about the application process for this position may be directed to Celine Li at celineli@princeton.edu . More information about the Center on Contemporary China may be found at https://ccc.princeton.edu . This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy.\u00a0 The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University.\u00a0 Position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. To be eligible, applicants should have no more than two years of post-PhD research experience prior to the start of the appointment, expected to be at the beginning of the academic year on September 1, 2027. Applicants should have completed a PhD or expect to have completed a PhD by the start of the appointment. Applicants should apply online.\u00a0 Applications are due by November 28, 2026 (11:59 p.m. EST). The following application items are required and should be uploaded by the applicant: \u00b7 Cover letter \u00b7 Curriculum Vitae \u00b7 A statement of research plans and their relation to CCC activities (1-3 pages) \u00b7 Two writing samples \u00b7 Names and email addresses for two references, who will be contacted automatically by the online application system with an invitation to upload their letter of recommendation by the application deadline.\u00a0 (**Please note: all letters of reference should be submitted by your referees before the November 28, 2026 deadline)"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194680": {"title": "Translator in Residence-Program in Translation & Intercultural Communication", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Princeton Institute for International and Regional Studies Location: Princeton, NJ, United States The Program in Translation and Intercultural Communication (PTIC) at the Princeton Institute for International and Regional Studies (PIIRS) and the Council of the Humanities invites applications for an appointment as a Translator in Residence for one academic semester, either fall or spring, during academic years 2027-28 and 2028-29.\u00a0 Interviews for both years may take place in January 2027.\u00a0 The primary rank will be Professional Specialist. The successful candidate for each semester will be an established translator into English with projects currently in progress, and will be expected to contribute to PIIRS, PTIC, the Council, and/or the Program in Creative Writing by mentoring students and participating in relevant aspects of these Programs,\u00a0 In consultation with the Director of PTIC, they may be asked to visit relevant classes and/or to run workshops in the areas of the theory and practice of translation in collaboration with faculty teaching the Program\u2019s core courses.\u00a0 The Translator in Residence must hold weekly office hours. In addition to salary and benefits, PTIC will provide up to $2,500 in reimbursement for expenses associated with relocating to Princeton, NJ.\u00a0 The program will also provide shared office space and a laptop computer. Due to the anticipated volume of applications, only candidates invited to interview will be contacted.\u00a0 Further information about the Program in Translation & Intercultural Communication may be found at https://ptic.princeton.edu . Questions about the application process for this position may be director to Yolanda Sullivan, syolanda@princeton.edu . This position is subject to the University's background check policy.\u00a0 The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University.\u00a0 These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships may be available, as appropriate. Applicants must apply online. Applications are due by December 1, 2026 (11:59 p.m. EST) .\u00a0 The following application items are required and should be uploaded by the applicant: \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Cover letter outlining research agenda and current projects \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Curriculum vitae \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Names and email addresses for two references who will be contacted automatically by the online application system.\u00a0 (Please note: all letters of reference should be submitted by your referees before the December 1, 2026 deadline.)"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181761": {"title": "Lecturer in Public and International Affairs", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: School of Public and International Affairs Location: Princeton, NJ The Princeton School of Public and International Affairs at Princeton University may have occasional curricular need for lecturers to teach or co-teach in areas such as International Relations, International Development, Domestic Policy, Global Health, Psychology and Public Policy and Economics and Public Policy, and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. Any appointments are subject to enrollments and may be for one or two semesters during the 2026-2027 academic year. An advanced degree or comparable experience required. Please provide a curriculum vitae (required); cover letter and teaching statement (optional); and contact information for one reference. No letters of recommendation will be required until the interview stage. Applications will be reviewed based on curricular need, and the School may not review all applications it receives. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. Applications will be reviewed starting on March 19, 2026, and will continue until any available positions are filled. Please submit a CV (required), and cover letter and teaching statement (optional). No letters of recommendation are required at this time. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188757": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate/Initiatives on Contemporary European Affairs (ICEA)", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: School of Public and International Affairs Location: Princeton, NJ Princeton University's Initiatives on Contemporary European Affairs (ICEA) through the Princeton School of Public and International Affairs and the Department of Politics, host postdoctoral associate positions for twelve-month appointments expected to start on September 1, 2027. ICEA welcomes applications from social scientists who specialize in comparative politics and whose research includes a focus on European politics. Postdoctoral associates will pursue research and contribute to the intellectual life of ICEA, the Department of Politics, and the Princeton School of Public and International Affairs. Postdoctoral associates are expected to fully participate in the daily intellectual life of the university. Successful candidates will receive a competitive salary and applicable Princeton University benefits in accordance with university guidelines, a taxable moving allowance, and a research allowance. PhD is required. Applicants for this 2027-2028 postdoctoral research associate position must apply online and submit a cover letter, CV, a 3\u20135-page (double-spaced) research statement, a writing sample, and the names of two (and not more than two) references. The deadline for receipt of complete applications is October 26, 2026, at 11:59 p.m. EST. Referees will be contacted directly by email with instructions for submitting a confidential recommendation on your behalf. Letters of reference are to be submitted by November 2, 2026, 11:59 p.m. EST. Please complete your application well before the deadline so that references letters can be submitted in a timely manner. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188761": {"title": "Visiting Research Scholar/Initiatives on Contemporary European Affairs (ICEA)", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: School of Public and International Affairs Location: Princeton, NJ Princeton University's Initiatives on Contemporary European Affairs (ICEA) hosts a Visiting Research Scholar Program for faculty members applying to spend their 2027-2028 academic leave year with ICEA. ICEA welcomes applications from social scientists who specialize in comparative politics and whose research includes a focus on European politics. ICEA visiting scholar positions are restricted to employed scholars on leave who are expected to return to their positions. Visiting scholars will pursue research and contribute to the intellectual life of ICEA, the Department of Politics, and the Princeton School of Public and International Affairs. To fully participate in the daily intellectual life of the university, they will be required to be in residence in Princeton or the local vicinity, or to demonstrate to the program's satisfaction the ability to be on campus on a daily basis and on short notice. Visiting scholars will normally receive a stipend of one-half of their academic year salary. This will be calculated based on the amount of support that each individual expects to receive from their home institution but will normally not exceed $100,000. Selected candidates will be asked to indicate, or provide written authorization for Princeton to confirm, what percentage of financial support their home institution and other sources are likely to provide during their visit, if any, and to submit a confidential statement indicating salary for the current academic year (September 1, 2027 - June 1, 2028) or provide written authorization for Princeton to confirm their salary; they will also be asked to provide a statement of their home institution's leave policy. Successful candidates will receive applicable benefits, travel to and from Princeton, and a research allowance. Verification of employment will be requested prior to approval by the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. PhD or equivalent doctoral degree required. Applicants must apply online and submit a cover letter, CV, a 3\u20135-page (double-spaced) statement of research interest/research proposal, a writing sample, and the name and contact information for one reference. The desired start date is July through August 2027, though some flexibility is possible. The deadline for receipt of complete applications is October 23, 2026, at 11:59 p.m. EST. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189843": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Julis-Rabinowitz Center for Public Policy & Finance", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: School of Public and International Affairs Location: Princeton, NJ The Julis-Rabinowitz Center for Public Policy & Finance (JRCPPF) at the Princeton School of Public and International Affairs seeks candidates for a postdoctoral research associate position with an anticipated start date of September 1, 2027. The primary criteria are the quality of the applicant's research on the linkages between finance and macroeconomics broadly. The candidate's work may use empirical or theoretical methods to address important policy questions. The position offers an outstanding opportunity for independent research, as well as opportunities for collaboration with Princeton faculty, graduate students, and undergraduate students. There are no teaching obligations. Applicants must have received their Ph.D. Postdoctoral Researchers are expected to participate actively in Princeton University's intellectual activities. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year, with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. Applicants must apply online and submit a cover letter, C.V., research statement, job market paper, and three references. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. This position is in-person and on-campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. The Princeton School of Public and International Affairs is especially interested in candidates who, through their research, teaching, and service, will contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community. We actively seek colleagues of exceptional ability who represent a broad range of viewpoints, experience, and value systems and who share Princeton University\u2019s dedication to excellence and service."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190354": {"title": "Associate/Full Professor in IR/Security Studies", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: School of Public and International Affairs Location: Princeton, NJ The School of Public and International Affairs at Princeton University invites applicants for a tenured position (associate or full professor) in Security Studies. The ideal candidate will have demonstrated scholarly excellence in security studies, broadly de\ufb01ned. Interested parties should apply online. The successful candidate will be appointed in the School with a joint appointment with Politics. Princeton is especially interested in candidates who, through their research, teaching and service, will contribute to the diversity of the curriculum, broadly de\ufb01ned. This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. The Princeton School of Public and International Affairs seeks faculty members who will create a climate that embraces excellence, with a strong commitment to teaching and mentoring that will enhance the work of the department and that will attract and retain a diverse student body. Interested candidates should submit a letter of interest, a CV, 2 \u2013 3 page statement summarizing research accomplishments and future plans. Please provide contact information for three references. No letters of recommendation are required until the interview stage. Screening will begin September 2026 and continue until the position is \ufb01lled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192828": {"title": "****REVISED**** Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Sociology Location: Princeton University For full consideration, applicants should submit all required materials by October 15, 2026. The Department of Sociology at Princeton University seeks applicants for multiple postdoctoral research associate positions or a more senior rank in the Eviction Lab at Princeton University. Successful candidates will have a Ph.D. and a background in statistics, quantitative social science, data science, economics, and/or computer science. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions as the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. The Eviction Lab at Princeton University is an interdisciplinary team who has built the first-ever national database of evictions in America. We have validated, mapped, and published our data through an interactive website (evictionlab.org). The Eviction Lab is currently working on a number of studies on the prevalence, causes, and consequences of housing displacement. Postdoctoral researchers will have access to novel and very large datasets not publicly released. The Eviction Lab is engaged in multiple long-term initiatives, including a Joint Statistical Project with the U.S. Census, which has allowed linking over 100 million eviction records with administrative databases and restricted-use files of the American Housing Survey. We seek self-driven, creative thinkers with strong quantitative skills to work with a team of researchers to analyze large administrative data sets and coauthor papers reporting findings on housing instability, urban inequality, housing markets, and public policy. Successful candidates will be comfortable with management of large datasets and advanced statistical modeling or computer programming skills. Preference will be given to candidates who have demonstrated experience with causal methods for observational data, spatial modeling, or other advanced quantitative methodologies. Previous experience working in a research team or lab is a plus. A working knowledge of the literature on housing instability and urban sociology is not required but useful; an intellectual interest in applying rigorous analysis techniques data to real-world problems is essential. Salary is competitive and is benefits eligible. Initial review of applications will begin on October 15th.\u00a0 The position will remain open until filled. Materials submitted by regular mail or email will not be accepted. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. This position is subject to Princeton University's background check policy. Required qualifications: PhD Background in statistics, quantitative social science, data science, economics, and/or computer science Ability to manage large datasets and advanced statistical modeling or computer programming skills Intellectual interest in applying rigorous analysis techniques data to real-world problems Preferred qualifications: Demonstrated experience with casual methods for observational data, spatial modeling or other advanced quantitative methodologies Previous experience working in a research team or lab Working knowledge of the literature on housing instability and urban sociology Applicants should submit: A complete vitae A cover letter of interest Names and contact information of three persons who can serve as references A writing sample that includes quantitative analysis For full consideration, applicants should submit all required materials by October 15, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189838": {"title": "Researcher in Space Physics - REVISED", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Astrophysical Sciences Location: 171 Broadmead, Princeton Postdoctoral Research Associate and/or Senior Research Positions in Space Physics at Princeton University The Space Physics Group in the Department of Astrophysical Sciences at Princeton University anticipates offering one or more postdoctoral or senior research positions in experimental and observational space physics. The Space Physics Group conducts research in many aspects of space physics (aka heliophysics), with a strong emphasis on experimental and observational space plasma physics. Among others, the Group currently leads NASA's Interstellar Boundary Explorer (IBEX) mission, the Parker Solar Probe (PSP) Integrated Science Investigation of the Sun (ISOIS) energetic particle instrument suite, and the Interstellar Mapping and Acceleration Probe (IMAP) mission, which launched 24 September 2025 to explore the details of particle acceleration and the Sun's interaction with the local interstellar medium. See https://spacephysics.princeton.edu/ for more information about the Space Physics Group at Princeton University. The successful candidate(s) will play a significant role in one or more of the following areas: 1) analysis and publication of Energetic Neutral Atom (ENA) data and other observations from IMAP, 2) analysis and publication of energetic particle observations from ISOIS, 3) analysis and publication of observations from other IMAP instruments and topics (particularly regarding solar wind and pickup ion data), and 4) other funded space physics research within the Group. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Preferred qualifications include prior experience in analyzing ENA, solar wind, pickup ion, and/or charged particle data, as well as a proven ability to lead or contribute to the rapid development and publication of numerous outstanding research articles. Experience in developing space instruments is also advantageous. A Ph.D. in Space Physics or a related field is required. The term of appointment depends on rank. Postdoctoral positions are for one year, with the possibility of renewal based on satisfactory performance and continued funding. Those hired at more senior ranks may receive multi-year appointments. Interested individuals must apply online. Submit a curriculum vitae, bibliography, cover letter, a brief statement of research interests, and contact information for three references. Applications will continue to be accepted until the positions are filled. For further inquiries, please contact spacephysics@princeton.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189969": {"title": "Visiting Fellows, Space Physics at Princeton University", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Astrophysical Sciences Location: Princeton, NJ The Space Physics at Princeton Group in the Department of Astrophysical Sciences at Princeton University (https://spacephysics.princeton.edu/) is excited to offer Visiting Fellow positions. The program supports faculty and equivalent senior researchers in conducting intensive research in residence at Princeton for a duration ranging from one month to a full year. Visiting Fellows will collaborate closely with members of the Space Physics at Princeton Group on one or both of two key areas: 1) Observations of Energetic Particles from the Parker Solar Probe, 2) Observations of Energetic Neutral Atoms (ENAs), solar wind, pickup ions, Interstellar Neutral Atoms, and/or any other observations from the \u00a0Interstellar Mapping and Acceleration Probe (IMAP). The environment is scientifically intense, highly collaborative, and extremely rewarding. The program aims to strengthen the Group by introducing new and diverse perspectives while conducting world-class research and publishing numerous papers in collaboration with the Group. Visiting Fellow positions are suitable for faculty members on sabbatical and other comparable senior researchers on leave from their primary appointments. In accordance with University policy, employment and salary verification and visitor participation agreements are required of all appointees. For Fellows coming with limited financial support from their regular employer, financial support (including stipend and/or housing) may be offered. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Suitable for faculty members on sabbatical and other comparable senior researchers on leave from their primary appointments 1) a brief cover letter stating the specific reasons you would like to visit the Space Physics at Princeton Group and the dates you are available to visit, and 2) a CV."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190943": {"title": "Professor in Plasma Physics", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Astrophysical Sciences Location: Princeton, NJ Princeton University is searching for a faculty member at the level of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor in the Program in Plasma Physics of its Department of Astrophysical Sciences, to begin in fall 2027. While candidates working in all areas of plasma physics are welcome to apply, we are especially interested in candidates whose experimental research programs focus on magnetic confinement fusion (MCF) and who would benefit from the operation of the nation\u2019s largest spherical tokamak, NSTX-U, and future MCF devices at the Princeton Plasma Physics Laboratory (PPPL). The primary selection criteria will be accomplishment and promise in research, as well as the ability and interest to teach a range of plasma physics courses and to supervise undergraduate and graduate student research projects. Information on departmental resources and research programs can be found at https://plasma.princeton.edu , https://www.pppl.gov , and https://web.astro.princeton.edu . This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. All applicants must have a Ph.D. and a record of research productivity. They must also be committed to teaching at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. Applications require: a cover letter, complete curriculum vitae including publications, and separate 3-4 page descriptions of research and teaching interests. Letters of recommendation (3) will be managed through Interfolio. For further inquiries, contact dlewis@princeton.edu . Applicants should apply via https://apply.interfolio.com/190943 . Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193062": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research in Astrophysical Sciences", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Astrophysical Sciences Location: Princeton, NJ PLEASE NOTE: APPLICATIONS ARE DUE NOVEMBER 1, 2026 FOR INITIAL CONSIDERATION. Applications will continue to be accepted until all positions are filled. The Department of Astrophysical Sciences, Princeton University, anticipates offering a number of postdoctoral or more senior research positions in theory, observation, and instrumentation, including (but not limited to): the Lyman Spitzer, Jr. Postdoctoral Fellowship; the Henry Norris Russell Postdoctoral Fellowship; The Vera C. Rubin Observatory Data Management Team; and positions in research related to exoplanet theory, interstellar medium/star formation, plasma astrophysics, and supernovae. Applicants may work with the Department\u2019s distinguished faculty and\u00a0research staff.\u00a0For a full list of department members and activities, see https://web.astro.princeton.edu/ . There are also strong research groups with interests in astrophysics at the Princeton Plasma Physics Lab and in the Physics, Geosciences, and Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Departments, and at the nearby Institute for Advanced Study. For details on specific positions, see https://web.astro.princeton.edu/job-opportunities-department . The work location for these positions are in-person on campus at Princeton University. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the expectation of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. The expected starting date is September 1, 2027, although this is flexible. A PhD in Astronomy or a related field is required. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. Interested persons should submit a curriculum vitae, a publication list, and a research statement. A cover letter is optional. Letters of recommendation (3) will be managed through Interfolio. All applications including references received by November 1, 2026 will be fully considered, but applications will continue to be accepted until all available positions are filled. All applications will be considered for all postdoctoral positions available in the department, including department fellowships and grant supported positions, but you will be asked in the application which positions you are interested in. For further inquiries, contact astropd@princeton.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193776": {"title": "Future Faculty in the Physical Sciences Postdoctoral Fellowship", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Astrophysical Sciences Location: Princeton, NJ PLEASE NOTE: APPLICATIONS ARE DUE NOVEMBER 1, 2026 FOR INITIAL CONSIDERATION. Applications will continue to be accepted until all positions are filled. Princeton University invites applications for the Future Faculty in the Physical Sciences (FFPS) Fellowship . We seek to attract a diverse cohort of early career scientists who will conduct research in physics, broadly defined, including astrophysics, biophysics, geophysics, and planetary physics, independently or in collaboration with Princeton faculty, postdoctoral researchers and students. The Fellowship fosters research excellence at Princeton by bringing together scholars with unique backgrounds and insights in physics and related fields. Candidates who have demonstrated a strong commitment to broadening participation in physics, or the physical sciences more generally, through teaching, advising, mentoring, advocacy, or public outreach are especially encouraged to apply. FFPS Fellows will have a primary home in the Department of Physics, of Astrophysical Sciences, or of Geosciences, and will be part of an actively mentored research community across institutional affiliations, including the Princeton Center for Theoretical Science, the Center for the Physics of Biological Function, the Leinweber Forum for Theoretical Physics, and other Centers and Programs. The Fellowship includes an annual research fund. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University.\u00a0 Appointments will be made at the postdoctoral or the associate research scholar rank. Postdoctoral appointments will be for one year, renewable annually based on funding and meeting all other requirements, for a total of up to three years; associate research scholar appointments will be for three years. The expected starting date is September 1, 2027, although this is flexible. A PhD in the physical sciences or a related field is required. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Interested persons should submit a curriculum vitae, a bibliography, and a statement of research interests and plans by November 1, 2026. A personal statement on mentoring, outreach/service, or teaching philosophy that works to broaden opportunities in the field should be included as a separate section of the research statement. A cover letter is optional. Letters of recommendation (3) will be managed through Interfolio. Applicants should apply online. All applications including reference letters received by November 1, 2026 will be fully considered, but applications will continue to be accepted until the position(s) are filled. For further inquiries, contact ffpsadmin@princeton.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181429": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Revised)", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Atmospheric & Oceanic Sciences Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Atmospheric and Oceanic Sciences Program at Princeton University, in association with NOAA's Geophysical Fluid Dynamics Laboratory (GFDL), seeks two postdoctoral or more senior research scientist for Earth System research and development activities focused on data science and engineering applied to stakeholder-relevant issues. The scientists will collaborate with Princeton and NOAA/GFDL researchers to expand the reach of GFDL\u2019s model data (both historical and future projections) to communities in need of improved decision-making guidance. The successful candidates will have the opportunity to develop actionable weather and Earth System data and knowledge that will be used directly by applied researchers and NOAA stakeholders interested in adaptation to natural hazards and extreme weather potentially spanning sub-seasonal to multidecadal timescales. The selected candidates will join a vigorous research group at Princeton University, working in close collaboration with federal scientists at GFDL with diverse expertise in areas such as hydrology, air quality, boundary layer turbulence, or empirical statistical downscaling.\u00a0 The individuals will have access to state-of-the-art numerical models and high-performance computing systems at Princeton and NOAA, working alongside GFDL researchers to advance the evaluation, translation and transfer of the state-of-the-art dynamical models results. This effort will lead to the creation and distribution of data products of value to key NOAA stakeholders involved in planning and decision-making. Candidates must have a PhD in atmospheric physics, hydrology, meteorology, Earth system science, climate studies, applied mathematics, or related fields. Candidates should demonstrate a strong background in Earth system science, as well as experience using and analyzing numerical model output and/or large observational datasets. Successful candidates are expected to be skillful in some, but not necessarily all, of the following areas: experience with software languages and tools such as Python, R, Linux, GitHub, Globus, high-performance computing and/or cloud computing; familiarity with Earth system models through model development, model execution, and/or model performance diagnoses; applied mathematics methods such as machine learning techniques, bias adjustment, and downscaling of meteorological fields; strong data analysis skills; and strong communication skills. The initial appointment is for one year with the possibility of renewal subject to satisfactory performance and available funding. The Term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. Princeton is interested in candidates who, through their research, will contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Complete applications should include a cover letter, CV, publication and presentation list, 3 letters of recommendation, and a one-to-two page personal statement and/or a research statement. Review of applications will begin March 31, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. For additional information, contact Aparna Radhakrishnan ( aparna.radhakrishnan@princeton.edu )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187243": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Atmospheric & Oceanic Sciences Location: Princeton, New Jersey Research in Climate Model Emulation in the Program in Atmospheric and Oceanic Sciences (AOS) at Princeton University The AOS Program and the Cooperative Institute for Modeling the Earth System seek an energetic and enthusiastic Postdoctoral Research Associate to participate in global atmosphere and coupled atmosphere-sea ice-ocean machine learning modeling research. This effort will elucidate how internal variability in recently developed machine learning models (i.e., climate model emulators) compares to physical models on decadal and longer timescales. The individual will join a vigorous research team with Timothy Merlis (Princeton University), Mitch Bushuk (Geophysical Fluid Dynamics Laboratory, GFDL), and Alistair Adcroft (Princeton University) with others at Princeton University and GFDL and external collaborators. Available resources include world-class high-performance computing platform and an exceptional intellectual community of atmospheric and oceanic science researchers. Candidates must have received a Ph.D. in the climate sciences (atmospheric science, oceanography), applied math, or related physical science disciplines. Rigorous training in atmospheric and oceanic sciences is preferred, along with a strong modeling background and/or observational experience (e.g., large datasets such as satellite data). Candidates with familiarity with machine learning and strong skills in statistical analysis are encouraged to apply. Positions at the rank of Postdoctoral Research Associate are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. Applicants are asked to submit a cover letter, CV, a publication list, a statement of research experience and interests, and names of at least 3 letters of recommendation.\u00a0Review of applications will begin as soon as they are received and continue until the position is filled. This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188956": {"title": "*Revised* Postdoctoral Research Associate - Atmospheric Influence on Caribbean Marine Heatwaves: Scientific Research and Educational Outreach", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Atmospheric & Oceanic Sciences Location: Princeton, New Jersey In this unique opportunity combining research and outreach, Princeton University\u2019s Atmospheric and Oceanic (AOS) Sciences Program, in cooperation with NOAA\u2019s Geophysical Fluid Dynamics Laboratory (GFDL) and Cooperative Science Center for Earth Systems Sciences and Remote Sensing Technologies II (CESSRST II), seeks applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate to: conduct research investigating the atmospheric drivers of marine heatwaves (MHWs) in the Caribbean using observations and climate model output, and to develop and teach an educational outreach curriculum to train adolescents from underserved communities in New Jersey and Puerto Rico in climate science and climate data analysis. Marine Heatwaves (MHWs) are periods of extreme, warm sea surface temperatures that can be devastating to marine ecosystems. Yet despite the danger MHWs pose, the physical drivers of these events --both in current and future climates-- are not well understood. Furthermore, it is important that communities most affected by MHWs are educated and engaged on the topic. This opportunity seeks applicants with a strong background in atmospheric science and/or physical oceanography research, in addition to education and outreach. A Ph.D. in oceanography, physical geography, mathematics/statistics, environmental, climate, atmospheric, and/or physical sciences is required. Candidates should be skilled in using computer programming to analyze climate data and be familiar with Caribbean/tropical climate from an atmosphere and/or ocean perspective. Candidates should also have very strong communication skills and be experienced in teaching science and/or coding to high school and/or undergraduate students. Other beneficial experiences include prior work with climate observations, global and/or regional climate model output, ocean mixed-layer heat budgets and/or water mass transformation, and interdisciplinary experience bridging climate science with education and outreach particularly in underserved communities. Positions at the rank of Postdoctoral Research Associate rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding.\u00a0 Princeton is interested in candidates who, through their research, will contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community.\u00a0 This position is subject to the University's background check policy.\u00a0 This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. The successful candidate\u2019s project will be primarily coordinated with members of GFDL\u2019s Atmospheric Physics, Ocean and Cryosphere, and Marine Ecosystems and Downscaling divisions. For additional information, contact David Paynter (dpaynter@princeton.edu). Complete applications include a 2-page cover letter outlining the candidate's relevant research and educational/outreach experience, CV, publication list, and 3 letters of recommendation. Application deadline is August 20, 2026, 11:59 pm EST. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190006": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Ocean ecosystem modeling and prediction for Southeast U.S. coasts", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Atmospheric & Oceanic Sciences Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Atmospheric and Oceanic Sciences Program at Princeton University in cooperation with NOAA\u2019s Geophysical Fluid Dynamics Laboratory (GFDL) seeks a postdoctoral research associate to develop, analyze, and apply high-resolution ocean-ecosystem hindcasts and predictions with a focus on the Gulf of America, Caribbean, and Southeast coast of the United States. The candidate will use existing numerical model simulations and, as required, generate new predictions to address a range of ocean prediction challenges including, but not limited to, numerical model development and comparison with observations to improve prediction skill; prediction of marine heatwaves, acidification, hypoxia, and compound extreme events on seasonal time scales; and application of forecasts to living marine resource challenges. The candidate will work with Dr. Andrew Ross (andrew.c.ross@noaa.gov) at GFDL, collaborate with an interdisciplinary project team as part of NOAA\u2019s National Ecosystem Assessment Program, and engage with climate and regional ocean model development and application efforts across Princeton, GFDL, and partners. Candidates must have received a Ph.D. in the earth sciences, applied math, or the physical, biological, or chemical sciences. Candidates with experience using numerical ocean-biogeochemical models and interdisciplinary knowledge of climate dynamics, ocean circulation, and marine ecosystems are particularly encouraged to apply. Positions at the rank of Postdoctoral Research Associate rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding.\u00a0 Princeton is interested in candidates who, through their research, will contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community.\u00a0 This position is subject to the University's background check policy.\u00a0 This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applicants are asked to submit a cover letter, CV, a publication list, a research statement and names of at least 3 letters of recommendation.\u00a0 Applicants should apply online .\u00a0 Application deadline is August 31, 2026, 11:59 pm EST. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/184402": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Chemistry Location: Princeton, New Jersey  Postdoctoral and more senior research positions are available in biological, inorganic, materials, organic, physical, theoretical, and computational chemistry. \u00a0The Term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Applicants must submit a cover letter, a CV with publication list, and the names and contact information of 3 references. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing. Other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Education Required: Doctorate Degree"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/179810": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Ecology & Evolutionary Biology Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology has postdoctoral associates and more senior research positions available in the areas of ecology, behavior, sensory ecology, conservation, evolutionary biology, infectious disease, and population biology. Candidates are required to have a Ph.D. or equivalent in biology, ecology or related fields. Excellent writing ability in English is essential as well as creativity, energy, and the desire to work in an interactive and inter-disciplinary environment. The Term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. Research staff are entitled to University benefits. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Applicants must apply online and submit a curriculum vitae, a one-page statement of research experience and interests, and a cover letter that includes names and contact information of three references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183442": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Ecology & Evolutionary Biology Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology has postdoctoral associates and more senior research positions available in the areas of ecology, behavior, sensory ecology, conservation, evolutionary biology, infectious disease, and population biology. Candidates are required to have a Ph.D. or equivalent in biology, ecology or related fields. Excellent writing ability in English is essential as well as creativity, energy, and the desire to work in an interactive and inter-disciplinary environment. The Term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. Research staff are entitled to University benefits. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Note: This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applicants must apply online and submit a curriculum vitae, a one-page statement of research experience and interests, and a cover letter that includes names and contact information of three references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193474": {"title": "Assistant Professor", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Ecology & Evolutionary Biology Location: Princeton, NJ Princeton University's Department of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology is seeking candidates for an Assistant Professor position in Ecology, Evolution and/or Behavior. We seek a scholar who is eager to interact with a vibrant and interdisciplinary group of colleagues both inside and outside the department. The successful candidate will address bold questions through a rigorous, creative, and integrative research program. The range of potential research areas should be understood broadly. We are excited to consider applicants focused on any timescale or level of biological hierarchy and employing any methodological approach. Princeton University is an interactive community that fosters cross-disciplinary thought and collaboration. The successful candidate will have opportunities to interact with colleagues across different departments, programs, and institutes including the Lewis-Sigler Institute for Integrative Genomics, the Princeton Neuroscience Institute, the Program in Applied and Computational Mathematics, the Data and Intelligent Systems Hub, the High Meadows Environmental Institute, t he School of Public and International Affairs, and the Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute. For those applying ecological and evolutionary principles outside its traditional domain, a joint appointment with another academic unit is possible. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in a relevant field. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Applicants should submit a vision statement, no longer than two pages, that outlines the bold question(s) they would pursue within their first 5 years and that places those questions broadly in the context of major unsolved problems in their field(s). In this respect, the vision statement should draw on, yet go beyond, a summary of the applicant's prior and current research. In addition to the vision statement, applications need to include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, three papers or preprints, and three letters of recommendation. Application review will begin on November 10, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194526": {"title": "Professional Specialist", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Ecology & Evolutionary Biology Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology at Princeton University has an opening for a research professional in evolutionary biology and microbiome science appointed at the rank of Professional Specialist. The position provides scientific leadership for computational genomics and metagenomics within the Moeller Laboratory. Working under the general direction of the Principal Investigator, the position leads computational research projects from conceptual development through analysis, interpretation, and dissemination; develops and implements analytical and statistical methodologies; coordinates research activities involving students, postdoctoral researchers, and collaborators; and exercises substantial independent scientific judgment in determining approaches and resolving complex research problems. The position is responsible for translating biological questions into rigorous computational research strategies and for communicating research findings. Duties will include but are not limited to: (1) development and implementation of computational workflows for genome-resolved metagenomic, comparative-genomic, and population-genomic analyses, including strain-, gene-, and allele-level analyses of microbial communities; (2) management, quality control, organization, and long-term stewardship of large-scale DNA, RNA, and other omics datasets and associated computational workflows; (3) interpretation and communication of results, including leadership of and contributions to grant proposals and manuscripts (e.g., research articles); and (4) coordination of research activities within the laboratory, including development and documentation of computational protocols and training and technical guidance for laboratory members. The position is available for one year, with a possibility for renewal contingent on meeting all the requirements. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing. Qualifications include a PhD in biology, microbiology, bioinformatics, computational biology, evolutionary biology, genomics, or a related field, or an advanced degree with equivalent combination of advanced training and relevant research experience. Candidates should have demonstrated expertise in microbiome or genomic research, quantitative analysis of large sequencing datasets, and the development or implementation of reproducible computational workflows. Experience with experimental microbiology, mammalian microbiome studies, and management of large-scale research datasets is preferred. Specific desired qualifications include: 1.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Advanced expertise in computational genomics, metagenomics, population genetics, bioinformatics, or related quantitative approaches. 2.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Demonstrated ability to lead complex scientific research projects from conceptual development through analysis, interpretation, and dissemination. 3.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Demonstrated ability to develop or substantially adapt computational and statistical methodologies for novel research problems. 4.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Advanced proficiency in scientific programming and reproducible computational research, including languages such as Python, R, Bash, and workflow-management systems. 5.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Demonstrated ability to independently evaluate analytical alternatives and make scientific and methodological decisions affecting project direction. 6.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Strong record of scientific writing and communication, including preparation of manuscripts, figures, research presentations, and components of grant proposals. 7.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Ability to coordinate the work of multiple research contributors and provide scientific guidance and mentorship to students and researchers. 8.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Excellent project-management, organizational, communication, and problem-solving skills. Applicants should include a cover letter, a curriculum vitae including a publication list, and contact information for three references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/182191": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Geosciences Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Geosciences at Princeton University invites applications for postdoctoral or more senior research positions in areas such as Geochemistry, Geomicrobiology, Environmental Chemistry, Biogeochemical Cycles, Paleoclimatology, Oceanography, Atmospheric Science, Geodynamics, Geochronology, Earth History, Seismology, and Planetary Science. Postdoctoral Associate positions are available for one year, with a possibility for renewal contingent upon satisfactory performance and available funding. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Geosciences or a closely-related field. Applicants should include a cover letter, a curriculum vitae including a publication list, and contact information for three references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181728": {"title": "***REVISED*** HMEI Environmental Fellows", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: High Meadows Environmental Institute Location: Princeton, NJ The High Meadows Environmental Institute (HMEI) at Princeton University is accepting applications for the 2027-28 HMEI Environmental Fellows Program. This postdoctoral program seeks scholars from all disciplines across the sciences, engineering, policy, and the humanities. The fellowship supports early career scholars in developing their research within a highly collaborative community of environmental scholars. In addition to their research, scholars are expected to contribute to the undergraduate program in Environmental Studies. This can take the form of supporting an existing environmental studies course, helping to mentor students as interns, or working on independent projects, or teaching a course of their own design. Eligible candidates should have no more than two years of post-PhD research experience prior to the anticipated start date of September 1, 2027. Note: c urrent Princeton University postdoctoral researchers are not eligible to apply. Research areas of interest include, but are not limited to: atmospheric and oceanic sciences, biodiversity, biogeochemical cycles, carbon mitigation, climate and energy, climate change, disease and health, climate justice, cities and the environment, earth history, ecology and ecosystem science, environmental chemistry and microbiology, environmental engineering, environmental humanities, environmental justice, environmental media/visual culture, narrative and science communication, food and environment, health and environment, water resources, and hydrology. Before applying, candidates must identify and contact at least one (preferably two) Princeton University faculty members who are willing to provide collaborative research supervision and teaching and professional mentorship. Candidates are required to provide written confirmation of faculty\u2019s commitment to serve as mentor. More than 140 members of the Princeton University faculty, representing 30 academic departments (in social sciences, humanities, natural sciences and engineering), are affiliated with HMEI. HMEI's Faculty and Associated Faculty are listed on the Institute's website: http://environment.princeton.edu . Note: Any Princeton University faculty member may serve as a mentor for the Environmental Fellows Program. The HMEI Environmental Fellowship provides a competitive annual salary based on the applicant's experience, along with an allowance for travel to meetings and for research support. Appointments are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Applications received before November 2, 2026, 11:59 p.m. Eastern Time, will be given full consideration. Questions about the application process may be directed to environment@princeton.edu. PhD Applicants should include a CV, cover letter (1-2 pages), a statement of research interests and goals for the fellowship period (no more than 2 pages) and written evidence from a Princeton University faculty member of commitment to mentor the candidate should they be accepted into the program. The application should also include the names and email addresses for three non-Princeton referees familiar with their work. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/182492": {"title": "Currie C. and Thomas A. Barron Visiting Professor in the Environment and Humanities - AY 2027-28", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: High Meadows Environmental Institute Location: Princeton, New Jersey Princeton University seeks to appoint a distinguished humanist whose work is related to the environment for the 2027-28 academic year. The on-campus position will provide salary plus benefits for the academic year. The funds may be used to supplement a sabbatical leave. The position is supported by the High Meadows Environmental Institute - the interdisciplinary center of environmental research, education, and outreach at Princeton University. The person appointed will hold the title of the Currie C. and Thomas A. Barron Visiting Professor in the Environment and Humanities. Applicants should be accomplished scholars on leave from their home institution, who have exceptional records of publication and teaching and whose interests lie at the intersection of environmental issues and the humanities. Of particular interest are scholars with expertise and interests related to air and water sustainability, biodiversity, energy, environmental justice, food system change, global health, global warming, and wilderness histories and futures. There is no geographic limitation. Backgrounds in American studies, settler colonial studies, Indigenous studies, environmental art and media, environmental history, literature and environment, cultural anthropology and geography, and religion and ecology are especially welcome. In addition to accomplished scholars, environmental practitioners who may not hold a university position, but who have a distinguished record as leaders in national environmental organizations or in the arts may also qualify for the Barron Visiting position. The visitor will have a shared appointment in the High Meadows Environmental Institute and a supporting department at Princeton University. The incumbent Barron Visitor will be expected to contribute to the life of this vibrant academic center and to cultivate dialogue at the intersection of the humanities and the environment at Princeton. They will be expected to teach one course per semester subject to sufficient enrollment and approval by the Dean of the Faculty and to mentor/advise two to three students on independent projects. Other activities may include the organization of University/public lectures and forums on related topics. Remaining duty time may be devoted to research and writing and other professional pursuits. The incumbent's annual salary will be determined based upon their salary at the home institution, not to exceed the level established for an equivalent rank of associate or full professor in the supporting department. Applications should include (1) a cover letter; (2) a brief description of any previous experience in interdisciplinary and/or collaborative research; (3) a statement of research and teaching plans at the intersection of environment and humanities; (4) a current curriculum vitae and (5) for scholars on sabbatical leave, an indication of and justification for the level of support requested. A short list of finalists will be asked to submit further materials. The names and contact information for three references should be indicated as part of the application. However, HMEI will request letters only for the finalists. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University.\u00a0This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H\u20111B visa requiring consular processing. To apply, please visit https://apply.interfolio.com/182492. Application deadline is October 31, 2026, 11:59 pm eastern time. Questions about the application process may be directed to environment@princeton.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188468": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: High Meadows Environmental Institute Location: Princeton, NJ  The High Meadows Environmental Institute (HMEI), ( https://environment.princeton.edu/ ) invites applications for postdoctoral research associates focusing on the scientific, technical, policy, and human dimensions of environmental issues. These areas include issues surrounding global change; energy and climate; biogeochemical cycles; molecular geochemistry; biodiversity; conservation; environmental science and policy; infectious disease and global health; and sustainable development in impoverished and resource-challenged regions of the world. The position is for one year with the possibility of reappointment based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is on campus, in person. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Candidates are required to have a Ph.D. degree in a related field. Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a brief statement of research interest, and names and contact information for three references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/182298": {"title": "***REVISED*** Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Integrative Genomics Location: Princeton, NJ The Lewis-Sigler Institute for Integrative Genomics at Princeton University was established to create innovative research and teaching programs at the interface of modern biology and the more quantitative sciences. We seek applicants for postdoctoral or more senior research positions to join any of the several interdisciplinary research groups in our Institute. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Ph.D., M.D., or equivalent is required. Applicants should have training and a significant track record in one of the following areas: developmental biology experimental and/or theoretical biophysics experimental and/or computational genomics computer science, statistics, and/or machine learning with applications relevant to genomics bioinformatics population genetics / genomics evolutionary biology ecological genetics metabolomics proteomics cell signaling bacterial/yeast/animal cell molecular biology and genetics Applications are submitted online and should include a cover letter, a CV that includes a list of publications, a brief statement of research interests and goals, and t he names and contact information of three references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189347": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Integrative Genomics Location: Princeton, NJ A Postdoctoral Research Associate or more senior research position in computational biology is available in the Pritykin lab at the Lewis-Sigler Institute for Integrative Genomics and the Computer Science Department at Princeton University. We seek candidates with computational biology, bioinformatics, computer science, machine learning, statistics, data science, applied math and/or other quantitative backgrounds who are enthusiastic about bringing their expertise to address fundamental problems in biology and medicine using cutting-edge technologies. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. The Pritykin lab ( http://pritykinlab.princeton.edu ) develops computational methods for design and analysis of high-throughput functional genomic assays and perturbations, with a focus on multi-modal single-cell, spatial and genome editing technologies; and applies these methods to study regulatory genomics of cell function and cell-cell interactions, with a focus on immunology and cancer. The successful candidate will have an opportunity to lead and contribute to a range of exciting collaborative projects and develop new projects. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. PhD in a relevant field is required. Strong track record of productivity is essential. Experience with biological data analysis is a plus but not required. Applications are submitted online and should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae that includes a list of publications, a brief statement of research interests and goals, and three confidential letters of recommendation . This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Please email Dr. Yuri Pritykin at pritykin@princeton.edu with any questions about the position."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181068": {"title": "**Revised** Ludwig Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Ludwig Princeton Branch Location: Princeton, New Jersey Postdoctoral or more senior research positions are available in the Ludwig Princeton Branch to study metabolism and tumor-microenvironment interactions during cancer progression. Ludwig Princeton Branch is dedicated to accelerating the study of metabolic phenomena associated with cancer to develop new paradigms for cancer prevention and treatment. Its main research areas include: - Metabolic interactions between the tumor and the rest of the body, focusing on how the body supports tumor growth and metastasis, and how tumors induce cachexia and other microenvironmental changes. - Interplay between host metabolism, gut microbiome, and anti-cancer immune response. - Diet-induced tumor microenvironment changes during cancer progression and metastasis, and dietary strategies for the prevention and treatment of cancer. We welcome highly motivated candidates who have or expect to have a Ph.D. degree in the fields of molecular cell biology, cancer biology, chemical biology, biochemistry, cancer genomics, genetics, mass spectrometry, physical chemistry, computational and systems analysis. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. Applicants must select one or more research mentor/co-mentor from the following Principal Investigators and Ludwig Princeton Branch members . Please visit https://ludwigcancer.princeton.edu/ to learn more about each Principal Investigator . This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in- person on campus at Princeton University. Must have or expect to have a PhD degree in the fields of molecular cell biology, cancer biology, chemical biology, biochemistry, cancer genomics, genetics, mass spectrometry, physical chemistry, computational and systems analysis. Please submit a brief statement of research interests, curriculum vitae, and the contact information for three referees."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/182772": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - AI/machine learning for biochemistry", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Ludwig Princeton Branch Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Skinnider Lab at Princeton University aims to recruit a postdoctoral fellow or more senior research position to work on projects related to the development of AI/machine learning approaches for chemical and biochemical datasets. A major focus will be on introducing new AI models to chart the chemical \u201cdark matter\u201d of the mammalian metabolome; examples of such models include large supervised or self-supervised AI models for mass spectrometry data, or multimodal models integrating multiple sources of analytical data for the discovery of metabolites and other types of small molecules. The position will remain open until an excellent fit is found. The successful candidate will develop and apply computational approaches to biochemical datasets, with artificial intelligence/machine learning (AI/ML) being a major focus. Many of the laboratory\u2019s interests center around the discovery of previously unknown small molecules (metabolites) using mass spectrometry data. The candidate will have the opportunity to work directly with experimentalists to validate predictions made by their machine-learning models and drive wet-lab discoveries. The candidate may also have opportunities to work with research software engineers to translate their research into user-friendly software tools that will be used by a broad community. The scope of the work builds on recent publications from the laboratory introducing new approaches or datasets for metabolite discovery from small molecule mass spectrometry (e.g., https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09969-x, https://www.nature.com/articles/s42256-021-00407-x, https://www.nature.com/articles/s42256-024-00821-x, https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.analchem.5c06256, https://www.nature.com/articles/s41570-023-00570-2). The research is computational in nature but involves close interactions with experimental collaborators. Many of the problems are constrained by scattered or noisy data, and the successful candidate will be enthusiastic about contributing to data preprocessing and curation in addition to model development and evaluation. This opportunity will prepare candidates for a range of competitive positions in academia or industry that involve machine-learning for biological or chemical data, computational biology/chemistry, or drug discovery/design, among others. Mentorship is taken seriously and every effort will be made to ensure the candidate is able to achieve goals in the next stage of their career. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. This position is subject to Princeton University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. The successful candidate will be motivated, independent, and have strong written communication skills. Candidates are required to have experience in one or more of the following areas as demonstrated through at least one first-author publication: machine learning/computer science, computational biology/bioinformatics, cheminformatics. Individuals should have or be expected to have a PhD with appropriate research experience in computer science, computational biology, biological or chemical engineering, chemistry, biochemistry, or a related field. To apply online, please submit CV and cover letter. Cover letter should highlight 1-3 publications or preprints that you feel best address the requirement for experience in above-mentioned areas. Please also include contact information for three references. Qualified candidates who pass an initial screening may be provided with short programming exercises to assess their skills. Only suitable candidates will be contacted."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/182814": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Computational biology/computational mass spectrometry", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Ludwig Princeton Branch Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Skinnider Lab at Princeton University aims to recruit a postdoctoral fellow or more senior research position to work on projects related to computational analysis of mass spectrometric datasets. A major focus will be on the application of AI/machine learning models and other computational methods to discover unknown metabolites that have strong associations to experimental variables or phenotypes, but which have eluded identification. The position will remain open until excellent fits are found. The successful candidate will develop and apply computational approaches to mass spectrometry-based metabolomics datasets, with discovery of unknown metabolites being a major focus. The laboratory is engaged in several metabolite discovery campaigns involving mining of large-scale metabolomic datasets to discover novel metabolites associated with human disease, genetics, and dietary exposures. The candidate will lead computational analyses of these datasets, using the laboratory\u2019s suite of existing AI/ML tools to assign structures to unidentified peaks in metabolomic datasets (e.g., https://www.nature.com/articles/s42256-021-00407-x), and extending these tools or developing new models as needed. The candidate will have the opportunity to work directly with experimentalists to validate predictions and drive wet-lab discoveries, or to perform experiments themselves if they wish. This opportunity will prepare candidates for a range of competitive positions in academia or industry that involve computational biology/chemistry, machine-learning for biological or chemical data, metabolism, and drug discovery/design. Mentorship is taken seriously and every effort will be made to ensure the candidate is able to achieve goals in the next stage of their career. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to Princeton University's background check policy. The successful candidate will be motivated, independent, and have strong written communication skills. Candidates are required to have experience in one or more of the following areas as demonstrated through at least one first-author publication: computational biology/bioinformatics, metabolism/metabolomics, analytical chemistry/mass spectrometry, cheminformatics, or machine learning/computer science. Prior experience in computational mass spectrometry will be viewed favorably but is not a requirement if the candidate has complementary expertise. Individuals should have or be expected to have a PhD with appropriate research experience in computer science, computational biology, biological or chemical engineering, chemistry, biochemistry, or a related field. Qualified candidates who pass an initial screening may be provided with short programming exercises to assess their skills. To apply online, please submit CV and cover letter. Cover letter should highlight 1-3 publications or preprints that you feel best address the requirement for experience in above-mentioned areas. Please also include contact information for three references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191852": {"title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor, Ludwig Princeton Branch", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Ludwig Princeton Branch Location: Princeton, NJ Princeton University seeks applications for an assistant, associate, or full professor to contribute to a major initiative at the interface between cancer, immunity, and metabolism ( Ludwig Princeton Branch ).\u00a0 We welcome applications from candidates using biological, chemical, physical, engineering and/or computational approaches to tackle questions at this interface.\u00a0 We are particularly interested in candidates with plans to develop a research program involving artificial intelligence, mining of large-scale data, or cancer prevention (even if they have not explicitly worked on cancer prevention before). Candidate selected will be appointed to a tenured or tenure-track position in an academic department at Princeton appropriate for the candidate\u2019s area of specialization, and will also hold a corresponding membership in the Ludwig Institute for Cancer Research. Responsibilities include teaching and service in accordance with the standards for the academic department in which the candidate is appointed, and research, including operation of a vibrant research group to benefit from both Princeton University and Ludwig Princeton Branch funding (with expectations to compete also for external funds). \u00a0The research focus of the Ludwig Princeton Branch is cancer metabolism (including diet and associated sociological factors, as well as immune metabolism and tumor microenvironment), broadly defined to also encompass development of relevant physical, chemical, computational, and engineering technologies and exploration of relevant biological factors including diet and the microbiome. Applicants are expected to hold a PhD or MD, optionally with relevant additional research experience (industrial, postdoctoral, or as a faculty member), and a strong track record of research accomplishments. We value building a strong community with varying viewpoints and perspectives. Applicants should submit a description of research interests (typically 2 \u2013 3 pages), curriculum vitae, a list of publications, and inititate contacting three referees for letters of recommendation via the application system. Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled.\u00a0 For fullest consideration please apply by November 1, 2026. This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183246": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Mathematics Location: Princeton, NJ The Mathematics Department at Princeton University seeks to appoint Lecturers to teach courses, sections, and/or do grading in linear algebra, calculus, or upper division math courses. Appointments may be made for one or two semesters with the potential for renewal, and are expected to begin on September 1, 2026, or January 16, 2027. Appointment duty time and renewal eligibility will depend on the number of sections taught, enrollments, and departmental need. Review of applications will begin immediately. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing. Ph.D. in mathematics or a related field, and college-level teaching experience. To apply, please submit a cover letter, CV, teaching statement and contact information for one referee."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190816": {"title": "Instructorships/Asst. Professorships/Postdoctoral Research Associates/Associate Research Scholars", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Mathematics Location: Princeton, NJ The Princeton University Mathematics Department expects to offer several junior faculty positions and postdoctoral appointments for the 2027\u20142028 academic year. Assistant Professorships: 3-year renewable appointments; teaching experience preferred.\u00a0\u00a0Ph.D. required. Postdoctoral Research Associates or Associate Research Scholars: The Term of appointment is based on rank. \u00a0Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments.\u00a0 Ph.D. required. Fellow/Associate Research Scholar:\u00a0 4-year position, offered jointly by the Princeton University Mathematics Department and the School of Mathematics at the Institute for Advanced Study, for outstanding new Ph.D.\u2019s.\u00a0 Two years will be at Princeton and two years at the Institute for Advanced Study spent conducting research (without teaching duties).\u00a0 (Please see the advertisement under THE INSTITUTE FOR ADVANCED STUDY for additional details about the fellow position. Applicants must also apply to Institute for Advanced Study posting.) Instructorships: 1-year positions; normally renewed for 1-2 additional years. Ph.D. required. Veblen Research Instructorships: 3-year positions (offered jointly by the Princeton University Mathematics Department and the School of Mathematics at the Institute for Advanced Study) for outstanding new Ph.D.\u2019s.\u00a0\u00a0Typically, the first and third years of these appointments are spent teaching and conducting research at Princeton University and the second year is spent conducting research (without teaching duties) at the Institute for Advanced Study.\u00a0\u00a0(Please see the advertisement under THE INSTITUTE FOR ADVANCED STUDY for additional details about the Veblen Research positions. Applicants must also apply to Institute for Advanced Study posting.) Please note: Applicants will automatically be considered for all open junior faculty positions and postdoctoral appointments.\u00a0 The Department is interested in candidates who, through their research, teaching, and service, will contribute to the academic community\u2019s excellence and its diversity, broadly defined. Salary range: $75,000 - $144,000 DEADLINE FOR APPLICATIONS:\u00a0November 15, 2026, 11:59 p.m. EST The work location is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The Postdoctoral Research Associate position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing.\u00a0 Other visa sponsorships (including H-B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. All applications should be submitted via MathJobs: https://www.mathjobs.org/jobs/list/28695 For inquiries, please e-mail: application@math.princeton.edu . Applicants must submit the following items online at https://www.mathjobs.org/jobs/list/28695 : \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Cover Letter \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Curriculum Vitae \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Research Statement \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Teaching Statement \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Publication List \u00b7\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Four Reference Letters (to be submitted by reference writers at this site) Ph.D. in mathematics or a related field All applications should be submitted via MathJobs: https://www.mathjobs.org/jobs/list/28695"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185165": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Molecular Biology Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Molecular Biology at Princeton University has an occasional curricular need for lecturers to teach, co-teach, or lead precepts in areas such as molecular biology, cell and developmental biology, biochemistry, and genetics , and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. Any appointments and duty time are subject to enrollments and may be for one or two semesters during the 2026-2027 academic year. Positions would have an expected start date of September 1, 2026, or January 16, 2027. Individuals should possess a Ph.D. in Molecular Biology or related field and have experience with teaching at the undergraduate level.\u00a0 Successful candidates will attend weekly lectures, be present during lab, lead weekly discussions, hold regular office hours, meet with the course instructor(s), and assist in exam writing and grading as it applies to the course being taught. Princeton University is especially interested in qualified candidates who can contribute, through their research, teaching, and/or service, to the diversity and excellence of our academic community. Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, contact information for three references and answer the provided custom questions .\u00a0 All materials must be submitted as PDF files. This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Essential Qualifications Ph.D. in Molecular Biology or a related field is required. Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching at the undergraduate level is strongly preferred."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188114": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Molecular Biology Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Molecular Biology at Princeton University currently has research positions available at the postdoctoral and more senior research levels in the areas of biochemistry, biophysics, cell biology, structural biology, microbiology, developmental biology, virology, genetics and cancer biology. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. Candidates must have (or expect to have at the time of appointment) a doctoral degree in a related field. Interested applicants must apply online and submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, contact information for three references and a brief statement of research interests. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190686": {"title": "Assistant Professor", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Molecular Biology Location: Princeton, NJ, USA The Molecular Biology Department at Princeton University invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level. We are seeking a colleague who will build a world-class research program in Molecular Biology, broadly defined. The applicant would join a vibrant community of researchers who study fundamental life processes in model organisms, including the disciplines of biochemistry, biophysics, structural biology, cell biology, computational biology, development, cancer, genetics and genomics, immunology, microbiology, and virology. We are especially interested in attracting colleagues who will create a climate that embraces excellence, with a strong commitment to teaching and mentoring that will enhance the work of the department and that will attract and retain a diverse student body. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree and must demonstrate the ability to develop a creative, independent research program. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Applications \u2013 including a cover letter, curriculum vitae, three-page summary of current and proposed research, and contact information for three references (limited to three references). For full consideration, all materials must be received by November 1, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/180850": {"title": "***REVISED***Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Physics Location: Princeton on-campus The Physics Department at Princeton University is seeking applicants for postdoctoral and more senior research positions. The position is available in Professor Ali Yazdani\u2019s group.\u00a0 The successful candidate will be working on a wide range of problems in condensed matter physics using scanning probe microscopy techniques in the study of two-dimensional (2D) material systems and their stacks. The term of appointment is based on rank.\u00a0 Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at a more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. The position is subject to the University's background check policy. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. The candidate must have a Ph.D. in physics or related discipline and several years of experience in research in low temperature experimental condensed matter physics. Candidates with experience in either scanning probe microscopy or 2D devices\u2019 fabrication and measurements would be considered. The successful candidate will work in collaborative teams and is expected to mentor more junior scientists. Applicants must apply online and submit a curriculum vitae, a list of publications, and three reference letters of support."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181347": {"title": "***REVISED*** Postdoctoral Research Associate - Moore Foundation", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Physics Location: Princeton University  Through the department of Physics at Princeton University, openings are available for a postdoctoral research associate or more senior rank as part of the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation's Emergent Phenomena in Quantum Systems initiative (EPiQS). The term of the appointment is based on rank.\u00a0 Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at a more senior rank may have multi-year appointments. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. To apply online, please submit a cover letter, CV, Research Statement, Publication list and three confidential letters of reference. About the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation and EPiQS Initiative. The Moore Foundation believes in bold ideas that create enduring impact in the areas of science, environmental conservation and patient care. Visit Moore.org or follow @MooreFound. The foundation's $185-million EPiQS initiative promotes discovery-driven research in the field of quantum materials ( http://www.moore.org/programs/science/emergent-phenomena-in-quantum-systems ). Through a variety of funding approaches, EPiQS aims to enable a community of leading experimentalists, materials synthesis experts and theorists to maximize their potential to explore, discover and understand emergent behavior of complex quantum matter. The Moore Postdoctoral Scholars in Theory of Quantum Materials program is an integral part of EPiQS and supports theoretical physicists of outstanding promise at an early stage of their careers. The Moore Postdoctoral Scholars are provided with a significant independence in selection of their research directions within their departments and can acquire a breadth of expertise by working with multiple faculty members. Ph.D.; Outstanding research in theoretical condensed matter physics, appropriate to level of the position; Ability to identify and pursue research problems independently while working well in an interactive and dynamic setting."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/182604": {"title": "***REVISED*** Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Physics Location: Princeton University, Fermi Lab, and or CERN - Switzerland Princeton University is seeking a postdoctoral research associate in experimental particle physics, accelerator physics, and/or scientific computing.\u00a0 The postdoctoral research associate(s) will work with the Princeton High Energy Experiment Group on research at the intersection of collider simulation, modern ML/computing, and future accelerator R&D, with a primary project in Muon Collider (MuC) R&D and the option to pursue a joint project connected to CMS analysis. A muon collider is a leading candidate for the next energy-frontier facility, and the postdoc will lead or co-lead work in one (or a combination) of several areas spanning detector, computing, and accelerator R&D. On the detector and computing side, this can include beam-induced background (BIB) simulation benchmarking and validation or development of fast or accelerated simulation workflows. On the accelerator side, the work can include beam simulations covering the front-end, transport, and interface regions (including cross-checks and uncertainty studies across toolchains), Muon Cooling or HTS magnet simulations relevant to muon collider systems, such as assessing field and engineering constraints and their impact on optics and overall performance. Postdocs will be embedded in a multi-institution collaboration (including partners at the University of Chicago and the University of Tennessee, and close connections to Fermilab and the international muon collider community). Responsibilities include mentoring junior researchers (post-bacs, graduate students, undergraduates), contributing to shared software practices (repos, documentation, validation), and presenting results in collaboration meetings and conferences. Optional joint project (CMS analysis): By mutual agreement, the postdoc may also lead or co-lead a complementary CMS analysis or analysis-adjacent effort (e.g., trigger/online ML, reconstruction performance studies, or a physics analysis aligned with the group\u2019s expertise). This joint structure is intended to provide a clear pathway to high-impact publications and visibility within a major LHC collaboration while building expertise relevant to future collider programs This postion will be based at Princeton University or at CERN in Geneva, Switzerland or at FermiLab near Chicago, Illinois, subject to discussion with the Principle Investigator.\u00a0 The work location for this position is in-person, on campus at the selected location.\u00a0 The term of the appointment is based on rank.\u00a0 Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance, continued funding and meeting all requirements; those hired at a more senior rank may have multi-year appointments. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Essential Qualifications: Ph.D. in Experimental Particle Physics, Accelerator Physics, or a closely related field (computational physics/scientific computing/data science/ML acceptable with relevant research experience), by the start date Strong programming skills in Python and/or C++, and experience with collaborative software development (version control, documentation) Demonstrated ability to deliver research outcomes (papers, talks, or documented software/analysis deliverables) Strong interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills; ability to work collaboratively across disciplines Preferred Qualifications: Experience with one or more of: accelerator/beamline simulation tools, particle-transport/detector simulation (e.g., Geant4/FLUKA/MARS), cooling-channel modeling, magnet/EM simulation, GPU/performance optimization, ML frameworks (PyTorch/TensorFlow), or computing on constrained systems Experience in a major collider collaboration (CMS/ATLAS) and/or a track record of analysis leadership Interest in bridging near-term LHC physics impact with longer-term future-collider R&D Applicants must apply online and must include a CV, a 1\u20132 page statement of research experience and interests, and a cover letter and three confidential letters of reference.\u00a0 The position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. Application review will begin in March 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. For additional information, contact\u00a0Prof. Isobel Ojalvo\u00a0(Princeton University)."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/182763": {"title": "***REVISED*** Postdoctoral Research Associate - Computational HEP", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Physics Location: Princeton, Switzerland (CERN) or Fermi Lab, Chicago, Illinois  Princeton University is seeking one (or more) postdoctoral or more senior research associates to work with the High Energy Experiment group in the Princeton Physics Department on computational and artificial intelligence research in experimental High Energy Physics (HEP). HEP experiments advance the understanding of elementary particles that are the fundamental constituents of matter and their interactions. Obtaining scientific results from these experiments requires complex software and computing systems, developed by international teams of researchers over decades. The resulting scientific data sets are among the largest in the world. The successful applicant(s) is expected to play a leadership role in one or more of the computational HEP R&D projects being pursued by the Princeton group for the High Luminosity Large Hadron Collider (HL-LHC) and future collider concepts. These include research into highly performant data analysis systems, design and implementation of novel event reconstruction and L1 trigger algorithms, and the application of novel artificial intelligence and machine learning techniques to all aspects of HEP experiments. Princeton leads the Institute for Research and Innovation in Software for High Energy Physics (IRIS-HEP, http://iris-hep.org/ ), which is developing innovative solutions to the computational and data challenges of the HL-LHC, which will collect data through the 2030's. Our research program also includes detector component simulation and data reconstruction algorithms of the key4hep software ecosystem being developed in the context of the future collider community (specifically Future Circular Collider (FCC) and muon collider concepts). The successful applicants will also have the opportunity to do their own research on the CMS experiment at the Large Hadron Collider at the European Laboratory for Particle Physics (CERN) in Geneva, Switzerland. For additional information, contact Dr. Peter Elmer ( Peter.Elmer@cern.ch). This position will be based at Princeton University or at CERN in Geneva, Switzerland or at Fermilab near Chicago, Illinois, subject to discussion with the principal investigator.\u00a0 The work location for this position is in-person, on campus at the selected location.\u00a0 The term of the appointment is based on rank.\u00a0 Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding and meeting all requirements; those hired at a more senior rank may have multi-year appointments. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Essential Qualifications: Ph.D. in Experimental Particle Physics or a closely related field (Research or Scientific Computing Software, Computational Science, Data Science, and Machine Learning), or advanced degree in Computer Science or a related field with a focus on applications Strong programming skills, in particular with Python and/or C++ Experience with AI/ML techniques and tools including model training and validation Experience with one or more of the following: data analysis of large scientific datasets, trigger/reconstruction algorithms for large high energy or nuclear physics detectors, software development for GPUs and other new architectures as well as related performance optimizations Experience developing scientific software applications for use by large scientific collaborations Strong interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills Ability to work collaboratively with researchers, faculty, and staff from diverse backgrounds Preferred Qualifications: Ability to direct efforts of others within teams of various sizes Experience working in large, international scientific collaborations and delivering software in such contexts Experience with AI/ML model deployment Applications must include a curriculum vitae, a one-page statement of research experience and interests, a cover letter, and three confidential letters of recommendation. The position is subject to the University's background check policy. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis as they arrive, and all applications received by May 31,2026 11:59 (EST) , will receive full consideration."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188568": {"title": "Associate Research Scholar, Dicke Fellow", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Physics Location: Princeton, NJ The Princeton University Physics Department seeks exceptional Ph.D. recipients in experimental physics for an honorific postdoctoral fellowship program. Candidates from all areas of experimental physics will be considered and selected through an annual competition. Appointments, to be made at the rank of Associate Research Scholar, will be for three years with an anticipated start date of September 1, 2027. Selected scholars will be \"Dicke Fellows\" in recognition of Princeton physicist Robert Dicke. In addition to salary and benefits, the program will provide a discretionary fund of $15,000 per year for each scholar. Selected scholars are encouraged to work with existing experimental groups in the department, although those who wish to pursue a specific independent research program will also be considered. Princeton has experimental physics research programs in Atomic/Molecular, Biological, Condensed Matter, Cosmology, Gravitation, High Energy, and Particle Astrophysics. Prospective Fellows are encouraged to discuss in their application the potential research group or groups they would be interested in collaborating with. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Proof of Ph.D. must be submitted prior to start date. Candidates who are not selected for a Dicke position may also be considered for other postdoctoral positions in the Department. The work location for this position will either be on campus at Princeton University or, subject to approval, at an off-campus location where the research project is undertaken. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B Visa requiring consular processing; other Visa sponsorships (including H-1B Visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applicants must apply online and must include a cover letter, CV, list of publications, a statement of research interests, and confidential letters from three referees. Finalists will be invited to give a research talk, either at Princeton or remotely, if travel to Princeton is not feasible. Applications will be reviewed as they arrive. To ensure full consideration, all materials must be received by October 19, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188739": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Physics Location: Princeton  Research Positions - The Physics Department at Princeton University is seeking applicants for postdoctoral and more senior research positions.\u00a0 Applicants with experience in the following areas are encouraged to apply:\u00a0 Experimental Condensed Matter Physics, Cosmology and Astrophysics, Particle Astrophysics, and Dark Matter, High Energy Physics, Atomic, Pulsar and Biophysics; Theoretical Cosmology, Condensed Matter, Mathematical, and Particle Physics. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy.\u00a0 The work location for these positions will be in-person on campus at Princeton University, or subject to approval, at an off-campus location where the research project is being undertaken. The term of the appointment is based on rank.\u00a0 Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibilty of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding and meeting all requirements; those hired at a more senior rank may have multi-year appointments. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B Visa requiring consular processing; other Visa sponsorships (including H-1B not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Candidates for the postion should have a Doctorate degree in Physics or a related discipline. Applicants must apply online and must include a cover letter, a CV, and three confidential letters of reference/support."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/188869": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Physics Location: Various   The Physics Department at Princeton University is seeking applicants for postdoctoral and more senior research positions. The term of appointment is based on rank.\u00a0 Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at a more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. The work location for this position may be in-person on campus at Princeton University, as well as, off-campus locations which include, but are not limited to, CERN in Geneva, Switzerland, FermiLab near Chicago, Illinois, at LMGS in L'Aguila, Italy, at CAPACITY in Caserta, Italy or at Ash River Laboratory near Ash River, Minnesota, subject prior approval and discussion with the Principal Investigator. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B Visa requiring consular processing; other Visa sponsorships (including H-1B Visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applicants with experience in the following areas are encourages to apply:\u00a0 Experimental condensed matter physics, cosmology and astrophysics, particle astrophysics and dark matter, high energry, atomic, pulsar and biophysics; theoretical cosmology, condensed matter, mathematical, and particle physics.\u00a0 Candidates for the position must have a Doctorate Degree in Physics or a related discipline. Applicants must apply online and must submit a cover letter, CV, and three confidential letters of reference/support.\u00a0 This position is submit to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192724": {"title": "Associate Research Scholar", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Physics Location: Princeton, NJ The Leinweber Forum for Theoretical Physics at Princeton University invites exceptional Ph.D. recipients for an honorific postdoctoral fellowship program. The Leinweber Forum at Princeton was recently established by a generous gift from the Leinweber Foundation .\u00a0 Princeton joins as the ninth member of th e Leinweber institutes for theoretical physics, which is focused on advancing curiosity-driven research on the theoretical principles underlying the physical universe . The forum will be an interdisciplinary hub, drawing researchers from the departments of physics, astrophysical sciences and mathematics working on topics connected to high-energy theoretical physics, gravitational physics, gravitational astrophysics, cosmology, and the mathematics research underpinning these areas. Appointments, to be made at the rank of Associate Research Scholar, will be for three years with an anticipated start date of September 1, 202 7 . The work location for this position is in- person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. To apply please submit a cover letter (which can include a research statement of up to three pages in length), CV, and contact information for three references. PhD must be completed prior to the start of the appointment. To ensure full consideration, all materials, including letters of reference, must be received by November 30, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194503": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Physics Location: Princeton University  Princeton University seeks a postdoctoral research associate or a more senior researcher to develop Penning-trap experiments for quantum sensing and precision studies of fundamental symmetries using trapped atomic and molecular ions. Specifically, the Udrescu Lab seeks a highly motivated applicant with a strong background in the design, construction, and operation of Penning traps. The lab, located within the Department of Physics, develops experimental techniques to study fundamental physics phenomena, with the long-term goal of building novel research platforms using negatively charged species and radioactive atoms and molecules. The successful candidate will play a leading role in developing experiments to investigate the low-energy nuclear structure of protactinium-229 and to trap and cool thorium negative ions (Th\u207b). Relevant expertise includes ion trapping and manipulation, ultrahigh-vacuum and cryogenic systems, charged-particle detection, low-noise electronics, and experimental control. Experience with laser spectroscopy, ion-optics and laser cooling simulations, or radioactive ion beams would also be valuable. The ideal candidate is an excellent troubleshooter with great technical abilities, a proven ability to work independently, and a collaborative mindset. They will be expected to lead the design and commissioning of experimental apparatus, develop and carry out research projects, mentor junior researchers, and collaborate with other laboratories and rare-isotope facilities on challenging long-term projects. The work location will be primarily in-person on campus at Princeton University, or subject to approval at an off-campus location where the research project is being undertaken. The term of the appointment is based on rank.\u00a0 Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year, renewable annually based on funding and meeting all requirements; those hired at a more senior rank may have multi-year appointments. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B Visa requiring consular processing; other Visa sponsorships (including H-1B not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Essential qualifications \u00b7 A doctoral degree in physics, electrical engineering, or a closely related field by the start of the appointment. \u00b7 Hands-on experience designing, constructing, and troubleshooting scientific instruments. \u00b7 Prior experience with designing and building Penning traps. \u00b7 Experience integrating hardware and software for experimental control and data acquisition. \u00b7 Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively. \u00b7 Excellent verbal and written communication skills in English. Preferred skills \u00b7 Experience designing, constructing and commissioning Penning traps. \u00b7 Experience in one or more of the following areas: o Ion loading, confinement, cooling, and detection. o Non-neutral plasma control, including rotating-wall techniques. o Ultrahigh-vacuum systems, cryogenics, and superconducting magnets. o Low-noise electronics, radiofrequency techniques, and precision electrode-voltage control. o Electrostatic modeling and charged-particle trajectory simulations (e.g., SIMION or COMSOL). o Laser spectroscopy, laser stabilization, or laser cooling. o Electron detection, charged-particle spectroscopy, or experiments with radioactive ions. \u00b7 Demonstrated ability to develop experimental apparatus to address a scientific question. \u00b7 Experience mentoring students and contributing to collaborative research projects. Applicants must apply online and must include a cover letter, a CV, and contact information for three references; however, letters of reference will only be requested for shortlisted candidates."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181383": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Princeton Neuroscience Institute Location: Princeton Neuroscience Institute  The Princeton Neuroscience Institute at Princeton University currently has research positions available at the postdoctoral or more senior research levels in the areas of neuroscience, psychology, molecular biology, biochemistry, physics, computer science, and genetics. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. Interested applicants must include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a brief statement of research interests, and\u00a0 at least two letters of recommendations. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Essential Qualifications: PhD in a relevant discipline."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192001": {"title": "Senior Research Scholar: Connectomics", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Princeton Neuroscience Institute Location: Princeton, NJ The Princeton Neuroscience Institute at Princeton University invites applications for a Senior Research Scholar to pursue an independent program in connectomics. The scholar will formulate and investigate scientific questions about the organization and function of nervous system through the analysis and interpretation of large-scale connectomes. As part of this research program, the scholar will develop computational methods, software, and data resources\u00a0 to enable original scientific investigations and reproducible analysis. The successful candidate will conduct independent and collaborative studies, and publish resulting scientific findings. The scholar will collaborate with Princeton faculty and external investigators and will seek external funding to advance independent and collaborative connectomics research. Key Responsibilities Conduct Original Research in Computational Connectomics: Formulate and investigate research questions in connectomics, reconstruction and annotation, cell-type organization, comparative connectomics, circuit analysis, and relationships between connectivity and function. Develop new computational approaches where needed to address these questions. Publish and Disseminate Research: Produce original scientific contributions in connectomics. Publish research findings, present results at scientific meetings, and release datasets, software, and analytical resources to the research community. Contribute to Mentoring and Teaching: Co-mentor undergraduate and graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, research staff, and software engineers, in collaboration with faculty. Contribute to workshops, tutorials, and other research training activities. Possibility of teaching or co-teaching undergraduate courses, subject to enrollment, curricular need and applicable University approvals. While teaching, the Senior Research Scholar will carry the additional part-time rank of Lecturer. Seek Funding for New Research Directions: Identify funding opportunities; prepare and submit grant applications, contribute to sponsored research projects, and develop new scientific collaborations and research initiatives. Essential Qualifications Ph.D. in computer science/computational neuroscience or a related field. A minimum of ten years of relevant research experience following completion of the Ph.D. Recognized distinction and sustained scholarly achievement in connectomics, computational neuroscience, or a closely related field. Demonstrated record of original and creative research and independent contributions to knowledge. Strong record of peer-reviewed publications demonstrating significant contributions to the field. Demonstrated ability to formulate and pursue an independent program of research. Ability to guide and direct researchers and students participating in programs of research. Substantial experience developing computational methods, research software, or data infrastructure for large-scale neuroscience. Strong scientific communication and collaboration skills. Preferred Qualifications Experience with analyzsing petabyte-scale electron microscopy connectomic data and associated connectivity/annotation meta data. Expertise in large graph algorithms, machine learning, circuit analysis, or comparative connectomics. Experience leading the development and operation of scientific software platforms, databases, or community data resources. Experience integrating and disseminating large scientific datasets. Experience preparing grant applications and managing externally funded research projects. Experience teaching connectomics, computational neuroscience, data analysis, or related subjects. A record of extensive collaboration across laboratories, institutions, or scientific consortia. Demonstrated experience leading interdisciplinary scientific and technical projects. Appointments, to be made at the rank of Senior Research Scholar, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027 . This position follows a hybrid work arrangement, with work performed both on the Princeton University campus and remotely. This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy and review and approval by the Advisory Committee on Appointments and Advancements for Academic Professionals. Interested applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement, bibliography and contact information for two references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192521": {"title": "Assistant Professor", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Princeton Neuroscience Institute Location: Princeton, NJ The Princeton Neuroscience Institute (PNI) at Princeton University invites applications for a new assistant professor, starting in 2028-29, in all areas of Neuroscience that use non-human primates as a model system. We are particularly interested in applications from individuals whose research program connects to, and expands on, the research areas within the department. This includes, but is not limited to, sensory processing, motor behavior, cognitive control, development, learning and memory, social behaviors, and neurotechnology. We welcome all approaches, including behavior, imaging, electrophysiology, causal manipulations, cellular and molecular approaches, and method development using non-human primates. We particularly encourage candidates whose work combines multiple experimental approaches with cutting-edge analytical approaches to understand the brain. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Neuroscience, or related discipline and have a demonstrated record of excellence and leadership in research, commensurate with career stage, with a strong commitment to teaching and mentoring that will enhance the work of the department and that will attract and retain a diverse student body. We welcome applications from members of all groups. The successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses in neuroscience and to establish or maintain an active, internationally-recognized research program. The candidate will also be expected to contribute to the development of interdisciplinary programs and collaborations within PNI and across the university. In addition to other outstanding neuroscience resources at Princeton, a 248K square foot building houses state-of-the-art experimental resources that support the full range of neuroscientific methods, including recording in animal model systems, cellular and circuit level imaging and brain imaging. PNI comprises 28 core faculty members, whose research interests span from molecular and cellular neuroscience, systems and circuits neuroscience, human cognitive neuroscience, to theory and computational neuroscience. We seek creative and enthusiastic candidates with the background and skills to build upon and complement these existing strengths and extend PNI into new and exciting research areas. Review of applications will begin on October 19, 2026 . Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, including a list of publications and presentations, a 3-5 page summary of research accomplishments and future plans, a 1-2 page teaching statement. Three (3) reference letters should also be submitted at the time of application. This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193741": {"title": "Lecturer - Computational and Cognitive Neuroscience", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Princeton Neuroscience Institute Location: Princeton, NJ, United States The Princeton Neuroscience Institute seeks a full-time lecturer to teach courses, and /or teach precepts with an anticipated start date of January 16, 2027. The initial appointment will be through July 1, 2027, and may be renewed at 12-month increments, contingent on satisfactory performance and curricular need. The primary responsibility of the successful applicant will be to teach neuroscience laboratory courses for both undergraduate and graduate students in computational and human cognitive neuroscience. The major objective of these courses is to introduce students to both computational modeling as well as the process of experimental design, execution and analysis using modern methods, including large scale electrode recording, fMRI, EEG, fNIRS, MEG, TMS. T he successful candidate may have the ability to teach precepts in other departments, as needed. The successful candidate will also have the opportunity to conduct independent research and publish. The Lecturer will work with faculty to design and update courses where appropriate. They will be responsible for preparing and presenting lectures, laboratory design and setup, creating/ updating assignments utilizing modern open-sourced Python-based analysis tools, writing and grading examinations, meeting with students outside of class time, monitoring teaching assistants, and assigning grades. Essential Qualifications: Ph.D. in neuroscience or related fields (such as computer science, math or engineering) with a specific focus on relevant applications in computational and human cognitive neuroscience. Proficiency with Python and Jupyter Notebooks. Must have an understanding of mathematical and computational tools used for computational modeling of, and analyzing, behavioral and neuroscientific data. Preferred Qualifications: Experience in teaching at the undergraduate and graduate level. Familiarity with machine learning. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching statement and contact information for three referees via Interfolio.\u00a0 Preference will be given to candidates who apply by November 30, 2026. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. We seek faculty members who will create a climate that embraces excellence and diversity, with a strong commitment to teaching and mentoring that will enhance the work of the department and attract and retain a diverse student body, both at the undergraduate and graduate levels."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194280": {"title": "C.V. Starr Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Princeton Neuroscience Institute Location: Princeton, NJ, United States Endowed by the Starr Foundation, the CV Starr Fellowship program supports postdoctoral positions for neuroscientists with exceptional skills and potential. The CV Starr Fellows Program encourages collaboration across fields, new perspectives and novel approaches, and promotes the Institute as an international center of excellence. Postdoctoral Research Associates are expected to pursue their research with the guidance and mentorship of one or more core faculty members in the Institute. Work at the Princeton Neuroscience Institute spans from molecular, cellular, and genetic approaches to systems and human cognitive neuroscience, and houses state of the art facilities for experimental research in all of these areas, as well as advanced computational resources that support theoretical and quantitative approaches to neuroscience. The C.V. Starr Fellowship program provides an attractive salary and a modest research budget. We encourage applicants to reach out to their prospective PNI faculty mentor(s) to gauge interest and discuss their research proposal prior to applying. Appointments are for one year and are renewable for up to two additional years based on performance and meeting all other requirements. A PhD is required at the time of appointment. The department seeks applicants at the rank of Postdoctoral Research Associate. Applications should include a 2-page research proposal; the candidate's C.V.; a cover letter; and three recommendation letters: - one from the candidate's current advisor (either Ph.D. advisor or postdoctoral advisor, depending on the candidate's current career stage), - a second letter from the candidate's prospective (or current) advisor at PNI, who should be either Core or Associated faculty at PNI. - and a third letter from a scientist who has knowledge of the candidate and their work. A review committee will weigh multiple factors in evaluating applications, including the quality and prospective impact of the research proposal, and the strength of the candidate's C.V. (scaled by years relative to graduation, meaning that the more time elapsed since graduation, the stronger the expected publication record). Applicants working across all areas of neuroscience-related research are strongly encouraged to apply. Deadline for submission is November 6, 2026. Decisions are expected by late November. For inquires, contact the faculty lead, Carlos Brody brody@princeton.edu . The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189683": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Psychology Location: Psychology The Princeton Social Neuroscience Lab invites applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate, or a more senior researcher, expected to begin in Fall 2026. We are looking for a creative and productive researcher with a doctoral degree in psychology, neuroscience, or a related field. The postdoctoral associate will lead research investigating the neurocognitive processes supporting naturalistic social interactions using fMRI hyperscanning. An ideal candidate will have (i) evidence of an existing research program in social or cognitive neuroscience, (ii) a strong background in designing and analyzing neuroimaging experiments, (iii) skills in advanced fMRI analyses and scientific computing, and (iv) excellent organizational, interpersonal, and communication skills. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. In addition to the aforementioned project, the appointee will have opportunities to develop additional projects with members of Dr. Tamir\u2019s lab and/or maintain their on-going work. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applicants must apply online and include a CV, cover letter indicating relevant experience and interests, 1-2 representative publications, and the contact information for two references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185848": {"title": "Senior Research Scholar", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment at Princeton University seeks a senior research scholar position within the Energy Systems Analysis Group. Individuals with a PhD and research interests in energy systems are encouraged to apply. The individual will lead the Center\u2019s multi-disciplinary collaborations seeking to better understand the opportunities and challenges facing the rapid decarbonization of major-emitting countries and sectors. This work builds on Princeton\u2019s influential Net-Zero America study and ongoing collaborations across the globe. The research aims to identify pathways to accelerate decarbonization of energy systems which effectively navigate bottlenecks, barriers and resistive forces. Increased temporal and spatial granularity, and deliberative engagement with companies, governments, and other stakeholders is paramount to enhance models and increase the robustness of insights that can help inform energy and industrial policies, identify needed technological and institutional innovations, and support behavioral changes. A PhD is required in relevant field. Applicants should have a strong background in relevant energy systems science and/or energy-relevant fields of engineering, economics, or social sciences. We are specifically interested in candidates with leadership experience in building collaborative research initiatives, and/or leadership experience in the private sector, public administration, or NGO\u2019s, related to energy and industrial decarbonization. Candidates must have excellent English written and verbal communication skills. To be considered for this senior leadership position, candidates must have a documented record of impact in research through publications, entrepreneurship, policy and business influence, and/or leadership roles in relevant research, industry, government, or non-profit organizations. They must further demonstrate that they possess recognized distinction at a national and international level, demonstrated independence of thinking, innovation, creativity and initiatives in research, and mentoring. Applicants must submit a CV, cover letter, research statement and contact information for three references. This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189606": {"title": "Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment Distinguished Postdoctoral Fellowships", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment invites applications for its Distinguished Postdoctoral Fellows program, with start dates as early as June 2027. This program supports outstanding scholars whose research focuses on innovative solutions to pressing technological, economic, and/or policy challenges relating to energy and the environment. Fellows must collaborate with two or more Princeton faculty or senior researchers within an energy or environment-related field and work with them to establish an interdisciplinary research plan that aligns with the Andlinger Center\u2019s mission. Research that aligns with one or more of the Center\u2019s Research Initiatives is particularly encouraged. Candidates whose plans forge new collaborations with or between associated faculty and researchers in the Andlinger Center\u2019s research directory are strongly preferred, though proposals that extend existing collaborations in creative new directions will be considered. Appointments are anticipated for two years, with renewal after the first year contingent on satisfactory performance. Fellows will hold the rank of postdoctoral research associate, with a salary of $75,000 and $3,000 per year for conference travel and career development. Teaching Participation in teaching is not required, though fellows may guest lecture in courses, with guidance from Princeton mentor(s). Interested fellows are encouraged to pursue workshops and training in pedagogy offered through Princeton\u2019s McGraw Center for Teaching and Learning. For full consideration, complete applications must be submitted by December 4, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. ET. Eligible candidates must have received their doctorate in a related field no more than three years prior to the anticipated start date. Preference will be given to candidates not currently affiliated with Princeton University. Applicants must develop a research proposal in consultation with two or more Princeton faculty or senior researchers with whom they intend to collaborate, at least one of whom must be identified as the primary mentor(s).\u00a0 Mentors will be required to submit a letter assessing the candidate\u2019s academic strength and proposed research plan and to commit a portion of the financial support. Princeton mentors who are currently hosting a Distinguished Postdoctoral Fellow are ineligible to serve as primary mentors; applicants should consult with their prospective mentors to ensure full eligibility. The materials listed below should be submitted to Princeton University academic hiring portal Interfolio by the application deadline. This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. Cover letter Curriculum vitae, including publications Research plan, developed in consultation with the prospective Princeton mentor(s), with a maximum length of 2 pages.\u00a0 The plan must explain the research context, the interdisciplinary framework of its objectives, and the anticipated impact. The plan should clearly articulate the technological or policy solutions proposed to address the challenges and demonstrate a strong connection to the Andlinger Center\u2019s mission. The research can be applied or fundamental, theoretical or experimental, or a combination of such. Prospective Princeton mentors will be contacted to ensure the applicant has consulted with them in developing the research plan. Personal statement (1-2 pages), demonstrating how the fellowship advances the applicant\u2019s career plans and identifying possible additional mentors that they plan to cultivate during the fellowship. Letters from 3 references, who will receive an email with instructions on how to send their recommendation. Contact information for the applicant\u2019s prospective Princeton primary mentor(s). Questions about the fellowships or application process may be directed to the Andlinger Center's assistant director for research, Charles Sharpless\u00a0( csharpless@princeton.edu ). This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189640": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment Location: Princeton, New Jersey One or more postdoctoral research associate or more senior research positions are available at Princeton University in the Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment. Researchers will join the Zero-carbon Energy systems Research and Optimization Laboratory (ZERO Lab) run by assistant professor Jesse D. Jenkins jointly appointed with the Andlinger Center and Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering. The ZERO Lab focuses on improving and applying optimization-based macro-energy systems to evaluate and optimize low-carbon energy technologies, guide investment and research in innovative energy technologies, and generate insights to improve energy and climate policy and planning decisions In particular, we are looking for scholars who can contribute to one of two research thrusts (or both): Applied operations research : scholars will develop and implement novel methods to improve the computational performance and resolution of large-scale optimization models for energy systems planning and operations, leveraging decomposition methods, machine learning, multi-scalar modeling, and other creative techniques. Applied energy systems modeling : scholars will help build and apply state-of-the-art macro-energy systems planning models to evaluate and optimize emerging low-carbon energy technologies and generate insights to improve energy and climate policy and planning decisions. A particular emphasis will be placed on candidates with familiarity with enhanced geothermal energy systems. Applicants should specify in their cover letters which of these two program areas (or both) they see themselves contributing to, the motivations for their research, and why they are qualified to contribute to cutting-edge research in these areas. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. Selected candidates may start on or after September 1, 2026. Ideal applicants will have prior experience in optimization methods and applied energy system modeling, particularly macro-scale energy systems; familiarity with algebraic modeling languages used for linear and mixed integer programming (e.g. Julia/JuMP, Python/Pyomo, AMPL, GAMS); and comfort working in a multidisciplinary and collaborative research environment. All candidates must have strong, demonstrated research ability, excellent English written and spoken communication skills and be very comfortable in an interdisciplinary research environment. Applicants should hold a PhD (or expect to complete a PhD prior to beginning employment at Princeton) in a relevant field, including mechanical engineering, chemical engineering, electrical engineering, systems engineering, energy science and engineering, energy resources, and operations research. The Andlinger Center is especially interested in candidates who will contribute to the excellence of the academic community and diversity of the curriculum, broadly defined. The term of appointment is based on rank. Candidates with three or more years of postdoctoral research experience may be considered for appointment at the rank of associate research scholar. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate . Inquiries about the positions may be sent to jessejenkins@princeton.edu . Application process Applicants must apply online using Princeton University\u2019s academic hiring portal Interfolio and submit a CV, cover letter, and contact information for three references. This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189677": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Hatzell group in the Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment at Princeton University is seeking applications for postdoctoral research associates or more senior research positions. The Hatzell group examines advanced x-ray and neutron characterization of battery applications. The position(s) will be based at the Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment, which is focused on resolving critical challenges to securing a sustainable energy and environmental future. We are seeking experts in electrochemistry, solid-state ionics, materials characterization and/or electrochemical engineering. We seek candidates with an interest in participating in large multi-PI grants and with collaborative research experiences, experience writing proposals for user-facilities, and/or experience leading and mentoring graduate and undergraduate students. A PhD in relevant fields of energy storage, electrochemistry, and materials characterization is required. Experience with solid electrolytes, membranes or synchrotron characterization is desirable but not a constraint. All candidates must have a strong research background and enjoy working in teams. All candidates must have strong, demonstrated research ability, be comfortable in an interdisciplinary research environment, and have excellent English language verbal and written communication skills. The Andlinger Center is especially interested in candidates who will contribute to the excellence of the academic community and diversity of the curriculum, broadly defined. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applicants must apply online using Princeton University\u2019s academic hiring portal Interfolio and submit a CV, cover letter, and contact information for three references. This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. Inquiries about the positions may be sent to kelsey.hatzell@princeton.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190394": {"title": "Gerhard R. Andlinger Visiting Fellow", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment invites applications for the Gerhard R. Andlinger Visiting Fellows Program , with start dates as early as August 2027. The program supports distinguished visitors-in-residence who can enhance the diversity and excellence of intellectual and academic life at the Center and strongly support its core mission . Applicants must propose a plan of activities to engage with the Center and broader Princeton community in ways that will significantly enrich the Center\u2019s research and/or educational programs. Proposals that address one or more of our Research Initiatives are of particular interest. This plan should extend beyond simply advancing the visitor\u2019s career development, research, teaching, or advancing a single collaborator\u2019s research. Examples include: Collaborations supporting team research with multiple Andlinger Center jointly appointed or associated faculty and/or research staff members on projects that advance core research areas . Guest lecturing and/or teaching an Andlinger Center energy studies (ENE) or ENE-cross-listed course, with the goal of having the course material absorbed into a permanent ENE or ENE-cross-listed course that will ensure sustained benefit for Princeton students. Teaching appointments require approval by the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. Presenting and/or organizing talks or workshops designed to augment conversation and expand expertise around key areas of interest to a broad spectrum of Andlinger Center associated faculty, research staff, and students. Successful candidates can come from a variety of backgrounds \u2013 industry, government, academia, and non-governmental organizations \u2013 with the key qualification being demonstrated in-depth expertise in energy and environmental issues. Duration and Funding The nominal duration for the visit is one year; shorter appointments may be requested and will be considered on an individual basis. Appointment rank is determined on the basis of seniority and current institutional affiliation. In accordance with University policy, employment verification and visitor participation agreements are required. The work location for this position is in person on campus at Princeton University. For full consideration, applications must be submitted by January 8, 2027 at 11:59 p.m. ET. Applicants must identify a Princeton sponsor with whom they should develop fellowship plans. Appropriate sponsors include Andlinger Center faculty, associated faculty, and senior researchers, who can be searched by research area . Proposals should also consider additional campus collaborators beyond the sponsor whose involvement would enrich the fellowship plans. Sponsors will be asked to submit a letter of support highlighting the candidate\u2019s unique attributes and the merit of their proposed activities. Prospective applicants are encouraged to contact the Andlinger Center\u2019s Assistant Director for Research prior to applying to discuss the program and their proposed plans. The following materials should be submitted by the application deadline: o\u00a0\u00a0 Cover letter; o\u00a0\u00a0 Curriculum vitae, including a list of publications or other professional accomplishments; o\u00a0\u00a0 Proposal of planned activities; o\u00a0\u00a0 List of potential collaborators briefly describing the possible collaboration; o\u00a0\u00a0 Contact information for the Princeton sponsor; and o\u00a0\u00a0 Contact information for two external references (collaborators or supervisors)."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191480": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Energy Systems Analysis Group and the Behavioral Science for Policy Lab (BSPL) are seeking an outstanding postdoctoral research associate or more senior researcher position expected to start in the Fall of 2026 .\u00a0 The appointment will be based in the Andlinger Center which is focused on resolving critical challenges to assuring a secure, resilient, and sustainable energy and environmental future. The post-doctoral researcher will report to Chris Greig and Elke Weber, and will collaborate with other researchers such as Richard Moss, post-docs, and graduate students across disciplines. The successful candidate will contribute to the design and implementation of an applied research project to understand the barriers to private sector investment climate adaptation and resilience and build new analytical tools to make the case for such investments. In particular, the position will seek to understand how private sector actors consider investments in energy and water infrastructure to make them more resilient to extreme but uncertain weather events, the frequency and severity of which are anticipated to increase with climate change.\u00a0 The researcher will also seek to develop analytical tools to model the returns on capital invested to strengthen infrastructure resilience under such climate uncertainty. This requires knowledge of investment decision processes and financial analysis under uncertainty.\u00a0 Research will include AI assisted text analysis of corporate sustainability reports, annual reports, interviews with company executives, discounted cash flow analysis with probabilistic cost and revenue projections, and agent-based modeling.\u00a0 We are particularly interested in candidates with experience in both social science methods and computational modeling. Other duties include contribution to other ongoing center projects, preparation of and participation in workshops, and drafting reports and papers for publication. The Andlinger Center is especially interested in candidates who will contribute to the excellence of the academic community and diversity of the curriculum, broadly defined. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Required qualifications. Ph.D. in operational research and financial engineering, economics, finance, or another relevant discipline. Computational or agent-based modeling experience Data analysis and modeling skills Proficiency in web-based computer programming (online experimentation and surveys). Desired qualifications: Publications Experience working as a member of interdisciplinary teams. Previous research or professional experience in energy and sustainability sectors. Application Materials: Cover letter CV 2 publications or writing samples 2 recommendation letters (to be submitted directly by references)"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192623": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment Location: Princeton, New Jersey Post-Doctoral Researcher: Blockchain-Enabled Virtual Chain-of-Custody Systems for Clean Molecule Markets The Energy Systems Analysis Group at Princeton University\u2019s Andlinger Center for Energy and the Environment is seeking an outstanding researcher for a full-time Post-Doctoral or more senior Researcher position. The appointment is expected to begin in Fall 2026, or as soon as practicable thereafter. The Andlinger Center is focused on resolving critical challenges to assuring a secure, resilient, and sustainable energy and environmental future. The post-doctoral researcher will report to Chris Greig and collaborate with researchers across engineering, computer science, and operations research. The successful candidate will contribute to an applied research project examining whether blockchain-enabled tokenization of low-carbon attributes can support credible and scalable virtual chain-of-custody systems\u2014and thereby improve the bankability of industrial decarbonization projects. The research will develop and evaluate a system architecture that combines distributed-ledger technology with measurement, reporting and verification, mass-balance accounting, and third-party certification. The initial use case will examine low-carbon ethylene and polyethylene value chains, in which physical materials may be commingled while verified environmental attributes are transferred digitally from producers to downstream manufacturers and brand owners. The researcher will compare conventional undifferentiated commodity sales, bilateral green-premium contracts, and token-based aggregation of dispersed downstream willingness to pay. Analysis will consider the implications for revenue visibility, risk allocation, financing costs, debt capacity, and final investment decisions for capital-intensive projects, including a large-scale steam-cracker retrofit. Research activities are expected to include: \u00b7 Designing a conceptual or prototype blockchain-based virtual chain-of-custody and token-transfer architecture. \u00b7 Developing rules for attribute definition, measurement, allocation, retirement, and claims, with reference to relevant mass-balance certification systems and Scope 3 greenhouse-gas accounting. \u00b7 Modelling chemical value-chain flows and comparing alternative commercial and contracting structures. \u00b7 Undertaking techno-economic and project-finance analysis to test how alternative demand-aggregation mechanisms affect project bankability. \u00b7 Conducting interviews and workshops with chemical producers, downstream manufacturers, brand owners, financial institutions, certification bodies, and technology providers. \u00b7 Preparing peer-reviewed papers, industry-facing reports, workshop materials, and presentations. Required qualifications \u00b7 PhD in engineering, operations research, computer science, or another relevant discipline. \u00b7 Strong quantitative research, data-analysis, and modelling skills. \u00b7 Demonstrated expertise in blockchain or distributed-ledger systems and at least one of the following additional areas: smart contracts or tokenization; life-cycle or greenhouse-gas accounting; measurement, reporting and verification; techno-economic or project-finance modelling; industrial supply chains or market design. \u00b7 Excellent written and oral communication skills and the ability to work across disciplinary and professional boundaries. Desired qualifications \u00b7 Research or professional experience in chemicals, polymers, energy, industrial decarbonization, or sustainability markets. \u00b7 Familiarity with mass-balance chain-of-custody standards, environmental-attribute markets, GHG Protocol Scope 3 accounting, or related certification systems. \u00b7 Experience conducting stakeholder interviews, workshops, or interdisciplinary applied research. \u00b7 A strong publication record or evidence of the ability to produce high-quality academic and industry-facing outputs. Application materials \u00b7 Cover letter describing the candidate\u2019s interest in the project and relevant experience. \u00b7 Curriculum vitae. \u00b7 Two publications or writing samples. \u00b7 Contact details for two references; recommendation letters may be requested from shortlisted candidates. The Andlinger Center is especially interested in candidates who will contribute to the excellence of the academic community and diversity of the curriculum, broadly defined. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194712": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Center for Information Technology Policy Location: Princeton, NJ The postdoctoral track of the CITP fellows' program is for people who have recently received or are about to receive a Ph.D. or doctorate degree and work on understanding and improving the relationship between technology and society. The priority areas of our center include (1) AI, Data Science and Society, (2) Privacy and Security, and (3) Platforms and Digital Infrastructure. CITP tightly integrates basic research, applied research, and societal engagement, enabling distinctive impact, both academic and societal. In this context, we welcome applicants from diverse backgrounds such as computer science, electrical and computer engineering, sociology, public policy, information science, communication, economics, political science, psychology, philosophy, and related technology disciplines. Selected candidates will be appointed at the postdoctoral research associate rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank will be for one year, renewable annually based on funding and meeting all other requirements. The anticipated start date is September 1, 2027. Fellows in the postdoctoral track are not required or expected to teach, but if they wish, they may have the option of teaching. When teaching, successful candidates will carry the additional part-time rank of Lecturer, and any teaching opportunities are subject to sufficient enrollments and the approval of the Dean of the Faculty. These positions are not elibile for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available as appropriate. Selected candidates are expected to pursue impact beyond the academy. There are many possible avenues, and we are happy to work with each fellow to help you figure out what works best for you. Postdoctoral track requirements: *Both CITP and Princeton University place high value on in-person collaborations and interactions. This position is in-person at CITP on the Princeton University campus. *All appointments are subject to the University background check policy. *Ph.D. or doctorate degree is required. The appointment will not be finalized, and an exact start date will not be set, until the candidate has defended their thesis or has proof of a doctorate degree. Official proof of Ph.D. should be provided by July 31, 2027. Applicants must apply on online and should include a current curriculum vitae, research statement, and a cover letter. Candidates must submit their applications by Monday, December 7, 2026 to receive full consideration. Please note that the online application will remain open until April 30, 2027; however, applications submitted after December 7, 2026 will only be reviewed if positions remain open. Contact information for three references is required. To learn more about CITP's fellows' program please visit our website ( https://citp.princeton.edu/programs/fellows/) . CITP is committed to building a culturally diverse and inclusive community. To maximize excellence, we seek talent from all segments of American society and the world, and we take steps to ensure everyone at Princeton can thrive while they are here. If you have any questions about this position or the application process, please feel free to contact us at citp@princeton.edu . All offers and appointments are subject to review and approval by the Dean of the Faculty. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194737": {"title": "Visiting Reseach Scholar", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Center for Information Technology Policy Location: Princeton, NJ The Visiting Professionals track of the Center for Information Technology (CITP) fellows' program is for professionals - academics and practitioners - who will be on leave from their job. These are typically nine-month appointments, commencing on or about September 1, 2027, but we are flexible, and positions can start anywhere between May 1, 2027 and October 1, 2027. The primary appointment will be at the rank of \"Visiting Research Scholar\" or \"Visiting Professional Specialist\" depending on experience. Selected candicates are expected to pursue impact beyond the academy. There are many possible avenues, and we are happy to work with each fellow to help you figure out what works best for you. *The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University *Advanced degree such as M.A., Ph.D. or J.D. *Applicants must be on leave from another institution. If you are selected, we will require documentation from your home institution stating that you are on leave during your entire appointment period while here at CITP. Candidates must submit their applications by Monday, December 7, 2026 to receive full consideration. Please note that the online application will remain open until April 30, 2027; however, applications submitted after December 7, 2026 will only be reviewed if positions remain open. Please submit a current curriculum vitae, a research statement, and a cover letter describing your background, interest in the program, and salary requirements, noting any support you will be receiving during that period, such as sabbatical funding. Selected candidates will be asked to indicate what percentage of financial support their home institution and other sources are likely to provide during their visit, if any. Written proof of employment and salary verification must be provided by the candidate's home institution for the appointment time period while at CITP. Applicants must apply online. To learn more about CITP's fellows' program please visit our website ( https://citp.princeton.edu/programs/fellows/) ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194745": {"title": "Microsoft Visiting Research Scholar/Visiting Professor of Information Technology Policy", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Center for Information Technology Policy Location: Princeton, NJ The Microsoft Visiting Research Scholar/Visiting Professor track of the Center for Information Technology Policy (CITP) fellows program is for visiting academics who are nationally or internationally recognized experts in technology policy. Appointments are typically for a term of up to nine months with an expected start date on or about September 1, 2027. The Microsoft Visiting Research Scholar/Visiting Professor is expected to collaborate and contribute to CITP\u2019s mission in at least one of the following ways: Teach one course related to their research during their nine-month term, subject to sufficient enrollment and with the approval of the Dean of the Faculty. Envision and lead a conference or a series of workshops during their time at CITP. Collaborate with existing faculty members on research and jointly mentor students or CITP\u2019s emerging scholars. Candidates will be required to provide detailed proposals in their cover letter pertaining to one or more of these three areas. The selected candidate\u2019s primary appointment will be at the rank of \"Visiting Research Scholar\" or \"Visiting Professional Specialist\" depending on experience. Typically, there will be one Microsoft Visiting Professor per year. If the appointed scholar teaches a course, they will carry the title of Microsoft Visiting (Assistant/Associate) Professor in the semester of teaching. *The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. *Ph.D. or J.D. *Nationally or internationally recognized expert in technology policy. *Applicants must be on leave from another institution. If you are selected, we will require documentation from your home institution stating that you are on leave during your entire appointment period while here at CITP. Candidates must apply online. Candidates must submit their applications by Monday, December 7, 2026 to receive full consideration. Please note that the online application will remain open until April 30, 2027; however, applications submitted after December 7, 2026 will only be reviewed if positions remain open. Please submit a current curriculum vitae, a research statement, and a cover letter describing your background, interest in the program, and salary requirements, noting any support you will be receiving during that period, such as sabbatical funding. Please include a proposal in your cover letter elaborating on how you plan to contribute to CITP during your term. If you are proposing to teach, please include details about potential topics you are willing to teach (provide a sample syllabus and reading list). If you are proposing to develop a conference or workshop series, please include a proposal describing the series, potential speakers, audience, and output of the conference. Finally, if you are proposing to collaborate on research with existing faculty members, please describe the joint project and potential mentorship opportunities associated with the project. Written proof of employment and salary must be provided by the candidate's home institution upon hire for the academic year. To learn more about CITP's fellow program please visit our website ( https://citp.princeton.edu/programs/fellows/ )."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/186022": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Chemical and Biological Engineering Location: Princeton, NJ The Rosen Research Group at Princeton University ( https://rosen.cbe.princeton.edu ) is searching for a postdoctoral researcher interested in computational materials science, particularly building upon recent developments in the area of machine learning interatomic potentials. The successful candidate will leverage and augment machine learning interatomic potentials to better address the complexities of real materials that are often neglected in traditional first-principles simulations. Expertise with density functional theory, molecular dynamics, AI model development, free energy predictions, and/or enhanced sampling methods applied to solid-state materials is desirable. Applicants must have (or expect to have at time of appointment) a Ph.D. in chemical engineering, materials science, physics, chemistry, or a closely related field are particularly encouraged to apply. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. This position is for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding.\u00a0 The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Candidates should include a cover letter, CV (including a list of publications), research statement (a discussion of past research, expertise, and research interests), and three confidential letters of referral."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192314": {"title": "*Revised* Open Rank Faculty Search", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Chemical and Biological Engineering Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Chemical and Biological Engineering at Princeton University seeks outstanding applicants for all tenure-track professor ranks with an effective start date as early as September 1, 2027. The search is open to candidates specializing in any area of chemical and biological engineering. A joint appointment with an appropriate academic unit may be considered. The successful candidate should have a Ph.D. in Chemical Engineering or a related field, demonstrated excellence in academic research, and a strong interest in teaching and advising undergraduate and graduate students. We seek faculty members who will create a climate that embraces excellence, with a commitment to teaching and mentoring that will enhance the work of the department and attract and retain a diverse student body. Candidates must complete an online faculty application at https://apply.interfolio.com/192314 . A curriculum vitae , detailed descriptions of teaching and research interests, and the names and contact information for at least three, but no more than five, references should be provided via the online application. This position is subject to Princeton University\u2019s background check policy.\u00a0 For fullest consideration, please apply by October 25, 2026."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181518": {"title": "***REVISED*** Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Civil and Environmental Engineering Location: Princeton, New Jersey Princeton University School of Engineering and Applied Science invites applications for postdoctoral and more senior research positions. Individuals with evidence of experience in scholarly research and a strong commitment to excellence in education are encouraged to apply. PhD is expected by the start date. Applicants must apply online and are required to include curriculum vitae, cover letter, research statement and names and contact information for three references. The Term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University.\u00a0 This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190584": {"title": "***REVISED*** Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Civil and Environmental Engineering Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering at Princeton University has an occasional curricular need for lecturers to teach, co-teach, or lead precepts in areas related to engineering in the modern world, and accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. Any appointments and duty time are subject to enrollments and may be for one or two semesters during the 2026-2027 academic year. Positions would have an expected start date of September 1, 2026, or January 16, 2027. Individuals should possess an advanced degree in civil and environmental engineering or a related field and should have experience with teaching at the undergraduate level.\u00a0 Successful candidates will attend weekly lectures, be present during lab, lead weekly discussions, hold regular office hours, meet with the course instructor(s), and assist in exam writing and grading as it applies to the course being taught. Princeton University is especially interested in qualified candidates who can contribute, through their research, teaching, and/or service, to the diversity and excellence of our academic community. Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references.\u00a0 All materials must be submitted as PDF files. This position is subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching at the undergraduate level is strongly preferred."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191055": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Urban and Regional Energy Systems Modeling", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Civil and Environmental Engineering Location: Princeton, New Jersey Princeton University\u2019s School of Engineering and Applied Science invites applications for a Postdoctoral\u00a0Research Associate position in Urban and Regional Energy Systems Modeling , with a focus on techno-economic, sustainability, and/or resilience analysis. The successful candidate will work with Professor Anu Ramaswami\u2019s Urban Nexus Lab in the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering at Princeton University . The successful candidate will contribute to several U.S.-based and international research projects related to urban and regional energy systems. The position will involve energy systems modeling and analysis across multiple spatial and systems scales, with opportunities to contribute to interdisciplinary research addressing the techno-economic performance, sustainability, and resilience of energy systems. The primary expertise sought for this position is in energy systems modeling . Candidates should have demonstrated experience working with energy systems models and applying quantitative and computational approaches to energy systems analysis. Applicants should have a Ph.D. in energy systems engineering, mechanical engineering, civil and environmental engineering, electrical engineering , or a closely related field.\u00a0 A Ph.D. in a relevant field is expected by the start date. Candidates should demonstrate: Experience in energy systems modeling and analysis, including model development, implementation, and interpretation of model outputs, and experience working with models at multiple spatial or systems scales. Experience with techno-economic analysis and/or sustainability and resilience analysis of energy systems. Proficiency in one or more programming, computational, or modeling environments commonly used for energy systems analysis, such as Python, MATLAB, R, Julia, GAMS, or equivalent tools . Strong quantitative, analytical, and problem-solving skills. Experience working with and analyzing quantitative datasets. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively as part of interdisciplinary research teams. Strong written and oral communication skills. Experience with mathematical optimization, scenario analysis, data visualization, GIS/geospatial analysis, or other computational approaches relevant to energy systems research is desirable but not required. Individuals with evidence of experience in scholarly research and a strong commitment to excellence in education are encouraged to apply. Applicants must apply online and are required to include a curriculum vitae, cover letter, research statement, and names and contact information for three references . Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding . This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University . This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192168": {"title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor, Civil and Environmental Engineering at Princeton University with focus on Interactions between Climate Extremes and Built Environment", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Civil and Environmental Engineering Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering (CEE) at Princeton University invites applications for a faculty position at rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor, in Interactions between Climate Extremes and Built Environment, with a preferred start date of September 1, 2027, or January 16, 2028. Candidates must have the ability and motivation to develop a vigorous independent research program and to participate in collaborative efforts of the faculty. The successful candidate would join a dynamic department with world class undergraduate and graduate programs in civil and environmental engineering. They will contribute to teaching foundational undergraduate and advanced graduate courses in CEE while also embracing the many transdisciplinary connections across Princeton. An appointment may be made jointly with another department or program. The department is committed to fostering an academic environment that acknowledges and encourages collegial, pleasant and welcoming working ambience. We seek faculty members who will create a climate that embraces excellence, with a strong commitment to teaching and mentoring that will enhance the work of the department and that will attract and retain talented students. Princeton University is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability status, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. This position is subject to the university's background check policy. The successful candidate will have a PhD in an appropriate field (including but not limited to Civil and Environmental Engineering, Engineering Mechanics, Fluid-Structure Interaction, Environmental Science, Systems Engineering, etc.) and a proven record of innovation and creativity in conducting research at, but not limited to, interactions between climate extremes and built environment, as applied to the pressing civil and environmental engineering problems related to resilience, sustainability, and climate change adaptation. Review of applications will begin November 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should apply online a nd should submit their (1) application cover letter, (2) CV, (3) contact information for three references, (4) research statement, (5) statement of teaching vision, and (6) a single published paper that they feel best represents their research and potential for future scholarship and, optionally, (7) short personal statement with any additional information that the candidate finds important, but out of the scope of the above material (1-6). Inquiries regarding this position should be addressed to the Chair of the Search Committee, Prof. Maria Garlock at mgarlock@princeton.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181987": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Computer Science Location: Princeton  NJ The Computer Science Department invites applications for postdoctoral and more senior research positions. Individuals with evidence of experience in scholarly research and a strong commitment to excellence in education are encouraged to apply. The Term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. PhD is required. Applicants must apply online and include curriculum vitae, research statement and three confidential letters of reference. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. For general application information and how to voluntarily self-identify, please link to https://dof.princeton.edu/apply"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/185365": {"title": "DeCenter Postdoctoral Research Associates", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Computer Science Location: Princeton  NJ The Princeton DeCenter invites applications for Postdoctoral Fellows , (including more senior researchers) with academic or industry experience expected to begin in Feb. 2027 through Feb. 2028. They will be appointed at the rank of Postdoctoral Research Associate or at a more senior rank, as appropriate. The Term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. Fellows must have their Ph.D. in hand by the start of the appointment. Fellows will join a vibrant intellectual community dedicated to advancing research on blockchain, decentralized technologies, and their applications, across computer science, economics, law, political science, philosophy, and related fields. The DeCenter is a newly established interdisciplinary hub at Princeton University devoted to exploring the decentralization of power and trust through blockchain (and similar) technology. Scope and Expectations We seek to create a truly interdisciplinary cohort of postdoctoral fellows to jointly lead research projects. Fellows\u2019 primary responsibilities will therefore be to conduct research and collaborate with others in cross-disciplinary research initiatives. We also seek to maintain a vibrant interdisciplinary community, and fellows will also be responsible for co-organizing weekly seminars, occasional workshops, etc. that are of interest to the broader DeCenter community. An ideal candidate would satisfy the following selection criteria: \u25cf\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A strong record of research in their primary discipline. \u25cf\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A demonstrated ability to lead independent projects. \u25cf\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A demonstrated ability (ideal) or demonstrated interest (necessary) in interdisciplinary engagements, and the ability to serve as a strong bridge between their primary discipline and others. \u25cf\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A strong record of research (ideal) or demonstrated interest (necessary) in foundational research concerning blockchain technology or similar technologies that support the decentralization of trust. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, but prefers to receive them by December 1, 2026 11:59PM EST. Applications must include: \u25cf\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A cover letter . \u25cb\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Please use the cover letter to briefly state the applicant\u2019s primary discipline and any qualifications related to the selection criteria. \u25cb Please also use the cover letter to briefly describe what motivates the candidate to pursue an interdisciplinary postdoctoral fellowship concerning blockchain technology and applications. \u25cf\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A research statement (1-3 pages) describing the candidate\u2019s prior research that best exemplifies the selection criteria.. \u25cf\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 A curriculum vitae . \u25cf\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 The names and email addresses of at least three references . These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/186904": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Computer Science Location: Princeton  NJ The Computer Science Department invites applications for postdoctoral and more senior research positions in two areas of computer science. 1) Quantum computing and quantum architecture and systems. 2) Reinforcement learning algorithms, including exploration, empowerment, and multi agent coordination. Individuals with evidence of experience in scholarly research and a strong commitment to excellence in education are encouraged to apply. This position is expected to begin in January 2027. The Term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. PhD required Applicants must apply online and include curriculum vitae, cover letter, research statement and three confidential letters of reference. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. For general application information and how to voluntarily self-identify, please link to https://dof.princeton.edu/apply"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191810": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Computer Science Location: Princeton  NJ The Department of Computer Science at Princeton University is seeking applications for postdoctoral or more senior research positions in theoretical computer science and theoretical machine learning. The term of the appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. Anticipated start date is in September 2027. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Candidates must have a PhD in Computer Science or a related field. For full consideration, we recommend that candidates apply (including letters of recommendation) by November 15, 2026 11:59pm, EST, though we will continue to review applications past that date. Applicants must \u00a0submit a cover letter, CV, research statement, and contact information for three references. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. For general application information and how to voluntarily self-identify, please link to https://dof.princeton.edu/apply"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/179869": {"title": "***REVISED***  Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering invites applications for postdoctoral, or more senior, research positions. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. Requirements include a Ph.D. in engineering, applied physical sciences or related areas and an experience in an area of research of the department.\u00a0 Individuals with evidence in scholarly research and a strong commitment to excellence in education are encouraged to apply. Applicants must apply online, include a CV, and request 3 confidential letters of recommendation. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190113": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Princeton, NJ  The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at Princeton University has an occasional curricular need for lecturers to teach, co-teach, or lead precepts in areas such as Applied Physics, Biological & Biomedical, Computing & Networking, Data & Information Science, Energy & Environment, Integrated Electronic Systems, Materials & Devices, Photonics, Quantum Engineering, Robotics & Cyberphysical Systems, Security, etc. The department accepts applications in advance in the event the occasion arises where such lecturers are needed. Any appointments and duty time are subject to enrollments and may be for one or two semesters during Fall 2026 and/or Spring 2027. Fall 2026 position would be from September 1, 2026 to January 16, 2027, and Spring 2027 position would be from January 16, 2027 to July 1, 2027. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering or a related discipline relevant to the teaching areas; experience with teaching at the undergraduate level highly preferred. Successful candidates will attend weekly lectures, be present during lab, lead weekly discussions, hold regular office hours, meet with the course instructor(s), and assist in exam writing and grading as it applies to the course being taught. Princeton University is especially interested in qualified candidates who can contribute, through their research, teaching, and/or service, to the diversity and excellence of our academic community. Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references. All materials must be submitted as PDF files. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Essential Qualifications: Ph.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering or a related discipline relevant to the teaching area. Preferred Qualifications: Experience teaching at the undergraduate level is strongly preferred."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190824": {"title": "Assistant Professor", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering seeks applications for a faculty position at the assistant professor (tenure-track) level. Candidates from all areas in electrical and computer engineering are encouraged to apply, with a special focus on the area of devices and/or device integration, and related topics. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering or a related field and should have a commitment to teaching and a demonstrated ability to pursue a high impact research program. An appointment may be made jointly with another department or program. A start date of September 1, 2027 is preferred. The department is committed to fostering an academic environment that acknowledges and encourages diversity and differences. We seek faculty members who will create a climate that embraces excellence, with a strong commitment to teaching and mentoring that will enhance the work of the department and that will attract and retain a diverse student body. Applicant review will begin in November 2026 . For full consideration, please submit applications no later than December 1, 2026. Applications require: a cover letter, complete curriculum vitae, descriptions of research and teaching interests, and four letters of recommendation. This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194623": {"title": "Professional Specialist", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Electrical & Computer Engineering Location: Princeton, NJ The Safe Robotics Laboratory seeks a part-time Professional Specialist to design and oversee human-subjects research for its work on safe and aligned artificial intelligence. The role requires expertise in cognitive and behavioral research methods. It will not include any teaching or precept duties. The supervising faculty member sets project goals. The appointee selects methods independently and consults with the supervisor when results, risks, or timelines change. Responsibilities Design protocols and measures for studies of human feedback and AI-human interaction. Oversee the execution and analysis of those studies. Advise graduate students on experimental design and cognitive-science methods. Set consistent methodological standards across laboratory projects. Contribute to publications arising from the research. The appointment will be at 50% duty time. Initial appointment will be for 5 months with the possibility of reappointment contingent on meeting all the requirements. Candidates should apply by submitting their CV, cover letter, and contact information of three references. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Required qualifications PhD in psychology, cognitive science, behavioral science, or a closely related field. Experience designing and conducting human-subjects research, including feedback elicitation and behavioral measurement. Peer-reviewed publications in a relevant field. Ability to work independently and advise researchers on study design. Preferred qualifications Research experience in behavioral science and artificial intelligence, AI alignment, or human-machine interaction. Experience advising doctoral students. Experience working with engineering or computational research groups."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193791": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Keller Center for Innovation in Engineering Education Location: Keller Center for Innovation in Engineering Education The Keller Center for Innovation in Engineering Education at Princeton University seeks to appoint a part-time Lecturer for the Spring 2027 semester, with the possibility of renewal for the 2027-2028 academic year, to teach EGR 491, High-Tech Entrepreneurship: a course in the School of Engineering and Applied Science that introduces students to the process of launching and commercializing technology ventures. The course is a core offering within the Keller Center\u2019s entrepreneurship curriculum. A general course syllabus is already in place. Appointment and duty time are subject to enrollments. The position will have a start date of January 16, 2027. Competitive candidates must hold a Master\u2019s degree in engineering, business, or a related field; a Ph.D. is preferred. Sufficiently advanced experience in entrepreneurship following a Master\u2019s program or a career founding, operating, or advising technology ventures will also be considered. Prior teaching experience in entrepreneurship, technology commercialization, or a related discipline is highly desirable. Princeton University is especially interested in qualified candidates who can contribute, through their research, teaching, and/or service, to the diversity and excellence of our academic community. Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae, a cover letter that includes a statement of teaching philosophy, and previous teaching evaluations. Contact information for three potential recommenders should be included. Only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. These positions are subject to the University\u2019s background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Term positions of one year or less are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181008": {"title": "***REVISED*** Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Operations Research and Financial Engineering Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Operations Research & Financial Engineering invites applications for postdoctoral and more senior research positions. Individuals with evidence of experience in scholarly research and a strong commitment to excellence in education are encouraged to apply. A Ph.D. in a related field is required. The Term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. Please include a cover letter, CV and contact information for three references. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/184121": {"title": "Lecturer", "description": "Posted: Apr 01, 2026 Deadline: Apr 01, 2027 Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Operations Research and Financial Engineering Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Department of Operations Research and Financial Engineering at Princeton University has an occasional curricular need for lecturers to teach, co-teach, or lead precepts in department courses, and accepts applications in advance in the event such lecturers are needed. Appointments and duty time assignments are subject to enrollment and may be for one or two semesters during the 2026-2027 academic year. Positions would have an expected start date of September 1, 2026, or January 16, 2027. Individuals must possess a PhD in a related field of interest to the department and have experience teaching at the undergraduate and/or graduate level. The successful candidate will lecture according to department scheduling along with exam writing and grading as it is applicable to the course being taught. To apply, please submit a CV, cover letter, and contact information for three references. Due to the volume of applications, only candidates selected for interview will be contacted. No letters of recommendation are required at this time. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. Individuals must possess a PhD in a related field of interest to the department and have experience teaching at the undergraduate and/or graduate level"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/182271": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate ***REVISED***", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Princeton Materials Institute Location: Princeton, New Jersey The Princeton Materials Institute (PMI) invites applicants for postdoctoral or more senior research positions. Individuals with evidence of experience in scholarly research and a strong commitment to excellence in education are encouraged to apply. We are interested in candidates who have an interest in: *Advanced Manufacturing and Integration of Scalable Structures *Soft and Living Materials *Natural and Engineered Materials for Energy, Environment and Sustainability *Optical Materials and Light Matter Interactions *Quantum Materials Science A PhD in Materials Science, Optics, Physics, Chemistry, Electrical, Chemical, Mechanical, Civil or Bio Engineering or related area is required. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applications must include a curriculum vitae along with three letters of recommendation. This position is subject to the University's background check policy.\u00a0 The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. It is recommended that you read through the entire application and gather the required application materials before beginning your application. You will not be able to save or return to edit a partial application. Only complete applications will be accepted for consideration and the application form must be completed in its entirety before it can be submitted. For more information send an email to PMIjobs@princeton.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193107": {"title": "***REVISED*** Princeton Materials Science Postdoctoral Fellowship (PCCM)", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Princeton Materials Institute Location: Princeton, NJ The Princeton Center for Complex Materials (PCCM), a U.S. National Science Foundation - supported Materials Research Science and Engineering Center (MRSEC) invites applicants for postdoctoral positions that will join the research group of a current PCCM investigator.\u00a0 Individuals with evidence of experience in scholarly research and a strong commitment to excellence in education are encouraged to apply. We are interested in candidates who have an interest in: *Quantum Materials *Biomolecular condensates and programmable soft materials *Emerging directions in complex materials More information for the current research groups can be found at https://pccm.princeton.edu/research . These prestigious postdoctoral positions are intended for early-career scientists with a research focus on materials science and engineering.\u00a0 PhD is required. The term of appointment will be full-time, 12-month period as a postdoctoral research associate, renewable annually for up to three years based on eligibility, performance and funding. The position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applications must include a curriculum vitae (CV), list of publications, and a statement of your proposed research plans at PCCM ( not to exceed four pages ).\u00a0 Applicants will need to provide contact information for two references who will be solicited to provide letters of recommendation. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. It is recommended that you read through the entire application and gather the required application materials before beginning your application.\u00a0 Only complete applications will be accepted for consideration and the application form must be completed in its entirety before it can be submitted."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192638": {"title": "IRS Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics: Industrial Relations Location: Princeton, NJ The Industrial Relations Section (IRS) and the Department of Economics invite outstanding researchers to apply for 10-month appointments as IRS Postdoctoral Research Associates for the academic year 2027-2028. Current IRS research interests cover labor economics, broadly defined, and include but are not limited to: aspects of unemployment and racial discrimination, the economics of labor supply and retirement, education, income distribution, wage and employment determination, labor turnover and job duration, and labor unions. Research Associates will work closely with one or more IRS faculty members during their stay and participate in the activities of the IRS, including faculty-graduate seminars, workshops and public lectures, and enjoy access to a wide range of other activities throughout the University. Postdoctoral Research Associates may participate in the teaching program depending on departmental need and if mutually agreed, with sufficient course enrollments, and with the approval of the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. Applications will be reviewed beginning December 2026; applications will be accepted until position is filled. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applicants must have (or expect to have by the start of the appointment) a PhD in Economics Applicants must apply online and include a cover letter, C.V., statement of research, one or at most two research papers, and contact information for three references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195645": {"title": "***REVISED*** Associate Professional Specialist for Princeton Industrial Relations Section-Carry Team", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics: Industrial Relations Location: Princeton, NJ The Industrial Relations Section (IRS) and the Department of Economics seek a highly motivated and experienced specialist to contribute to the research of Assistant Professor Pauline Carry during the 2027-2028 academic year. Carry\u2019s applied economics research interests focus on labor economics and macroeconomics. In particular, she is interested in how employment contracts are determined and the role of labor market regulations. The appointment will be made at the rank of Associate Professional Specialist. The Associate Professional Specialist (APS) will contribute towards the development of novel French datasets, will access existing datasets that are only available in France, and will implement cutting-edge methodologies for the statistical analysis of these datasets. The APS will both collaborate with members of Carry\u2019s research team as well as work independently during research trips to France. Responsibilities include: Construction and management of a database of collective agreements for France, relying on the combination of the Legifrance base, the DARES base, and institutional knowledge. Construction and management of a comprehensive worker-level longitudinal panel using the French firm-level employment records DADS Postes, employing advanced statistical skills to build an algorithm combining information on job in current year with worker situation in past year to chain observations and recover unique worker identifiers. Analysis of firm balance sheet databases only available in France, applying an advanced understanding of accounting practices to use detailed information on capital and inputs to estimate production functions, employing cutting-edge Industrial Organization methodologies Managing\u00a0 and providing quality control of project work completed by third-party contractors Supervising undergraduate research assistants and other casual/temporary research staff and serving as project liaison with external consultants Produce detailed reports, prepare policy briefs and presentations, and assist with drafting materials for publication The work location for this position is primarily in-person on campus at Princeton University with occasional remote work in France. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. This is a one-year term position with the potential for renewal for a second term. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Masters degree in Economics, applied Mathematics, Computer Science, Statistics, or related discipline, or equivalent combination of training, experience, and accomplishments Prior research experience in the fields of Economics Proficiency in accounting practices and terminology Experience working with real-world data sets Strong coding/algorithm skills and proficiency in statistical programming, especially Stata, R, Python, MATLAB, SAS, LaTeX Must be able to access certain French datasets Ability to work independently, especially for remote work in France Strong written and oral communication skills Excellent organizational and time management skills To apply, please provide the following information and documents: Cover letter that briefly describes your academic background, research experience, goals for the future CV Writing sample Three references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195670": {"title": "***REVISED*** Associate Professional Specialist for Princeton Industrial Relations Section-Dobbie Team", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics: Industrial Relations Location: Princeton, NJ The Industrial Relations Section (IRS) and the Department of Economics seek a highly motivated and experienced specialist to contribute to the research of Professor Will Dobbie during the 2027-2028 academic year. Dobbie\u2019s research interests focus on causes and consequences of poverty to understand how we can give individuals from all backgrounds a better chance of success. His recent work has examined racial discrimination in the criminal justice system, the changing nature of economic mobility, and how to build more human-centered AI. Appointment will be at the rank of Associate Professional Specialist. Responsibilities include: Constructing\u00a0 and managing administrative datasets in a variety of high-stakes settings, including criminal justice and child protective services Building advanced machine learning models using both structured and unstructured data to explore human-AI complementarities Maintaining a multi-module codebase; working with Pydantic or similar data models Conducting applied econometrics tasks in Stata or R, such as randomization and balance checks, regressions with extensive controls and fixed effects, and generating publication-ready tables and figures. Managing\u00a0 and providing quality control of project work completed by third-party contractors, including software engineers Producing detailed reports, prepare policy briefs and presentations, and assist with drafting materials for publication Coordinate and submit applications for review by Princeton\u2019s Institutional Review Board The work location for this position is primarily in-person on campus at Princeton University with occasional research trips to Italy. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. This is a one-year term position with the potential for renewal for a second term. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Masters degree in Economics, applied Mathematics, Computer Science, Statistics, or related discipline, or equivalent combination of training, experience, and accomplishments Prior research experience in the fields of Economics Experience working with real-world data sets, including education or school administrative data Graduate-level coding/algorithm skills and proficiency in statistical programming, especially Stata, R, Python, MATLAB, SAS, LaTeX Proficiency in quantitative text analysis methods Proficiency in, or experience with, large language models in a research context: prompt design, structured output, and evaluating model output Strong written and oral communication skills Excellent organizational and time management skills Proficiency in Italian and knowledge of Italian school system and institutions is a plus To apply, please provide the following information and documents: Cover letter that briefly describes your academic background, research experience, goals for the future CV Writing sample Three references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191238": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - International Economics", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics: International Economics Section Location: Princeton University Department of Economics The International Economics Section of the Department of Economics will have an opening for one or more Postdoctoral Research Associates, anticipated to start in September 2027. Primary criteria are the quality of the applicant's research and interest/experience in international economics. These positions provide an outstanding opportunity for independent research as well as opportunities for collaboration with Princeton faculty and graduate students. Postdoctoral Research Associates may participate in the teaching program if mutually agreed, with sufficient course enrollments, and with the approval of the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. Postdoctoral Research Associates are expected to participate actively in the intellectual activities of Princeton University. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one academic year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. Proof of Ph.D. completion is required. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions are in-person on campus at Princeton University. Applicants must have (or expect to have by the start of the appointment) a PhD in Economics. Applicants must apply online and include a cover letter, C.V., statement of research, one or at most two research papers, and contact information for three references. Applications will be reviewed beginning December 2026. For more information, please refer to: http://ies.princeton.edu/fellowship/ ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192261": {"title": "Peter B. Kenen Visiting Fellowship", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics: International Economics Section Location: Princeton, NJ The International Economics Section of the Department of Economics invites applications for the Peter B. Kenen Visiting Fellowship for the 2027-2028 academic year. One or more Fellows will be chosen to spend an academic year in residence at Princeton as visitors of the Section and the Department. The Fellows will be given office space and the opportunity to pursue their own research on any topic in international economics. Fellows may participate in the University's teaching program if mutually agreed, pending sufficient course enrollments, and with the approval of the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. Compensation for Fellows varies with circumstances, including seniority, standing in the profession, and teaching assignment (if any). All Fellows receive a modest fund for research expenses. Fellows may combine the fellowship support from Princeton with other sources of funding to replace up to the full amount of their academic-year salaries at their home institutions. The Fellowship is open to any Ph.D. economist who currently holds a full-time position in a university or a research organization. The work location for these positions is in-person, on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing. Applicants must apply online and include a cover letter, C.V., research statement and contact information for two references. Applications will be reviewed beginning December 1. For more information, please refer to: https://ies.princeton.edu/kenen-fellowship/ ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181050": {"title": "***REVISED*** Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering Location: Princeton, NJ The Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering is seeking candidates for postdoctoral and more senior research positions. Individuals with evidence of experience in scholarly research and a strong commitment to excellence in education are encouraged to apply. A Ph.D. is required. Postdoctoral appointments are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; a ppointments made at a more senior rank may have multi-year appointments . We welcome applications from all areas in mechanical and aerospace engineering, including but not limited to the fields of: Bioengineering Combustion and Energy Science Computational Science and Engineering Dynamics and Controls Systems Energy and Climate Fluid Mechanics Lasers and Applied Physics Materials and Mechanical Systems Robotics. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applicants must apply online and include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement, and contact information for three references. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183373": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - AI for Plasma Physics and Control", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering Location: Princeton, NJ We invite applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate or more senior researcher at the intersection of artificial intelligence, signal processing, and plasma control. The successful candidate will join a leading AI for fusion research group, developing data-driven methods to analyze and control fusion plasmas using large experimental datasets. Research areas may include: \u25cf Machine learning for plasma diagnostics and real-time monitoring \u25cf AI-enabled plasma control and instability prediction \u25cf Data-driven modeling and surrogate models of plasma dynamics \u25cf Signal processing and multimodal data fusion Requirements: \u25cf Ph.D. in computer science, physics, engineering, applied mathematics, or related field \u25cf Strong experience in advanced machine learning, signal processing, or control \u25cf Strong programming skills (Python/C/Matlab) \u25cf Ability to conduct independent and collaborative research The term of appointment depends on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year, with the possibility of renewal based on satisfactory performance and continued funding. Those hired at more senior ranks may receive multi-year appointments. Research staff are eligible for University benefits. Inquiries about the position may be sent to ekolemen@princeton.edu and aj17@princeton.edu with the subject line \u201cPlasma Control Postdoc Inquiry 2026\u201d. Applicants must apply online\u00a0and include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement, and contact information for three references. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183375": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Tokamak Physics and Control (Experimental)", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering Location: Princeton, NJ We invite applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate or more senior researcher for a tokamak researcher role with a focus on experimental plasma physics and control. The successful candidate will join a leading AI for fusion research group and contribute to experimental campaigns on tokamak devices. Research areas may include: \u25cf Tokamak plasma stability, transport, and boundary physics \u25cf Plasma diagnostics and experimental data analysis \u25cf Plasma control and instability prediction \u25cf Signal processing and data-driven analysis of plasma measurements Requirements: \u25cf Ph.D. in plasma physics, physics, or a related field \u25cf Experience participating in or running tokamak experimental campaigns \u25cf Background in control, signal processing, or data analysis \u25cf Ability to work effectively in interdisciplinary teams The term of appointment depends on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year, with the possibility of renewal based on satisfactory performance and continued funding. Those hired at more senior ranks may receive multi-year appointments. Research staff are eligible for University benefits. Inquiries about the position may be sent to ekolemen @princeton.edu and aj17@princeton.edu with the subject line \u201cPlasma Control Postdoc Inquiry 2026\u201d. Applicants must apply online\u00a0and include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement, and contact information for three references. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/183965": {"title": "***REVISED*** Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering Location: Princeton, NJ The Deike Research Group in the Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Department at Princeton University invites applications for postdoctoral and more senior research positions (higher rank will be considered based on experience). The candidate will be working at the intersection of the Deike (MAE/HMEI/PPPL) and the Weichman (Chemistry/PPPL) labs; with strong ties to other members of the Simons Foundation international collaboration on the Princeton campus (Carter, MAE/ACEE/PPPL) and beyond. We are looking for a postdoctoral researcher or more senior researcher to design and develop a stratospheric conditions aerosol chamber, able to conduct long-time interrogation of suspended aerosols. The chief goals of this facility will be to address open questions surrounding stratospheric processing of particles, including aerosol aging, aggregation, and transport, as well as absorption and reflection of sunlight and terrestrial infrared radiation. The designed facility should be able to operate in conditions relevant to stratospheric processing of aerosols, in terms of pressure and temperature, and maintain such conditions over an extended period of time. Expertise in working, operating, and designing low- temperature (down to -80 to -90 o C ) and low-pressure conditions would be ideal, together with a strong motivation to develop a novel experimental platform. The candidate will also be responsible for collaborating with other members of the collaborative initiative, including on optical diagnostics to probe aerosol properties in-situ. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. To be eligible for this position, a PhD in the following related fields is expected: Mechanical Engineering, Physics, or Chemistry. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Applicants must apply online and include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement, and contact information for three references. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Inquiries about the position may be sent to ldeike@princeton.edu with the subject line \"Deike Lab Postdoc Inquiry 2026\". Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189474": {"title": "MAE/HMEI Stratospheric Aerosol Chamber Specialist", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering Location: Princeton, NJ The Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering at Princeton University is seeking a highly skilled and experienced Professional Specialist to design and develop a Stratospheric Aerosol Chamber. The specialist will be appointed at the rank of Associate Professional Specialist or Professional Specialist, depending on qualifications and experience. This is a 3-year term position, supported by a 3-year Simons Foundation Grant to Profs. Luc Deike (MAE/HMEI) and Marissa Weichman (Chemistry) as part of the Simons Foundation International Collaboration on Solar Radiation Management. The ideal candidate should possess substantial expertise in atmospheric aerosol sciences, in particular stratospheric aerosols, aerosol chemistry, and solar radiation management, together with expertise in experimental design and low-temperature experimental techniques. The candidate will be tasked to design and develop a stratospheric conditions aerosol chamber, able to conduct long-time interrogation of suspended aerosols. The chief goals of this facility will be to address open questions surrounding stratospheric processing of particles, including aerosol aging, aggregation, and transport, as well as absorption and reflection of sunlight and terrestrial infrared radiation. The designed facility should be able to operate in conditions relevant to stratospheric processing of aerosols, in terms of pressure and temperature, and maintain such conditions over an extended period of time. Expertise in working, operating, and designing low- temperature (down to -80 to -90 oC) and low-pressure conditions would be ideal, together with a strong motivation to develop a novel experimental platform. The candidate will also be responsible for collaborating with other members of the collaborative initiative, including on optical diagnostics to probe aerosol properties in-situ. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing. Responsibilities: Research Design (85-90%) \u00b7 Design the stratospheric aerosol chamber able to study aerosol processes in stratospheric conditions. \u00b7 Interact with other members of the collaboration and with experts in large scale cloud and aerosol chamber to design the aerosol chamber. \u00b7 Identify, purchase, and install aerosol and aerosol chemistry instrumentation for the facility. \u00b7 Coordinate with other members of Deike and Weichman groups on optical analysis implementation. \u00b7 Demonstrate proof of concept of the facility, lead initial scientific reporting on the facility (conferences and publications). Contribute to grant applications, fundraising proposals, and reporting. Administrative and Development Support (10-15%) Assist with budget planning and financial oversight. \u00b7 Ensure the effective operation of the laboratory where the chamber is housed, including the management of safety protocols, equipment, and resources. Essential Qualifications: \u00b7 A PhD\u2019s degree in natural sciences, atmospheric sciences, chemistry, chemical engineering, climate science, or a related field is required. \u00b7 In-depth knowledge of aerosol sciences, atmospheric/aerosol chemistry, in-situ diagnostics, or solar radiation management \u00b7 Proven research experience in above mentioned fields with publication track record. \u00b7 Strong organizational and communication skills to effectively manage a collaborative laboratory space. Preferred Qualifications: \u00b7 3-5 years of research experience involving aerosol sciences, atmospheric/aerosol chemistry, in-situ diagnostics, or solar radiation management is recommended. \u00b7 Expertise in aerosol diagnostics. \u00b7 Expertise in low-temperature instrumentation. Applicants must apply by submitting a CV , a cover letter , a research statement including your interest, summarizing your past research achievements, and a personal statement explaining interest in developing such a facility. Candidates should also provide contact information of 3 referees for letter of recommendations. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189524": {"title": "John T. Maltsberger III '55 Associate University Librarian for Special Collections *REVISED*", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Library Location: Princeton, NJ  Princeton University Library (PUL) seeks an accomplished, visionary, and collaborative leader to serve as the John T. Maltsberger III \u201955 Associate University Librarian for Special Collections (AUL SC). This highly visible role offers a unique opportunity to shape the future of one of the world\u2019s premier special collections programs. Reporting directly to the Dean of Libraries and serving on the Library Strategy Council, the AUL SC provides strategic and operational leadership for Princeton\u2019s Special and Distinctive Collections, including exhibitions and public programming. The successful candidate will combine scholarly distinction with strong administrative acumen and a demonstrated ability to lead organizational change. PUL is one of the world\u2019s leading research libraries, with over 300 staff and several specialized branches, supporting a diverse community of 5,500 undergraduates, 3,100 graduate students, 1,200 faculty members, and many visiting scholars. Further information: http://library.princeton.edu Primary Responsibilities: \u25cf Strategic Leadership & Collections Oversight \u25cb Define and implement a compelling vision and strategy for Princeton\u2019s special and distinctive collections. \u25cb Oversee collection development, stewardship, access, and instruction for rare and unique materials in both physical and digital formats. \u25cb Lead approximately 60 staff across four major units \u25cf Library-Wide Leadership \u25cb Serve as a key member of the Library Strategy Council. \u25cb Lead strategic portfolios for Special & Distinctive Collections and Exhibitions & Public Programming. \u25cb Sponsor and guide cross-divisional initiatives aligned with library priorities. \u25cf Exhibitions & Public Engagement \u25cb Provide end-to-end leadership of the Library\u2019s exhibitions program, including strategy, curation, scheduling, and budget \u25cb Deliver a dynamic, high-impact public programming portfolio that connects collections to teaching, research, and contemporary discourse \u25cb Expand the reach of collections through digital platforms and partnerships. \u25cf Collaboration & External Relations \u25cb Build strong partnerships with faculty to support research and teaching. \u25cb Engage with campus partners, including research centers, the Princeton University Art Museum, and digital humanities initiatives \u25cb Represent Princeton nationally and internationally within the special collections community. \u25cf Operational Excellence & Change Leadership \u25cb Lead workflow modernization efforts to improve efficiency, access, and integration across the Library. \u25cb Advance digitization, metadata, and preservation practices in partnership with IT and digital scholarship teams. \u25cb Foster a culture of accountability, collaboration, and continuous improvement. Required Qualifications: \u25cf Doctorate in library science or a relevant field, or significant experience leading a similar special collections department or library. \u25cf Minimum of 10 years of senior leadership experience in a research library, cultural institution, or similar organization. \u25cf Strong record of scholarly achievement, including publications and presentations. \u25cf Demonstrated success leading complex organizational and workflow change. \u25cf Deep knowledge of special collections practices, including acquisitions, preservation, access, and digital initiatives. \u25cf Experience leading institutional decision-making related to cultural heritage stewardship and provenance research. \u25cf Proven ability to manage budgets, staff, and large-scale programs. \u25cf Commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, and broad access to collections. \u25cf Excellent communication and relationship-building skills. \u25cf Ability to navigate complex organizations and lead through ambiguity. \u25cf Reading knowledge of relevant research languages. Preferred Qualifications: \u25cf MLS from an ALA-accredited program. \u25cf Experience with born-digital archives and digital special collections. \u25cf Knowledge of preservation standards for analog and digital materials. \u25cf Active engagement in professional organizations (e.g., ARL, RBMS). \u25cf Experience with capital projects or major space planning for special collections facilities. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. The successful candidate will be appointed to an appropriate Librarian rank depending upon qualifications and experience. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. This position is not eligible for H-1B sponsorship that requires consular processing. Applications must include a resume, cover letter, and a list of three references with full contact information. Applications will be reviewed as they are received."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191821": {"title": "Librarian for Ancient Mediterranean and Hellenic Special Collections", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Library Location: Princeton, NJ  Princeton University Library (PUL) seeks a knowledgeable, collaborative, and classroom-oriented librarian for this new position, which oversees the library\u2019s Special Collections for the study of ancient Greece and Rome, Byzantium, and early modern Hellenic history and culture, as well as PUL\u2019s collection of 120,000+ international numismatic objects. The librarian will be integral to instruction, research, and digital initiatives related to these materials and will work closely with the university\u2019s Classics, Art and Archaeology, and History departments, the Princeton University Museum of Art, and the Stanley Seeger Center for Hellenic Studies, which supports Special Collections (SC) acquisitions related to Ancient and early modern Greece. The librarian will work closely with colleagues to steward, interpret, and provide access to PUL\u2019s outstanding collections in these areas for undergraduate and graduate courses (overall, SC hosts 400 classes each academic year), as well as the international research community. They will facilitate discovery and use of the numismatic collection; engage diverse audiences through exhibitions, scholarship, and collection description; lead digital projects, which may utilize advanced imaging technology; and collaborate with colleagues and community partners to advance equitable collection practices. Numismatics work represents 50% of FTE; a core project will be to devise and implement a description strategy for the numismatics holdings within the first 3 years. This new, full-time position joins a highly collaborative curatorial team of six librarians in other fields and reports to the Assistant University Librarian for Special Collections, Rare Books and Manuscripts. The initial appointment will be for three years with the possibility of reappointment. Responsibilities include: Collaborates with Princeton faculty and PUL specialists on instruction Collaborates on curatorial acquisitions supporting the PUL's educational and research mission Promotes donor relations and manages gifts Works with SC Public Services to provide on-site and remote reference Contributes to outreach activities, blogs, library publicity, and public events Develops and prepares content as part of an exhibitions team Partners with SC Technical Services on accessioning, cataloging, archival processing and description; attends to issues of ethical custodianship Collaborates with Preservation and safekeeping of SC materials Represents SC across campus, in professional organizations and on multi-institutional projects Conducts collection-based research for conferences and publications Required Qualifications Advanced degree in humanities or social sciences field engaged with primary sources, such as History, Area/Cultural Studies, Classics, or Art & Archaeology Minimum three years of relevant work experience with special collections Excellent communication and presentation skills Demonstrated service orientation, initiative, flexibility, engagement in the wider academic community, and willingness to learn new curatorial approaches Ability to thrive in an expansive, changing, collaborative environment Preferred Qualifications MLIS or equivalent degree Familiarity with numismatics descriptive practices Five years of professional experience working with special collections Experience with original materials from the times and regions covered Experience making or advising relevant acquisitions Experience building relationships with stakeholders, including marginalized communities Working knowledge of multiple languages relevant to the collections, e.g. Ancient or Byzantine Greek, Latin, or Arabic Work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University The successful candidate will be appointed to an appropriate Librarian rank depending upon qualifications and experience. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. This position is not eligible for H-1B sponsorship that requires consular processing. Applications must include a resume, cover letter, and a list of three references with full contact information. Applications will be reviewed as they are received."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190730": {"title": "Visiting Lecturer in the Humanities/Ferris Professor of Journalism", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Humanities: Council of the Humanities: Program in Journalism Location: Princeton, NJ The Program in Journalism at Princeton University welcome proposals from journalists to teach seminars in journalism and creative nonfiction as Visiting Ferris Professors of Journalism. Full-time and part-time positions are available for one-semester terms: Fall 2027 or Spring 2028. The Journalism program shares a local and global vision that spans disciplines and borders. We view a strong, ethical, and representational press as essential to participatory democracy, and we champion innovation, public engagement, collaboration, diversity, inclusion, and access. Journalists from a range of backgrounds and media are encouraged to apply. In addition to print journalism, we welcome high-level practitioners of audio and data journalism. Full-time visitors take a formal leave from daily journalism to devote themselves to teaching. They must be on campus four days a week, attend all faculty gatherings, and participate in University life. They give talks, participate in panels, advise students, and join in events. Part-time visitors must spend two full days on campus each week for the 12-week term, as well as during the week of Reading Period. Part-time professors are expected to attend faculty gatherings, and relevant JRN events, whenever possible. Seminars meet once a week for three hours, with enrollment limited to 15. Students devote about six hours a week to class preparation. Every week or two, students submit assignments, which the professor critiques during mandatory one-on-one writing conferences. Professors often invite guest speakers and arrange a class visit to a newspaper or magazine. All application materials must be received by October 30, 2026. The Selection committee aims to complete its work by January 2027 . Questions may be addressed to Jeannine Matt Pitarresi, Journalism Program Manager, at jp16@princeton.edu or 609-258-9083. Additional information is available at journalism.princeton.edu. Essential Qualifications: A record of distinction in journalism or other kinds of nonfiction writing. The ability to communicate effectively to students, peers, and members of the community. A practicing journalist- a reporter, editor, producer, photographer, critic, biographer, or documentarian. At least five years of experience working at a news organization or writing regularly for major publications, including in the years immediately prior to submitting an application. Must not have a current tenure-track or administrative position at an academic institution. Must have a bachelor's degree or equivalent experience. Applicants should submit a r\u00e9sum\u00e9 or CV that includes recent publications, a proposal for a seminar, a cover letter that describes their interest in teaching, and contact information for two references. Innovative and cross-disciplinary proposals are encouraged. Many seminars fall under one of these broad course rubrics: Data Journalism Visual Journalism Investigative Journalism Writing about the Environment Race, Politics, and the Media Writing about Ideas (Philosophy, Law, Religion) History in Journalism; Journalism in History Covering Politics International News Audio Journalism Please include the following with your seminar proposal: One or two paragraphs about the focus of the course and specific skills students will learn A list of specific topics for each of the 12 weeks of the course A sample reading list of no more than six titles (books, articles, websites, etc.) Two possible writing assignments for short pieces during the course. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H\u20111B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193433": {"title": "Visiting Fellow", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: School of Public and International Affairs: Center for Health and Wellbeing Location: Princeton, NJ The Center for Health and Wellbeing (CHW), part of the Princeton School of Public and International Affairs, invites outstanding researchers to apply for Visiting Fellow appointments. CHW supports research on the multiple aspects of health and wellbeing in both higher- and lower-income countries. Applications are welcomed from researchers in a variety of disciplines, including biology, economics, epidemiology, medicine, psychology, sociology, and related social science disciplines. Visiting Fellows enjoy access to a wide range of scholarly activities throughout the University. Successful candidates will devote most of their time at Princeton to research, discussions, and scholarly collaborations concerned with the determinants of physical, mental, and emotional wellbeing, and the effects of public policy on health and wellbeing. Some may also be invited to teach contingent upon sufficient enrollments and with the approval of the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. Fellows can come for the entire academic year, a single semester, or a portion of a semester. Applicants must have a doctorate or equivalent degree. The program is open to all regardless of citizenship, but it does not support work toward the completion of a degree. Visiting Fellows must be employed at an institution other than Princeton during their visit and are expected to return to their primary appointment at the end of their visit. There is no salary support provided for this position. Visiting Fellows must have salary support from their home institution throughout their visit. Prior to approval by the Office of the Dean of the Faculty, employment and salary verification will be required. Selected Fellows must provide documentation confirming they will receive at least the minimum required salary, which is currently $5,290 per month. Applicants are evaluated on the basis of (1) the quality of their achievements in their field of specialization and their ability to benefit from the activities of CHW; (2) the significance of their proposed contribution to CHW's mission; and (3) the contribution they are likely to make to research in the future. Applicants must apply online at https://apply.interfolio.com/193433 and submit a cover letter, a CV, a writing sample (such as research paper, book chapter, or a dissertation chapter), a statement of no more than 1,500 words describing preferred dates and duration of visiting appointment, how you plan to use your time at Princeton, and the Princeton faculty you expect to interact with, and a statement of the home institution\u2019s leave policy (if applicable). For full consideration, please apply by December 7, 2026. However, applications will continue to be reviewed until all available positions are filled. Applicants will be notified of the outcome of their applications in March 2027. For more information about the Center for Health and Wellbeing, please visit its website at http://chw.princeton.edu . The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195302": {"title": "Visiting Research Scholar/Visiting Fellow, Epidemiology", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: School of Public and International Affairs: Center for Health and Wellbeing Location: Princeton, NJ The Center for Health and Wellbeing (CHW), part of the Princeton School of Public and International Affairs, invites outstanding researchers with experience teaching epidemiology to apply for a Visiting Research Scholar or Visiting Fellow appointment. Appointment rank will be determined based on an individual\u2019s qualifications and experience. The successful candidate will be expected to teach the spring semester undergraduate Global Health Program Epidemiology course , contingent upon sufficient enrollment and with the approval of the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. Accordingly, the appointee must be in residence at Princeton during the spring semester. Consideration may also be given to an appointment for the full academic year, depending on programmatic needs and mutual interest. CHW supports research on the multiple aspects of health and wellbeing in both developed and developing countries. Applications are welcome from researchers in a variety of disciplines, including epidemiology, demography, evolutionary ecology, infectious disease, disease ecology, environmental health, biostatistics, public health, and related disciplines. Visiting researchers enjoy access to a wide range of scholarly activities throughout the University. The successful candidate will devote most of their time at Princeton to research, discussions, and scholarly collaborations concerned with the determinants of physical, mental, and emotional wellbeing, and the effects of public policy on health and wellbeing. Researchers from academic institutions may be eligible to receive up to 25% of their academic-year salaries from their home institutions for the spring 2028 semester. The amount that Princeton contributes to the researcher\u2019s salary is based on the financial support provided by their home institution and will normally not exceed $50,000, plus applicable benefits. The selected candidate will be asked to indicate, or provide written authorization for Princeton to confirm, what percentage of financial support their home institution and other sources are likely to provide during their visit, if any, and to submit a confidential statement indicating salary for the 2027-2028 academic year or provide written authorization for Princeton to confirm their salary. Applicants must have a doctorate or equivalent degree. The program is open to all regardless of citizenship, but it does not support work toward the completion of a degree. Visiting research scholars and fellows must be employed at an institution other than Princeton during their visit and are expected to return to their primary appointment at the end of their visit. Applicants must have experience teaching epidemiology. Applicants are evaluated on the basis of (1) the quality of their achievements in their field of specialization and their ability to benefit from the activities of CHW; (2) the significance of their proposed contribution to CHW's mission; and (3) the contribution they are likely to make to research in the future. Applicants must apply online at https://apply.interfolio.com/195302 and submit a cover letter, a CV, teaching evaluations, syllabi from past courses taught, a writing sample such as research paper, book chapter, or a dissertation chapter; a statement of no more than 1,500 words describing how you plan to use your time at Princeton, and a statement of the home institution\u2019s leave policy (if applicable). For full consideration, please apply by December 7, 2026. However, applications will continue to be reviewed until all available positions are filled. Applicants will be notified of the outcome of their applications in March 2027. For more information about the Center for Health and Wellbeing, please visit its website at http://chw.princeton.edu . The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190700": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: School of Public and International Affairs: Center for the Study of Democratic Politics Location: Princeton, NJ Princeton University's Center for the Study of Democratic Politics (CSDP) seeks post-doctoral associates for 12-month positions starting in August 2027. The Center supports empirical research on democratic political processes and institutions. PhD required. Each post-doctoral associate will pursue research and contribute to the intellectual life of the Center, the Princeton School of Public and International Affairs, and the Department of Politics. CSDP scholars are expected to participate in the weekly CSDP seminars and related weekly research seminars in the Department of Politics, as well as occasional lectures, conferences, and other events. Successful candidates will receive a competitive salary and applicable Princeton University benefits in accordance with university guidelines, as well as a taxable moving allowance and a research allowance. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. It is necessary to have the ability to be on campus daily and on short notice in order to fulfill responsibilities relating to in-person participation for the duration of the fellowship starting in August of 2027. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. PhD required. Applicants must apply online and submit a CV, a 3\u20135 page (double-spaced) statement of research interests/proposal, a writing sample, and two confidential letters of recommendation. A cover letter is optional. Applicants for the 2027\u20132028 Postdoctoral Research Associate position must apply to the position using the \"Apply Now\" button. All application materials, including letters of recommendation, must be received by Monday, October 19, 2026, at 11:59 p.m. EDT."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190732": {"title": "Visiting Research Scholar", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: School of Public and International Affairs: Center for the Study of Democratic Politics Location: Princeton, NJ Princeton University's Center for the Study of Democratic Politics (CSDP) seeks visiting scholars for 12-month positions expected to start in August 2027. The Center supports empirical research on democratic political processes and institutions. Applications are welcome from political scientists and scholars in related social science disciplines. CSDP visiting scholar positions are restricted to employed scholars on leave who are expected to return to their positions. Each scholar will pursue research and contribute to the intellectual life of the Center, the Department of Politics, and the Princeton School of Public and International Affairs. CSDP scholars are expected to participate in the weekly CSDP seminars and related weekly research seminars in the Department of Politics, as well as occasional lectures, conferences, and other events. Visiting scholars from academic institutions often receive up to one-half of their academic-year salaries from their home institutions for the appointment period. The amount that Princeton contributes to the scholar's salary will be determined based upon their salary at the home institution and will normally not exceed $70,000.00, plus benefits support. Selected candidates will be asked to indicate, or provide written authorization for Princeton to confirm, what percentage of financial support their home institution and other sources are likely to provide during their visit, if any, and to submit a confidential statement indicating salary for the current academic year (September 1, 2027 - July 1, 2028) or provide written authorization for Princeton to confirm their salary. Successful candidates will also receive one trip to and from Princeton and a research allowance. Selected candidates will be required to be in residence in Princeton or the local vicinity or demonstrate to the program's satisfaction the ability to be on campus on a daily basis and on short notice in order to fulfill responsibilities relating to in-person participation for the duration of the fellowship (August 2027 - August 2028). Verification of employment will be requested prior to approval by the Office of the Dean of the Faculty. PhD or equivalent doctoral degree required. Applicants must apply online and submit a CV, one (1) reference contact information, a 4-6 page (double-spaced) statement of research interest/research proposal, a writing sample, and a statement of the home institution's leave policy. A cover letter is optional. The deadline for receipt of complete applications is Monday, October 19, 2026, at 11:59 p.m. EST. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194413": {"title": "Crane Fellow in Law & Public Policy - Visiting Professional Specialist", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: School of Public and International Affairs: Program in Law and Public Policy Location: Princeton, NJ The Princeton School of Public and International Affairs invites applications for visiting law fellows in Law and Public Policy for the academic year 2027-28. This full-time visiting program is open to legal scholars actively engaged in research and writing on topics related to contemporary issues in law and public policy. The anticipated start date is on or about September 1, 2027. \u00a0The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University.\u00a0Fellows are expected to demonstrate to the program\u2019s satisfaction the ability to be on campus during all days of the week in the academic year.\u00a0Fellows are expected to pursue their own research project and to be active contributors to the intellectual life of the School of Public and International Affairs (SPIA), including through participation in works-in-progress lunch seminars with Princeton colleagues and visiting speakers. While no teaching is required, fellows are expected to conceive and organize an interdisciplinary Problem-Solving Workshop to be held at some point during their fellowship focused on a policy problem related to the subject of their research. Guidance and staff support will be available to help fellows design a useful workshop that advances their research and thinking, with space available on campus or at SPIA\u2019s meeting facilities in Washington, D.C. \u00a0Candidates are expected to hold a JD degree or equivalent and have a strong record of research publications appropriate to their career stage. Typically, fellows hold full-time law faculty positions at other universities or colleges. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing. The application period is open from October 16, 2026 through January 22, 2027. Fellowship decisions expected by March 2027. Applications should be accessible to an interdisciplinary academic audience and will be evaluated based on the strength of the candidate\u2019s past achievements; the scholarly significance of the proposed project; the likelihood that the project will contribute to advancing thought on a problem of policy importance; and the likelihood that the project would benefit from being conducted at SPIA. Please submit: CV Three Confidential Letters of Recommendation Project Proposal or Writing Sample"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191331": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate at Princeton's Research Program in Development Economics (RPDE)", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: School of Public and International Affairs: Research Program in Development Economics Location: Princeton, NJ  The Research Program in Development Economics (RPDE) at Princeton University\u2019s School of Public and International Affairs invites applications for two Postdoctoral Research Associate positions. The candidates will join the RPDE community. RPDE carries out economic research on the causes and consequences of global poverty, with the goal of informing policy-making and thereby improving the lives of people living in low-and middle-income countries. The Postdoctoral Research Associate will be able to pursue their own research, with the expectation of actively participating in RPDE events such as the weekly seminar and graduate student lunch and engaging with the undergraduate community. This position will not involve any teaching responsibilities. This position is a 12-month term appointment. The start date is summer 2027. Salary is competitive and benefits are provided. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. The Postdoctoral Research Associate will have completed a Ph.D. after September 1, 2026 and before the start date of the appointment. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applications should include a cover letter with the application\u2019s status of dissertation, resume, graduate school transcripts, job market paper, and three academic references. For questions about the position, contact Tejal Johri (Associate Director, RPDE) at rpde@princeton.edu . The submission must be online via Interfolio. We will begin processing applications after November 18 th , 2027, but applications may continue to be considered until the ad close date."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/184922": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute Location: Princeton, NJ Postdoctoral research positions are available at Princeton University through the Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. A PhD is required, with appropriate research experience in quantitative biology, (bio)physics, (bio)engineering or related Engineering and Physical sciences disciplines, and a solid publication record. We seek faculty/research members who will be instrumental to creating an ecosystem of excellence and diversity, with a strong commitment to teaching and scholarship, and who will bring a diversity of viewpoints and cultures. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for these positions is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applicants should upload: -a cover letter -statement of research -teaching background, interests, and philosophy -curriculum vitae -contact information for three references as part of the application process."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/186085": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute Location: Princeton, NJ The Atkinson laboratory in the Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute at Princeton University is seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Research Associate. The Atkinson lab focuses on using protein engineering and synthetic biology approaches to control gene expression and metabolism in cells. The position will be advised by Josh Atkinson and will work in close collaboration with the Avalos lab at Princeton. The main responsibilities of the Postdoctoral Research Associate is to lead the design and implementation of experiments for a DARPA-funded project starting in Summer 2026. This position offers the opportunity to gain research skills in bioengineering and perform team-based research on a fast-paced project. The ideal candidate will have experience in optogenetics, protein engineering, engineering DNA polymerases, protein purification, in vivo growth selections/screens, directed evolution. This position will allow for both professional and laboratory skill development as well as co-authorship on high-impact research articles. This is a 1-year term position, with opportunity for renewal depending on good performance and continuted funding of the project. The PI is devoted to developing a supportive research environment and fostering career development for all members of the lab. Responsibilities - Performing a variety of defined research and laboratory tests and experiments according to prescribed protocols and assigned schedules and developing/documenting new laboratory protocols. All these activities should be done in accordance to Lab safety standards set by Princeton University as well as the Atkinson and Avalos labs. - Recording data and performing data analysis; organizing data into figures. Regular reporting to Atkinson in individual as well as group meetings. - Assisting and collaborating with other researchers in the lab as well as on the project team - Establishing and maintaining laboratory equipment and inventories of supplies, reagents and samples. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Minimum required knowledge, skills, competencies and abilities: - Ph.D. in bioengineering, synthetic biology, biophysics, or related field - Prior experience working in a research environment - Experience in working in a microbiology lab, molecular biology, biochemistry, synthetic biology or equivalent. - Effective communication skills - Exceptional organizational skills and attention to detail - Expertise or interest in learning protein engineering techniques - Must be able to manage own time and prioritize tasks. - Growth mindset - Safety conscious - Minimum 1+ year work experience Preferred knowledge, skills, abilities, experience, and other education - Experience in using optogenetic approaches, specialized biochemistry techniques, spectroscopy and microscopy techniques - Strong interpersonal and communication skills - Proficient in Microsoft Office - Proficient in Python or other scripting language - Experience with safety practices in an academic laboratory environment desired Applicants must include: - Cover letter - Curriculum vitae - The names and contact information of three (3) references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187973": {"title": "Institute Research Program Manager (Associate Professional Specialist)", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute Location: Princeton, NJ The Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute is seeking an Institute Research Program Manager (iRPM) to support the full lifecycle of complex, multi-investigator research programs, including large federal awards (e.g., DARPA, ARPA-H, NIH, NSF). This role, hired at the rank of Associate Professional Specialist, integrates scientific program management, research development, and grant execution, spanning both pre-award proposal development and post-award program oversight. The individual in this role will work closely with faculty as a scientific and programmatic partner, helping translate research concepts into competitive proposals and executable plans, while ensuring the successful coordination and delivery of funded programs. This is an on-campus position designed for individuals with a strong scientific background who are interested in contributing to the strategy, development, and management of high-impact research initiatives. The initial appointment is for 3 years, with the possibility of renewal. Role Scope and Funding This position supports the end-to-end management of complex sponsored research programs. A majority of effort will be allocated to active grants, where the role contributes to program execution, coordination of multi-investigator teams, and technical reporting. A portion of effort will be institutionally supported to lead the development of new funding opportunities, including large-scale, multi-institutional, multi-component proposal submissions. This integrated pre- and post-award structure ensures continuity between proposal development and program execution, which is critical for success in complex federal funding environments. Responsibilities: Research Development & Proposal Leadership (Pre-Award) Lead development of complex grant proposals, including multi-investigator and multi-institutional submissions (e.g., DARPA, ARPA-H, NIH, NSF) Partner with faculty to shape and refine research aims, aligning scientific ideas with sponsor priorities Translate technical concepts into clear, compelling proposal narratives and supporting materials Coordinate proposal development across collaborators, including subawardees and external partners Manage timelines, compliance requirements, and submission processes for large-scale proposals Identify and track strategic funding opportunities aligned with institutional strengths Scientific Program Management (Post-Award) Serve as program manager for awarded projects, supporting alignment between proposed aims and ongoing research activities Track milestones, deliverables, and progress across multi-investigator teams Support development of technical reports and sponsor-facing communications (e.g., DARPA-style reporting) Facilitate regular program meetings, working groups, and sponsor interactions Identify risks or delays and work with faculty and teams to implement solutions Research Portfolio Coordination & Systems Support management of a portfolio of complex research programs Develop and maintain systems for tracking aims, deliverables, publications, and outcomes Coordinate cross-project activities and ensure consistency in reporting and execution Contribute to continuous improvement of processes supporting large-scale research programs Administration & Program Support Coordinate administrative components of complex sponsored awards, including subaward setup and management Work in partnership with departmental and central offices to support budget tracking, compliance, and reporting requirements Ensure timely submission of progress reports and maintenance of accurate grant documentation Support audit readiness and adherence to sponsor guidelines across all assigned programs Qualifications PhD (or equivalent terminal degree) in a scientific or engineering discipline required Demonstrated experience with research proposal development and/or management of sponsored research programs Familiarity with complex federal funding mechanisms (e.g., DARPA, ARPA-H, NIH, NSF) strongly preferred Excellent scientific writing, editing, and communication skills Ability to engage with faculty and researchers on technical subject matter Strong organizational and project management skills, with the ability to coordinate across multiple stakeholders Experience with multi-institutional collaborations and subawards preferred Applicants should upload a cover letter, statement of research and teaching background, interests and philosophy, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references as part of the application process. The cover letter should address the applicant's qualifications for each aspect of the responsibilities listed above. Applicants will be considered until the position is filled. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University and is subject to the University's background check policy. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189168": {"title": "Lab Bioreactor Manager, Princeton Sabre (Associate Professional Specialist)", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute Location: Princeton, NJ The Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute at Princeton University is seeking a skilled Lab Bioreactor Manager, at the rank of Associate Professional Specialist, to join the Avalos Lab focused on microbial production of biofuels, chemicals, food, materials, and other products for renewable energy and sustainable biomanufacturing. This includes working with unique reactor designs and operation protocols for light-controlled microbial cultivations, as well as auxiliary equipment linked to bioreactor operations. Responsibilities - Maintaining proper operation of all bioreactors in the lab and auxiliary equipment associated with them (such as computers, flow cytometers, samplers, probes, etc.). - Ensuring bioreactors and auxiliary equipment are properly maintained and up to date with service contracts, as well as inhouse and professional preventive maintenance services. - Training students and postdocs on the proper use of bioreactors and auxiliary equipment to ensure safe and proper use of the equipment. - Managing the user schedule for bioreactors and auxiliary equipment. - Occasionally, working closely with students and postdocs on their research projects as a collaborator. - Communicating effectively with vendors, suppliers, and professional repair technicians of bioreactors and auxiliary equipment to ensure lab capabilities are maintained or expanded. - Participating in research is a plus. The initial appointment is for one year, with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. - A PhD degree in a relevant science or engineering, such as chemical/biological engineering, biology (synthetic, systems, molecular, cellular, microbial, marine), bioengineering, biochemistry, chemistry, or similar. - Experience in bioreactor operation and maintenance. - Excellent leadership and interpersonal skills. - Strong organizational skills. - Interest in participating in research is a plus. Applicants should upload a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, and contact information for two references as part of the application process. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University and is subject to the University's background check policy. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190060": {"title": "**Revised** Lab Manager, Avalos Laboratory, Princeton University", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute Location: Princeton, NJ The Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute at Princeton University is seeking a highly motivated Lab Manager to join the Avalos Laboratory at the rank of Associate Professional Specialist. The Avalos Lab develops innovative technologies at the interface of synthetic biology, metabolic engineering, systems biology, protein engineering, biomolecular condensates, optogenetics, and sustainable biomanufacturing . Our research aims to engineer microorganisms to produce fuels, chemicals, pharmaceuticals, food ingredients, and advanced materials from renewable feedstocks while developing new tools that advance fundamental biology and biotechnology. The Lab Manager will play a central role in supporting a dynamic and collaborative research environment composed of graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, undergraduate students, visiting scholars, and research staff. Responsibilities Lead the day-to-day operation of a dynamic and collaborative research laboratory. Maintain a safe, organized, and efficient laboratory environment, including oversight of laboratory safety, compliance, and standard operating procedures. Manage purchasing, inventories, equipment maintenance, vendor relationships, and laboratory budgets. Coordinate onboarding and training of new laboratory members in laboratory practices, equipment, and safety. Maintain strain, plasmid, reagent, and sample inventories, as well as laboratory databases and documentation. Coordinate the maintenance and scheduling of shared laboratory instrumentation. Organize laboratory meetings, seminars, and visits by collaborators. Assist with laboratory communications, and maintenance of the laboratory website. Participate in research projects involving microbial bioengineering and/or protein engineering. Mentoring of junior researchers. What We Offer The opportunity to work at the forefront of synthetic biology and metabolic engineering at Princeton University. A highly collaborative and intellectually stimulating research environment with access to state-of-the-art facilities. Opportunities to contribute scientifically to high-impact research and co-author publications. Professional development through interactions with leading researchers, national research centers, and international collaborators. The initial appointment is for one year, with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. PhD or master's in Chemical Engineering, Bioengineering, Biology, Biochemistry, Biotechnology, Molecular Biology, Microbiology, or a related discipline. Previous experience in an academic, industrial, or government research laboratory. Excellent organizational, communication, and interpersonal skills. Strong leadership, initiative, and the ability to manage multiple priorities simultaneously. Experience with molecular biology, microbial engineering, synthetic biology, metabolic engineering, protein engineering, or related laboratory techniques is highly desirable. Experience with laboratory automation, liquid handling robotics, biofoundry technologies, or laboratory information management systems (LIMS) is a plus. Experience with scientific writing, grant preparation, website management, or laboratory outreach is a plus. Enthusiasm for mentoring young scientists and fostering a collaborative, inclusive, and intellectually stimulating research environment. Applicants should upload a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, and contact information for two references as part of the application process. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University and is subject to the University's background check policy. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191394": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, Professor", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute Location: Princeton, NJ The Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute at Princeton University, in partnership with The Department of Computer Science, invites applications for new faculty positions in the area of Computational Bioengineering, at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor rank. We welcome applications from all areas of computational bioengineering. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, the development and application of novel algorithms and machine learning/AI techniques for engineering and extracting insights from biological systems and data sets (genomics, proteomics, imaging, neuroscience), synthetic and chemical biology approaches to cellular computation and biomolecular circuit design, and related areas at the interface of computer/data science and the life sciences. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Engineering, Computer Science, Physics, Chemistry, Neuroscience, Quantitative Biology or related disciplines, and have a demonstrated record of excellence and leadership in research, commensurate with career stage. We seek faculty members who will be instrumental to creating an ecosystem of excellence, with a commitment to teaching and service in our growing community. Princeton recently launched the Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute, including a new Bioengineering Ph.D. program, and a new state-of-the-art facililty. The institute brings together a strong group of faculty engaged in Bioengineering-related research, spanning engineering departments including Chemical & Biological Engineering, Electrical and Computer Engineering, Computer Science, Civil and Environmental Engineering, and Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering, as well as across Princeton\u2019s strong interdisciplinary biology units including Molecular Biology, Ecology & Evolutionary Biology, the Lewis Sigler Institute for Integrative Genomics, and the Princeton Neuroscience Institute. We seek creative and enthusiastic candidates with the background and skills to build upon and complement existing strengths and help expand Princeton's Bioengineering program into new and exciting research areas. Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026. Applicants should submit online: a cover letter, curriculum vitae, including a list of publications and presentations, a 3 page summary of research accomplishments and future plans, a 1 page teaching statement, and contact information for three references. In addition, candidates progressing in the selection process will be asked to provide a link to a short video presentation of a research talk. Princeton University is committed to equal opportunity and non-discrimination. To maximize excellence, we seek talent from all segments of American society and the world, and we take steps to ensure everyone at Princeton can thrive while they are here. That is the sole rationale and the purpose of our diversity and inclusion programs, all of which are voluntary and open to all, and which comply with federal and state non-discrimination laws. Princeton does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, ethnicity, color, national origin, religion, disability, or any other protected characteristic, and Princeton does not provide special benefits or preferential treatment on the basis of a protected characteristic.\u00a0 This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194962": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Atkinson Lab)", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute Location: Princeton University  The Atkinson laboratory in the Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute at Princeton University is seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Research Associate. The Atkinson lab focuses on using protein engineering and synthetic biology approaches to control gene expression and metabolism in cells and assembly biological materials. The position will be advised by Josh Atkinson and will work in close collaboration with a collaborative team of researchers based in the UK and the US. The main responsibilities of the Postdoctoral Research Associate is to lead the design and implementation of experiments for an ARIA-funded project. We will engineer microbial S-layer proteins into large-area, defect-free 2D lattices as tunable materials for molecular separations, catalysis, and photonics. This position offers the opportunity to gain research skills in bioengineering and perform team-based research on a fast-paced project. The ideal candidate will have experience in computational protein design, experimental protein engineering, protein purification, and characterization of protein materials (e.g., AFM, TEM). This position will allow for both professional and laboratory skill development as well as co-authorship on high-impact research articles. The PI is devoted to developing a supportive research environment and fostering career development for all members of the lab. Postdoctoral appointments will be for one year, renewable annually based on funding and meeting all other requirements. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Minimum Requirements: - Ph.D. in bioengineering, synthetic biology, biophysics, or related field - Prior experience working in a research environment - Experience in working in a microbiology lab, molecular biology, biochemistry, synthetic biology or equivalent. - Effective communication skills - Exceptional organizational skills and attention to detail - Expertise or interest in learning protein engineering techniques - Must be able to manage own time and prioritize tasks. - Growth mindset - Safety conscious Preferred Requirements: - Experience with protein engineering and computational protein design - Experience/willingness to learn biochemistry techniques, spectroscopy, scanning-probe and electron microscopy techniques - Strong interpersonal and communication skills - Proficient in Microsoft Office - Proficient in Python or other scripting language - Experience with safety practices in an academic laboratory environment desired Applicants must include: - Cover letter - Curriculum vitae - The names and contact information of three (3) references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191224": {"title": "****REVISED**** Assistant Professor", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Princeton Quantum Initiative Location: Princeton, NJ The Princeton Quantum Initiative, Department of Computer Science, and Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at Princeton University seek applications for a tenure-track assistant professor faculty position in quantum information theory and quantum computer science. Candidates in all areas of quantum science and engineering are encouraged to apply, with a special focus on quantum information theory and quantum computer science, including quantum algorithms and applications, quantum computer architecture, quantum error correction, quantum complexity theory, and fundamentals of quantum information theory. Candidates must hold a PhD in engineering, physics, computer science, materials science, or a related technical field. Candidates should have a commitment to teaching and a demonstrated ability to pursue a high impact research program. A start date of September 1, 2027 is preferred. The University is committed to fostering an academic environment that acknowledges and encourages diversity and differences. We seek faculty members who will create a climate that embraces excellence, with a strong commitment to teaching and mentoring that will enhance the work of the department and that will attract and retain a diverse student body. Applicant review will begin in December 2026. Applications will receive full consideration if they are submitted by December 1, 2026 at 11:59 PM . Applications require: a cover letter, complete curriculum vitae, descriptions of research and teaching interests, and four letters of recommendation. Princeton University is an Equal Opportunity Employer and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, national origin, disability status, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191252": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Princeton Precision Health Initiative Location: Princeton, NJ The mission of the Princeton Precision Health (PPH) initiative is to revolutionize the understanding and advancement of human health by conducting interdisciplinary foundational research, developing and harnessing advanced computational approaches, and training the next generation of leaders. PPH seeks applicants for postdoctoral or more senior research positions to join an interdisciplinary group that is tackling a wide variety of health-related research problems using advanced data-driven, computational approaches. Successful candidates will be willing and able to work across a breadth of disciplines - from genomics to computer science, sociology to psychology, engineering to environmental studies - to make novel insights and to tackle the full complexity of human health. We seek applicants to join any of the several interdisciplinary research projects within Princeton Precision Health. The term of appointment is based on rank. Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding; those hired at more senior ranks may have multi-year appointments. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Essential Qualifications: Ph.D., M.D., or equivalent is required. Applicants should have training and a significant track record in one of the following areas: -computational biology -computer science -electrical or computer engineering -genomics -neuroscience -population genetics / genomics -sociology Applications must be submitted online through Interfolio and should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae that includes a list of publications, a brief statement (3 pages max) of research interests and goals and the names and contact information for three references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187077": {"title": "Lecturer in Data and Intelligent Systems", "description": "Posted: Jun 04, 2026 Deadline: Jun 04, 2027 Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Data & Intelligent Systems Location: Princeton, NJ Data and Intelligent Systems (DaIS) at Princeton University seeks to appoint outstanding lecturers to join our faculty in developing and teaching exciting undergraduate Data Science, Statistics, and Machine Learning courses. Depending on the applicant's background and DaIS\u2019 needs, responsibilities may include a combination of teaching or co-teaching full courses, giving individual lectures, leading precepts, supervising graduate student Teaching Assistants, participating in course and curriculum design, grading Independent Work, and connecting research to courses. Full-time positions may be available. Any appointments and duty time are subject to enrollments and may be for one or two semesters during the 2026-2027 academic year. Positions would have an expected start date of September 1, 2026, or January 16, 2027. Appointments will be made at the rank of Lecturer and will be made through Data and Intelligent Systems. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. Elevating the individual within a culture of collaboration, Princeton University is a powerful foundation upon which to build one\u2019s career and family. Princeton\u2019s competitive array of benefits reflects a holistic approach to supporting individuals and their families at work and beyond. The University is also an engaging, transformative resource in and of itself. From opportunities for collaboration to world-class research and cultural facilities, you will find all manner of spaces that bring people together and inspire new ideas. DaIS is a new unit within Princeton University which will succeed the Center for Statistics and Machine Learning ( https://csml.princeton.edu/ ) as of the 2026-2027 academic year. Essential Qualifications: Advanced degree in computer science, statistics, or a related field. Ability to program in Python or R. Preferred Qualifications: Ph.D., prior teaching experience at the college or university level, and some background in applications to experimental science. Princeton University is especially interested in qualified candidates who can contribute, through their research, teaching, and/or service, to the diversity and excellence of our academic community. To apply, please submit a cover letter, CV, and three confidential letters of recommendation. For full consideration, please submit your application by July 15, 2026, at 11:59 EST. These positions are subject to the University background check policy. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192161": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Data and Intelligent Systems (DaIS)", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Data & Intelligent Systems Location: Princeton, NJ Join Princeton AI Alignment and Safety (AIAS) to advance the frontier of AI Alignment as DaIS Postdoctoral Research Fellows at Princeton University. DaIS Postdoctoral Research Fellows will be part of a dedicated research community that includes leading faculty, research fellows, scientists, software engineers, postdocs, and graduate students working at the forefront of AI alignment. Fellows will have access to a state-of-the-art Princeton AI GPU cluster to support large-scale experimentation and evaluation. Ideal candidates will have a strong interest and demonstrated experience in AI alignment, including areas such as robustness, interpretability, safety, evaluation, and the alignment of advanced AI systems with human values. Research may span theoretical foundations, empirical methods, and system-level approaches to ensuring reliable and trustworthy AI. Ongoing work includes alignment challenges in language models, multimodal systems, code generation, and scientific AI, with an emphasis on safety, fairness, and responsible deployment. Postdoctoral Research Fellows will be appointed through Data and Intelligent Systems (DaIS) and will receive a competitive salary, benefits, office space and equipment, dedicated compute resources, and annual research support. Appointments will be made a the Postdoctoral Research Associate rank.\u00a0 Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year with the possibility of renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. Postdoctoral requirements: Princeton University places high value on in-person collaborations and interactions, and we expect selected candidates to participate in-person. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H- 1 B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate . To learn more about Princeton Data and Intelligent System, please visit https://dais.princeton.edu .\u00a0 If you have any questions about this position or the application process, please feel free to contact us at ai-alignment@princeton.edu . Applicants must have (or expect to have at the time of appointment) a Ph.D. Applicants will need to submit: Cover letter Current curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for three references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194534": {"title": "AI Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Natural and Artificial Minds", "description": "Posted: Oct 09, 2026 Deadline: Feb 28, 2027 Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Data & Intelligent Systems Location: Princeton  Princeton University is seeking to hire an AI Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join the Natural and Artificial Minds (NAM) research initiative. NAM Fellows will take advantage of advances in AI to study natural and artificial minds in parallel, creating the opportunity to make discoveries about ourselves and to find new ways to understand and improve AI systems. NAM Postdoctoral Research Fellows will have access to the Princeton AI GPU cluster (300 H100s). Candidates should have a Ph.D. or doctorate degree in cognitive science, psychology, computer science, philosophy, or a related discipline, and have a strong interest and track record in designing, understanding, or engineering of AI models or methods and their applications in cognitive science. AI Postdoctoral Research Fellows joining the NAM initiative will contribute to one or both current core projects, \"Explanation and Intelligibility\" or \"Developing and Testing AI Models of Human Cognitive Function.\" Candidates can read more about these core projects at https://nam.ai.princeton.edu/research . Postdoctoral Research Fellows will be appointed through Data and Intelligent Systems (DaIS) and will receive a competitive salary, benefits, office space and equipment, dedicated compute resources, and annual research support. Appointments will be made at the Postdoctoral Research Associate rank. Postdoctoral appointments will be for one year, renewable annually based on funding and meeting all other requirements. Candidates are encouraged to apply by the start-of-review date, December 31, 2026 to guarantee consideration, but some applications may be screened and processed before or after that at our discretion. The expected start date for this position is September 1, 2027. We are also open to considering candidates who are available to start before or after that date. Princeton University places high value on in-person collaborations and interactions, and we expect selected candidates to participate in-person. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. To learn more about Princeton Data and Intelligent System, please visit https://dais.princeton.edu . If you have any questions about this position or the application process, please feel free to contact us at nam-ai@princeton.edu . Applicants must have (or expect to have at the time of appointment) a Ph.D. Applicants will need to submit: Cover letter Current curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for three references"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193934": {"title": "Professor at Princeton University and Principal Scientist at PPPL", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Other: Physical Science and Engineering with PPPL Location: Princeton, NJ Princeton University (PU) and the Princeton Plasma Physics Lab (PPPL) are searching for an intellectual leader to establish a new interdisciplinary program in the new Princeton Plasma Innovation Center (PPIC).\u00a0 We seek a leader who will broaden PPPL\u2019s research portfolio while concurrently strengthening ties with the University community. The ideal candidate would articulate a vision for a new experimental research program at the boundary of plasma science and other disciplines. Possible areas of research include materials science, plasma-surface science, applications of plasma to quantum information science, manufacturing, and microelectronics. We anticipate that the successful candidate will establish a new group and laboratory at PPPL, largely in the PPIC, which is currently under construction and scheduled to be completed in 2027. The successful candidate will hold a tenured appointment in a relevant department at the University as well as an appointment as a Principal Scientist at PPPL. \u00a0 As PPPL is a Department of Energy (DOE)\u00a0 National Laboratory, familiarity with DOE processes and a successful track record of DOE support are important considerations. Applications must be submitted online at https://apply.interfolio.com/193934 and should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, a research statement, a teaching statement, and contact information for at least three, but no more than five references. Review of applications will begin by December 1, 2026. References will not be contacted until later in the process. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. All inquiries will be treated confidentially within the committee and should be addressed to Lyman Page, Dept of Physics, Princeton University, page@princeton.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195658": {"title": "Professional Specialist", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Integrative Genomics Location: Princeton, NJ Posted: Oct 26, 2026 Deadline: Dec 31, 2027 The Shvartsman laboratory in the Lewis-Sigler Institute for Integrative Genomics at Princeton University seeks applicants for a scientist, hired at the rank of Professional Specialist, to develop mathematical and computational models of developmental processes. The key roles for this position involve the formulation of data-driven models of developmental dynamics and model-based design of experiments in model organisms. We are seeking a candidate with expertise in analyzing live imaging and transcriptional profiling data, and with the development of statistical and mechanistic models. The appointment is for three years, with the possibility for renewal pending satisfactory performance and continued funding. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Essential qualifications include: A PhD in mathematics, applied mathematics, or statistics. Proven ability to successfully complete major independent research projects. Proven ability to work well in a group, including collaboration and taking direction. Knowledge and skills as described above. Applications are submitted online and must include a curriculum vitae that includes a list of publications and contact information for three references."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194706": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate - Targeted Simons Research Group on Dark Matter Dynamics", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Physics Location: Princeton University Posted: Oct 21, 2026 Deadline: Apr 21, 2027 We invite applications for a postdoctoral position with the Targeted Simons Research Group on Dark Matter Dynamics at the rank of postdoctoral research associate or a more senior rank. The primary mission of this working group is to build a theoretical framework that translates particle physics models of dark matter with new forces into galactic predictions. The successful candidate will contribute to this effort through research connecting numerical simulations, dark matter phenomenology, and current/future observations. The work location will be primarily in-person on campus at Princeton University, or subject to approval at an off-campus location where the research project is being undertaken.\u00a0 The successful candidate will collaborate closely across several working group nodes, with a particular emphasis on interactions with Rutgers University and Stony Brook University. The expected start date is between Summer and Fall 2027, with flexibility to accommodate the selected candidate\u2019s availability. The term of the appointment is based on rank.\u00a0 Positions at the postdoctoral rank are for one year and multi year at the more senior rank,\u00a0 with the possibility of renewal based on funding and meeting all other requirements. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B Visa requiring consular processing; other Visa sponsorships (including H-1B not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. The successful candidate will work closely with Alyson Brooks (Rutgers), Rouven Essig (Stony Brook), Mariangela Lisanti (Princeton), and other members of the collaboration to develop and lead research efforts that advance the scientific goals of the Targeted Simons Research Group on Dark Matter Dynamics. Candidates should have a Doctorate degree in Physics, Astronomy, or a closely related field, by the start of the appointment with the ideal candidates further demonstrating in their application: \u25cf Simulation Experience: Experience working with hydrodynamical or N-body simulations, including at least one publication in this area. Preference will be given to candidates with experience developing and executing new simulations. \u25cf Subject Matter Expertise: Working knowledge of dark matter phenomenology and/or small-scale structure observables for dark matter (e.g., dwarf galaxies, streams, lensing). Applicants must apply online and must include the following: \u25cf A Research Statement (maximum of 2 pages, excluding references). This statement should (i) describe how the applicant\u2019s past research and relevant experience prepare them for this position, and (ii) outline how their research interests and expertise will contribute to the collaboration's scientific goals. The statement should specifically address the research directions described in this advertisement and highlight prior collaborative experiences, scientific creativity, and demonstrated potential for research independence. \u25cf A curriculum vitae (including a list of publications) \u25cf Contact information for three references. Letters of reference will be collected from shortlisted candidates."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195898": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Hasson Lab)", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Princeton Neuroscience Institute Location: Princeton Neuroscience Institute Posted: Oct 23, 2026 Deadline: Oct 23, 2027 The Hasson Lab at Princeton University's Department of Psychology and Princeton Neuroscience Institute is seeking a postdoctoral researcher to develop computational world models of children's everyday environments. The position is part of the First 1,000 Days Project, a large-scale effort to continuously record children's natural audiovisual environments during their first years of life. The research will use these rich, longitudinal recordings to train deep learning models that reconstruct and simulate children's environments, including egocentric visual perspectives, physical interactions, and actions. The goal is to build computational models that capture the structure and dynamics of the world developing children experience, and to explore how these representations emerge through everyday experience. The ideal candidate will have a strong computational background and prior experience in deep learning, computer vision, and training large-scale visual or multimodal models. Experience with world models, generative video models, 3D scene reconstruction, virtual reality environments, or the Unity game engine is particularly desirable. We welcome applicants from computer science, machine learning, computational neuroscience, and related fields who want to apply advanced computational methods to understand learning and development in naturalistic environments. Positions at the postdoctoral research associate rank are for one year, renewable annually based on funding and meeting all other requirements. Those hired at a more senior rank may have multi-year appointments. Interested applicants must include a cover letter, curriculum vitae with publication list, a brief statement of research interests, and two reference contacts. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. Essential Qualifications: PhD in a relevant discipline."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195280": {"title": "The NJ Community College Connections in Materials Research (C3MR) Visiting Research Scholar (VRS) Program", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Princeton Materials Institute Location: Princeton, NJ Posted: Oct 23, 2026 Deadline: Jan 08, 2027 The C3MR visiting research scholar program is a 9-week (June 1, 2027-July 30, 2027) summer appointment at Princeton University for NJ community college faculty to engage in a research and educational partnership with faculty affiliated with the Princeton Center for Complex Materials (PCCM). This program aims to increase community college students' exposure to materials science and engineering and to cutting-edge research. The C3MR visiting research scholar (VRS) is expected to collaborate with an existing PCCM faculty member and jointly mentor two students from the VRS\u2019s home institution. The specific nature of the collaboration will be defined by the visiting research scholar and the partnering PCCM faculty and may involve one or more of the following: 1.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Pedagogical innovations: summer research aimed at developing or reworking pedagogical content that aligns materials research (inspired by PCCM research) with the VRS\u2019s teaching curricula as, for example, in research-infused lecture courses, new lab module development, and course-based undergraduate research experiences. 2.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Education research: collaborative research on STEM learning processes or teaching methods to increase student engagement and success in materials science and engineering. 3.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Contributing to PCCM research: the C3MR VRS and summer students collaborate on and contribute to existing research projects led by the collaborating Princeton faculty. Projects that expose students to instrumentation training and technical lab skills involving PCCM- and PMI-affiliated shared facilities, e.g., the Imaging and Analysis Center (IAC) and the Micro/Nanofabrication Center (MNFC), are encouraged but may require approval of and potentially partnership with the core facilities director. 4.\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Development of other activities that may integrate PCCM research, STEM education, and STEM workforce development for community college students. Requirements for C3MR VRS Responsible for selecting two students from their home institution to participate in the Research Experience for Undergraduates program. Responsible for submitting an end-of-summer report describing any accomplishments, outcomes, the potential mid- and long-term impact of the work beyond the summer program, and proposed pathways to sustain a relationship with PCCM in the academic year. Please see the PCCM website for more information about the C3MR program. The applicant must hold a full-time faculty position at a New Jersey Community College and teach in a STEM discipline. Appointment will be at the rank of Visiting Research Scholar. The position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applicants must submit a current CV and a cover letter. The CV should include their educational background, current employment, courses taught, and any research experience. The cover letter should briefly describe the applicant\u2019s background and interest in the program. It should identify the Princeton faculty member(s) the applicant is interested in collaborating with, the research areas of interest, and include a brief proposal outlining the general direction of the collaboration (research, pedagogy, etc.) and its relevance to the C3MR program goals. The cover letter should also briefly outline how the applicant intends to select two students for this opportunity (whether the positions are offered to students in courses taught by the applicant according to specific criteria, through a nomination process, an internal call for applications, etc.). Please provide contact information for two references who may be solicited to provide recommendation letters. Please see the PCCM website for our current research groups. This position is primarily in person on Princeton University\u2019s campus, with possibly up to 25% remote. This is a 9-week (June 1, 2027 - July 30, 2027) summer position. The successful candidate must also provide an endorsement letter (template will be provided) from their home institution supporting their participation in this program. Written proof of employment must be provided by the candidate's home institution upon hire. Interested applicants are encouraged to discuss their applications with the PCCM Education Outreach Director before applying. To schedule a virtual meeting, email nunes@princeton.edu."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195178": {"title": "Associate Professional Specialist for Princeton Program In Public Finance", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Social Sciences: Economics: Center for Economic Policy Studies Location: Princeton University Posted: Oct 20, 2026 Deadline: Dec 31, 2026 The Princeton Program in Public Finance (PPPF) in the Department of Economics seeks a highly motivated and experienced Associate Professional Specialist. PPPF research interests include wealth inequality, entrepreneurship, gender equality, regional economic development, political economy, and the analysis of tax subsidies on the housing market and social insurance policies. The successful candidate will provide advanced research support to Henrik Kleven on specific applied economics research projects during the 2027-2028 academic year. The Associate Professional Specialist (APS) works under general faculty guidance and must interpret complex ideas, concepts, and methodologies and apply them effectively to new projects and scenarios.\u00a0 \u00a0Professor Kleven's research focuses on questions in public economics, labor economics, and gender economics. He combines theory and data\u2014often large administrative datasets\u2014to study the effects and optimal design of government policies. He also works on gender inequality in the labor market, and especially the impact and drivers of child penalties. While this position will report to Professor Kleven, the successful candidate will also have the opportunity to interact closely with other faculty in the section, including Ilyana Kuziemko, Owen Zidar, and Evan Soltas. The work for Professor Kleven involves econometric analyses, calibration methods, and theoretical modeling in public finance. On empirical projects, the APS is expected to develop empirical models and to independently implement quasi-experimental analyses (such as regression discontinuity, bunching, and event study designs). Succeeding in these tasks requires a good command of graduate-level econometrics, including econometric modeling, identification, and inference. On theoretical projects, the APS is expected to develop theoretical models (such as general equilibrium models of taxation), to derive analytical results within these models, and to calibrate and implement numerical simulations of the models. Succeeding in these tasks requires graduate-level economic theory (including general equilibrium theory, perturbation methods, and fixed-point equations) and numerical methods (including root finding, solving large systems of nonlinear equations, and approximating integrals). In both types of projects, the APS is expected to proactively verify the assumptions underlying the empirical and theoretical approaches and to propose alternative approaches where appropriate. Other responsibilities may include: De signing online survey instruments, managing survey recruitment, and processing survey data. Training, optimizing, and testing advanced AI models on various forms of raw data. Managing and supervising quality control of project work completed by third-party contractors, including software engineers. Coordinating and submitting applications for review by Princeton\u2019s Institutional Review Board. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. These are one-year term positions with the potential for renewal for a second term.\u00a0 The expected start date is on or around June 15, 2027. The Associate Professional Specialist is an important part of the PPPF program. In addition to working closely with faculty, the APS has opportunities to participate in the vibrant academic life of Princeton University through attending seminars and interacting with students and faculty in the Department of Economics. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Master's degree in Economics, Applied Mathematics, Computer Science, Statistics, or a related discipline Prior research experience with econometric modeling, identification, and inference Prior research experience with general equilibrium theory, perturbation methods, fixed-point equations, and numerical methods Experience working with real-world data sets Strong coding/algorithm skills and proficiency in statistical programming, especially Stata, R, Python, MATLAB, SAS, LaTeX Ability to work independently Strong written and oral communication skills Excellent organizational and time management skills Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, and as such, we encourage applicants to apply early. Short-listed applicants will be contacted for an interview and technical assessment. Candidates should upload the following: Cover letter and resume List of three references Transcripts from all previous academic programs A research paper in economics or finance (e.g., master's course paper, independent research, etc.) Sample of code (ideally for the same paper) Address all queries to: pppecon@princeton.edu ."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195025": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Classical Art and Archaeology", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Art Museum Location: Princeton University Art Museum  Posted: Oct 21, 2026 Deadline: Jan 17, 2027 The Princeton University Art Museum, together with the Department of Art and Archaeology and the Seeger Center for Hellenic Studies, seeks a Postdoctoral Research Associate with expertise in the eastern Roman Empire to assist with the research, teaching, and planning for a future exhibition related to material from Antioch-on-the-Orontes. Located at the crossroads of major trades in the ancient world, Antioch was a cosmopolitan urban center that regularly came into contact with a diversity of peoples, objects, ideas, and materials from across the empire. Excavations conducted by a Princeton-led team (1932-39) yielded a large number of luxurious mosaics, numerous sculptural fragments, coins, and over 12,000 small finds. The 100th anniversary of the excavations offers the opportunity to reconsider the legacies of the archaeological work undertaken at Antioch and to advance our knowledge of the site through new avenues of scholarly inquiry. The appointment is expected to start on July 1, 2027, through July 1, 2028, with the possibility of renewal for a second year, following academic review and contingent upon availability of funding and meeting all other requirements. Candidates must have completed all requirements for the doctoral degree by March 1, 2027. Candidates may have up to two years of post-Ph.D. research experience by the start date. Candidates will receive support for research-related expenses, up to $3,000 per academic year. Postdocs will be required to work full-time, fulfilling responsibilities related to in-person participation. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at the Princeton University Art Museum. When teaching, successful candidates will carry the additional part-time rank of Lecturer, and any teaching opportunities are subject to sufficient enrollment and the approval of the Dean of the Faculty. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. PhD in art history or classical art and archaeology, or a related field, with demonstrated familiarity with the material culture from the ancient Mediterranean. Experience working with museum collections and developing compelling exhibition projects is preferred, as is familiarity with the history of archaeological excavations at Antioch. The position will also involve conducting archival research in Visual Resources at Princeton and collaborating with specialists. Applicants should apply online and submit a cover letter, CV, short writing sample, and contact information for three references. DEADLINE: All materials must be received by 11:59 pm EST on January 17, 2027. This position is subject to the University's background check policy."}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195460": {"title": "Princeton Laboratory for Artificial Intelligence Postdoctoral Research Fellows - Princeton Societal AI", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Engineering: Artificial Intelligence Lab Location: 41 William Street, Princeton, NJ 08540 Posted: Oct 19, 2026 Deadline: Dec 01, 2026 Princeton University is seeking to hire AI Postdoctoral Research Fellows to join the Princeton Societal AI (PSAI) Initiative as part of Data and Intelligent Systems (DaiS) to conduct research focused on societal AI. Postdoctoral Research Fellows will be part of a dedicated research community that includes leading faculty, research fellows, scientists, software engineers, postdocs, and graduate students. Fellows will have access to a state-of-the-art AI Lab GPU cluster to support large-scale experimentation and evaluation. Ideal candidates will have demonstrated experience in \u201csocietal AI\u201d especially as related to culture. We seek scholars working on AI as a \"cultural technology\" \u2014 including its role in disciplinary practice across the arts and humanities and in knowledge infrastructures - as well as those examining AI's broader societal effects - including on creative and cultural industries (literature, music, film, visual arts). A strong foundation in computational humanities or cultural analytics, combined with humanistic and interpretive approaches to AI systems, is expected. Postdoctoral Research Fellows will be appointed through Data and Intelligent Systems (DaiS) and will receive a competitive salary, benefits, office space and equipment, dedicated compute resources, and annual research support. Appointments will be made at the Postdoctoral Research Associate rank. Postdoctoral appointments will be for one year, renewable annually based on funding and meeting all other requirements. Postdoctoral requirements: Princeton University places high value on in-person collaborations and interactions, and we expect selected candidates to participate in-person. PhD is required. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H- 1 B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. To learn more about Princeton Societal AI, please visit https://societal.dais.princeton.edu/ To learn more about Data and Intelligent System, please visit https://dais.princeton.edu . Princeton University is committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive academic community. If you have any questions about this position or the application process, please feel free to contact us at PSAI@Princeton.edu . All offers and appointments are subject to review and approval by the Dean of the Faculty. These positions are subject to the University's background check policy. Ideal candidates will have demonstrated experience in \u201csocietal AI\u201d especially as related to culture. We seek scholars working on AI as a \"cultural technology\" \u2014 including its role in disciplinary practice across the arts and humanities and in knowledge infrastructures - as well as those examining AI's broader societal effects - including on creative and cultural industries (literature, music, film, visual arts). A strong foundation in computational humanities or cultural analytics, combined with humanistic and interpretive approaches to AI systems, is expected. Applicants must apply online and submit the following: Cover letter Current curriculum vitae Research statement Contact information for three references is required"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194841": {"title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (AY27-28) - DDSS", "description": "Department: Princeton University: Office of the Dean of the Faculty: Natural Sciences: Data & Intelligent Systems Location: Princeton Posted: Oct 23, 2026 Deadline: Feb 28, 2027 Princeton University's Initiative for Data-Driven Social Science (DDSS) invites applications for the Postdoctoral Research Associate role. DDSS is part of Data and Intelligent Systems (DaIS) \u2013 Princeton\u2019s new interdisciplinary hub for research and education in artificial intelligence, data science, and statistics. DDSS supports technical and methodological innovation in quantitative and computational social science, addressing a diverse array of new data and analytic challenges, facilitating impactful multidisciplinary collaboration, scholarly advancement, and the creation of tools and public goods. Responsibilities This role's emphasis is on making substantive technical and methodological contributions that either directly or indirectly support DDSS projects or its mission. Candidates will be able to apply and further develop their technical skills in a dynamic research environment. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to work closely with our engineers and other research staff. Projects may include software package creation and maintenance, data engineering, development and/or implementation of advanced statistical methodologies, and supporting research on high performance and cloud computing. The successful candidate will also be expected to offer 2-3 advanced technical or methodological workshops each semester, assist in organizing research-based events (e.g., speaker series, symposia, or reading groups), present their work at research methods and field-specific seminars, assist with DDSS and DaIS projects on occasion, and offer consultation to faculty, graduate students, and research staff on topics related to the candidate's area of expertise. Three days per week can be dedicated to independent research. Postdoctoral research associates work under the direct supervision of the DDSS Faculty Director and the Director for Research and Strategy. Area of expertise is open, but preference will be given to candidates with expertise in one or more of the following: software package development and maintenance in R; record linkage/entity resolution; data privacy techniques; large data processing and high-performance computing; advanced causal inference and statistics; computer vision and novel applications of machine learning. Advanced knowledge of R or Python is required. Intermediate knowledge of C/C++ and/or at least one SQL dialect is preferred. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Postdoctoral appointments will be for one year, renewable annually based on funding and meeting all other requirements. The appointment will be through DaIS. This position is subject to the University's background check policy. The work location for this position is in-person on campus at Princeton University; the position is not eligible for remote work. This position is not eligible for sponsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing; other visa sponsorships (including H-1B visas not requiring consular processing) may be available, as appropriate. Applicants must have (or expect to have at time of appointment) a PhD in the social sciences, statistics, computer science, or related fields, and their interests must fall at the technical forefront of quantitative social science. Candidates should offer state-of-the-art technical or methodological skills, applying innovative techniques in the examination of substantive social science research questions. In addition to innovation, candidates will be evaluated on their potential to create public goods and their capacity for interdisciplinary engagement. Please upload the following documentation: Cover letter C.V. 1-2 page research statement, specifying research interests and example training workshops the candidate could offer 1-2 research papers (please upload multiple research papers as one document) Contact information for two references"}}, "rows": {"https://apply.interfolio.com/193577": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/193577", "job_title_in_post": "Professor/Associate Professor", "position_type": "Associate_Professor; Full_Professor", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "School of Engineering and Applied Science", "area_key_words": "", "deadline_of_application": "November 15, 2026", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": "September 1, 2027"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/180973": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/180973", "job_title_in_post": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Robotics and Autonomous Systems) ***REVISED***", "position_type": "Research_Fellow", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "human-robot collaboration; advanced manufacturing", "deadline_of_application": "", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": ""}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/181066": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/181066", "job_title_in_post": "***Revised***Associate Professional Specialist", "position_type": "Unclassified", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "PhD students; Science Foundation\u2019s Future of Work", "deadline_of_application": "", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": ""}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191961": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/191961", "job_title_in_post": "Associate Professor, Professor in Architectural Design", "position_type": "Associate_Professor; Full_Professor", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "School of Architecture", "area_key_words": "Architectural Design", "deadline_of_application": "November 1, 2026", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": ""}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194279": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/194279", "job_title_in_post": "Fellow in Architecture, Urbanism and the Humanities; Princeton Initiative in Architecture, Urbanism and the Humanities Untitled Position", "position_type": "Research_Fellow", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "Center for Global India, and the School of Architecture", "area_key_words": "Architecture, Urbanism and the Humanities Untitled Position; approval", "deadline_of_application": "", "position_start_date": ""}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190626": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/190626", "job_title_in_post": "Assistant, Associate, Full Professor", "position_type": "Assistant_Professor; Associate_Professor; Full_Professor", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "Department of Classics", "area_key_words": "historical research related; Roman History", "deadline_of_application": "October 1, 2026", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": ""}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/192160": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/192160", "job_title_in_post": "Lecturer", "position_type": "Lecturer", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "Department of Classics", "area_key_words": "", "deadline_of_application": "", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": ""}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/193338": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/193338", "job_title_in_post": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in the Cognitive Science of Values", "position_type": "Research_Fellow", "job_term": "Part-time", "department_or_school": "Department of Psychology", "area_key_words": "Values; Department Chair", "deadline_of_application": "December 7, 2026", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": "September 1, 2027"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/195297": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/195297", "job_title_in_post": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in the Social Science of Human Values", "position_type": "Research_Fellow", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "Department of Psychology", "area_key_words": "Human Values", "deadline_of_application": "November 20, 2026", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": "July 1, 2027"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/191304": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/191304", "job_title_in_post": "Lecturer", "position_type": "Lecturer", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "Department of Comparative Literature", "area_key_words": "", "deadline_of_application": "", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": "September 1, 2026"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194499": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/194499", "job_title_in_post": "Professor/Associate Professor", "position_type": "Associate_Professor; Full_Professor", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "Department of French and Italian OR the Department of Spanish and Portuguese", "area_key_words": "", "deadline_of_application": "December 15, 2026", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": "September 1, 2027"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/194670": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/194670", "job_title_in_post": "Lecturer, Council of the Humanities", "position_type": "Lecturer", "job_term": "Part-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "Council of the Humanities; European Cultural Studies", "deadline_of_application": "December 7, 2026", "position_start_date": "January 16, 2027"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/189234": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/189234", "job_title_in_post": "Lecturer", "position_type": "Lecturer", "job_term": "Part-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "Chinese", "deadline_of_application": "", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": "September 1, 2026"}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/190630": {"school_id": 882, "posting_url": "https://apply.interfolio.com/190630", "job_title_in_post": "Lecturer in English", "position_type": "Lecturer", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "Department of English", "area_key_words": "English", "deadline_of_application": "", "posted_date": "", "position_start_date": ""}, "https://apply.interfolio.com/187419": {"school_id"